      "rules": [
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233015",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233015",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000014",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233015",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000014",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233015r960759_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233015r960759",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000014-CTR-000035",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must use TLS 1.2 or greater for secure container image transport from trusted sources.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to use TLS 1.2 or greater when components communicate internally or externally. The fix ensures that all communication components in the container platform are configured to utilize secure versions of TLS.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to verify that TLS 1.2 or greater is being used for secure container image transport from trusted sources. \n\nIf TLS 1.2 or greater is not being used for secure container image transport, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The authenticity and integrity of the container image during the container image lifecycle is part of the overall security posture of the container platform. This begins with the container image creation and pull of a base image from a trusted source for child container image creation and the instantiation of the new image into a running service. If an insecure protocol is used during transmission of container images at any step of the lifecycle, a bad actor may inject nefarious code into the container image. The container image, when instantiated, then becomes a security risk to the container platform, the host server, and other containers within the container platform. To thwart the injection of code during transmission, a secure protocol (TLS 1.2 or newer) must be used. Further guidance on secure transport protocols can be found in NIST SP 800-52.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000068"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "6fed8a79-2eb2-5549-9aff-d98725fe1be5",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Saved Zarf/Core source evidence identifies an HTTP internal-registry default and supported secure transport options, but that default does not establish the actual MPN image-pull configuration or registry ownership. Application gateway/mesh TLS does not automatically cover node-to-registry image pulls. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work with verification first: inspect actual image-transfer paths and responsibility boundaries, credit existing TLS 1.2+ protection and configure only confirmed gaps. Do not infer that a bootstrap migration is needed from a local setup default.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE / MPN platform engineering\n\nNext action: In the post-classification verification pass, identify the actual MPN image sources, staging/registry and node image-pull paths and their owners. Inspect deployed registry/runtime/bootstrap settings and verify TLS 1.2 or newer for the applicable transport paths from trusted sources. Credit an existing approved MPN registry. Configure only confirmed gaps using an appropriate supported secure path; assess migration impact before changing an initialized registry/bootstrap. Retain configuration and transport-verification evidence.\n\nEvidence:\n[mpn-core-init-default] mpn-core-init-default\nCore1.14 local setup initializes from Zarf init v0.86.0 without a registry-mode override. bundles/k3d-standard likewise includes that init package without mTLS/proxy selection. Production Core explicitly assumes a separately provided cluster and init bundle, not a DU-operated cluster.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/tasks/setup.yaml\n\n[mpn-registry-mode] mpn-registry-mode\nZarf0.86 FillInEmptyValues defaults internal registry mode to nodeport; MTLSStrategy defaults none. KnownPlainHTTP identifies internal registry without mTLS as HTTP. Proxy mode is a distinct configurable transport.\nhttps://github.com/zarf-dev/zarf/blob/v0.86.0/src/pkg/state/state.go\n\n[mpn-registry-proxy] mpn-registry-proxy\nExact Zarf0.86 init reference documents --registry-mode=proxy as built-in cross-node mTLS. This is a Gallatin bootstrap choice, not unknown vendor behavior. Proxy mode is marked alpha.\nhttps://github.com/zarf-dev/zarf/blob/v0.86.0/site/src/content/docs/commands/zarf_init.md\n\n[mpn-registry-tls-floor] mpn-registry-tls-floor\nThe registry chart configData helper inserts server certificate/key/client CA settings with minimumtls tls1.2 when mtls.enabled. Proxy daemonset uses OPENSSL with client certificate and verified CA; clear node-side listener is local-loopback rather than cross-node plaintext.\nhttps://github.com/zarf-dev/zarf/blob/v0.86.0/packages/zarf-registry/chart/templates/_helpers.tpl",
          "finding_details": "Saved Zarf/Core source evidence identifies an HTTP internal-registry default and supported secure transport options, but that default does not establish the actual MPN image-pull configuration or registry ownership. Application gateway/mesh TLS does not automatically cover node-to-registry image pulls. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work with verification first: inspect actual image-transfer paths and responsibility boundaries, credit existing TLS 1.2+ protection and configure only confirmed gaps. Do not infer that a bootstrap migration is needed from a local setup default.\n\nNext action: In the post-classification verification pass, identify the actual MPN image sources, staging/registry and node image-pull paths and their owners. Inspect deployed registry/runtime/bootstrap settings and verify TLS 1.2 or newer for the applicable transport paths from trusted sources. Credit an existing approved MPN registry. Configure only confirmed gaps using an appropriate supported secure path; assess migration impact before changing an initialized registry/bootstrap. Retain configuration and transport-verification evidence."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233016",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233016",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000014",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233016",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000014",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233016r960759_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233016r960759",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000014-CTR-000040",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must use TLS 1.2 or greater for secure communication.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to use TLS 1.2 or greater for node and component communication.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to verify that TLS 1.2 or greater is being used for communication by the container platform nodes and components. \n\nIf TLS 1.2 or greater is not being used for secure communication, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The authenticity and integrity of the container platform and communication between nodes and components must be secure. If an insecure protocol is used during transmission of data, the data can be intercepted and manipulated. The manipulation of data can be used to inject status changes of the container platform, causing the execution of containers or reporting an incorrect healthcheck. To thwart the manipulation of the data during transmission, a secure protocol (TLS 1.2 or newer) must be used. Further guidance on secure transport protocols can be found in NIST SP 800-52.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000068"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "c4db583c-0a11-5b18-964e-f2ec4212a145",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Core root PeerAuthentication requires STRICT mTLS for enrolled workloads; gateway minimum is TLS1.3 (optionally 1.2), and Horizon declares ambient enrollment. This supports protected traffic without adding application-local TLS everywhere.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-mtls] core-mtls\nRoot-namespace PeerAuthentication requires STRICT mTLS. Horizon opts into ambient mesh; in-mesh plaintext-looking URLs are protected by mesh rather than application TLS.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/common/chart/templates/peer-authentication.yaml\n\n[core-gateway] core-gateway\nGateway redirects HTTP to HTTPS and requires TLS 1.3 by default, optionally TLS 1.2; credentialName references Kubernetes TLS secrets.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/charts/uds-istio-config/templates/gateway.yaml\n\n[mesh] Mesh and policy\nPackage chart declares ambient mesh and generated network policy. Disabled chart-local NetworkPolicy and plaintext application URLs do not establish missing mesh enforcement.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-uds-package/templates/package.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Core root PeerAuthentication requires STRICT mTLS for enrolled workloads; gateway minimum is TLS1.3 (optionally 1.2), and Horizon declares ambient enrollment. This supports protected traffic without adding application-local TLS everywhere.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233019",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233019",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000023",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233019",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000023",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233019r1043176_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233019r1043176",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000023-CTR-000055",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must use a centralized user management solution to support account management functions.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to use a centralized user management system for user management functions.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform to determine if it is using a centralized user management system for user management functions. \n\nIf the container platform is not using a centralized user management system for user management functions, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Enterprise environments make application account management challenging and complex. A manual process for account management functions adds the risk of a potential oversight or other error.\n\nA comprehensive application account management process that includes automation helps to ensure accounts designated as requiring attention are consistently and promptly addressed. Examples include, but are not limited to, using automation to take action on multiple accounts designated as inactive, suspended, or terminated or by disabling accounts located in non-centralized account stores, such as multiple servers. This requirement applies to all account types, including individual/user, shared, group, system, guest/anonymous, emergency, developer/manufacturer/vendor, temporary, and service.\n\nThe application must be configured to automatically provide account management functions, and these functions must immediately enforce the organization's current account policy. The automated mechanisms may reside within the application itself or may be offered by the operating system or other infrastructure-providing automated account management capabilities. Automated mechanisms may be comprised of differing technologies, that when placed together, contain an overall automated mechanism supporting an organization's automated account management requirements.\n\nAccount management functions include: assignment of group or role membership; identifying account type; specifying user access authorizations (i.e., privileges); account removal, update, or termination; and administrative alerts. The use of automated mechanisms can include: using email or text messaging to automatically notify account managers when users are terminated or transferred; using the information system to monitor account usage; or using automated telephonic notification to report atypical system account usage.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000015"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "a4ab1066-0ac8-5966-90e3-80b329a56e46",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS provides centralized Keycloak identity, OIDC/OTP/X509 flows and individual user identities; Grafana rejects unmatched groups and disables its local login. Native cluster/API administration remains MPN-owned and must retain its inheritance mapping.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-keycloak] core-keycloak\nCore uses identity-config 0.32.0, supports X509 and OTP by default; inactivity auto-disable is opt-in, and concurrent-session limit defaults to unlimited (0). This is platform SSO, not Horizon bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[core-id-realm] core-id-realm\nUDS realm defines three-failure lockout, permanentLockout=true, 600-second idle, PBKDF2-SHA256 password policy with 15 characters and classes/history/expiry; JSON event listener is enabled. Some controls remain optional.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[core-grafana] core-grafana\nGrafana disables local login, uses UDS OAuth, maps Admin group to Admin and Auditor to Viewer, and rejects unmatched groups. OAuth secrets are file-mounted.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS provides centralized Keycloak identity, OIDC/OTP/X509 flows and individual user identities; Grafana rejects unmatched groups and disables its local login. Native cluster/API administration remains MPN-owned and must retain its inheritance mapping.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233020",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233020",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000024",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233020",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000024",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233020r1268078_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233020r1268078",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000024-CTR-000060",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must automatically remove or disable temporary user accounts after 72 hours.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to automatically remove or disable temporary user accounts after 72 hours.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to determine if temporary user accounts are automatically removed or disabled after 72 hours. \n\nIf temporary user accounts are not automatically removed or disabled after 72 hours, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "If temporary user accounts remain active when no longer needed or for an excessive period, these accounts may be used to gain unauthorized access. To mitigate this risk, automated termination of all temporary user accounts must be set upon account creation.\n\nTemporary user accounts are established as part of normal account activation procedures when there is a need for short-term accounts without the demand for immediacy in account activation.\n\nIf temporary user accounts are used, the application must be configured to automatically terminate these types of accounts after a DoW-defined period of 72 hours.\n\nTo address access requirements, many application developers choose to integrate their applications with enterprise-level authentication/access mechanisms that meet or exceed access control policy requirements. Such integration allows the application developer to off-load those access control functions and focus on core application features and functionality.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000016"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "651a159d-fa31-523f-b764-697c53fe7fa1",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs N/A justification\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Delivered UDS profile delegates human accounts to shared UDS identity, and neither package nor realm defines a temporary-user account type/provisioning flow. The72-hour rule is a temporary-account control; it should not create an app expiry subsystem when temporary accounts are not used. Record the account-type exclusion, or enable provider expiry if MPN permits temporary accounts.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Record whether MPN permits temporary human accounts; accept N/A for no temporary accounts or configure expiry in the shared IdP.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-platform-delivered-profile] re-platform-delivered-profile\nSuccessful UDS publication2026.45.4-uds.1 records package d528, app b84b9af, Core1.14.0, identity.mode=uds issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds and12 digest-pinned images; no bundled Keycloak image. This is delivered artifact evidence, not assumed default profile.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-platform-realm] re-platform-realm\nUDS realm provides user/admin event logging and JSON event listener, native enabled/account roles, strong default password policy. Inactivity disable workflow defaults false; terms required-action enablement defaults false; CRL checking defaults false with OCSP checking true. No password blacklist or24h-min-age/eight-character-distance in passwordPolicy.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Needs N/A justification. Delivered UDS profile delegates human accounts to shared UDS identity, and neither package nor realm defines a temporary-user account type/provisioning flow. The72-hour rule is a temporary-account control; it should not create an app expiry subsystem when temporary accounts are not used. Record the account-type exclusion, or enable provider expiry if MPN permits temporary accounts.\n\nNext action: Record whether MPN permits temporary human accounts; accept N/A for no temporary accounts or configure expiry in the shared IdP."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233021",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233021",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000025",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233021",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000025",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233021r960774_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233021r960774",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000025-CTR-000065",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must automatically disable accounts after a 35-day period of account inactivity.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to automatically disable accounts after a 35-day period of account inactivity.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Determine if the container platform automatically disables accounts after a 35-day period of account inactivity. \n\nIf the container platform does not automatically disable accounts after a 35-day period of account inactivity, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Attackers that are able to exploit an inactive account can potentially obtain and maintain undetected access to an application. Owners of inactive accounts will not notice if unauthorized access to their user account has been obtained. Applications need to track periods of user inactivity and disable accounts after 35 days of inactivity. Such a process greatly reduces the risk that accounts will be hijacked, leading to a data compromise.\n\nTo address access requirements, many application developers choose to integrate their applications with enterprise-level authentication/access mechanisms that meet or exceed access control policy requirements. Such integration allows the application developer to off-load those access control functions and focus on core application features and functionality.\n\nThis policy does not apply to either emergency accounts or infrequently used accounts. Infrequently used accounts are local login administrator accounts used by system administrators when network or normal logon/access is not available. Emergency accounts are administrator accounts created in response to crisis situations.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000017"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "64091a00-8d69-554c-9ebc-7263a3aaa248",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Actual delivered identity is UDS, so this belongs to the shared platform provider rather than a bundled Horizon realm. Pinned UDS identity0.32 explicitly disables its35-day inactivity workflow unless ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS is supplied. The delivered application profile supplies no such Core override; group/device certificate exceptions may tailor scope but do not turn disabled user automation on. Inactivity implementation already exists in Core and is Gallatin-operated; the source-known disabled default needs Gallatin configuration, not documentation from an external provider. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the same 35-day inactivity decision to similar requirements. Share V-222411 configuration and verification work; confirm current configuration first and preserve only applicable authorized exceptions. The user-account workflow alone does not establish coverage for every group, role or device identifier in broader rules.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Verify current identity/directory inactivity controls and ownership, then configure the existing 35-day workflow for in-scope human accounts if not already enabled, coordinated with V-222411. Persist the Core override and update the existing realm through a controlled change where needed; verify effective disablement. Confirm permitted emergency/infrequently-used account exceptions against the exact rule and retain configuration and test evidence. Credit an existing compliant directory service only where supported by the rule and deployment evidence.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-platform-delivered-profile] re-platform-delivered-profile\nSuccessful UDS publication2026.45.4-uds.1 records package d528, app b84b9af, Core1.14.0, identity.mode=uds issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds and12 digest-pinned images; no bundled Keycloak image. This is delivered artifact evidence, not assumed default profile.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-platform-realm] re-platform-realm\nUDS realm provides user/admin event logging and JSON event listener, native enabled/account roles, strong default password policy. Inactivity disable workflow defaults false; terms required-action enablement defaults false; CRL checking defaults false with OCSP checking true. No password blacklist or24h-min-age/eight-character-distance in passwordPolicy.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-platform-realm-params] re-platform-realm-params\nCore exposes realmInitEnv overrides for ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS, TERMS_AND_CONDITIONS_ENABLED and X509_CRL_CHECKING_ENABLED; these are commented/unset in shipped defaults. detailedObservability.alerts.enabled defaults false.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-values] mpn-identity-values\nCore1.14 chart default fields: realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER=\"0\"; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset; terms enabled unset; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=false; realmAuthFlows username/password/X509/OTPtrue, X509MFAfalse. Gallatin owns these settings on MPN, independent of DU managedIL5/IL6 clusters.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-override] mpn-identity-override\nCore value sourcePath .keycloak.keycloak maps to chart root; bundle override path is packages[core].overrides.keycloak.keycloak.values[].path (e.g.realmInitEnv), not Horizonpackageapplication values.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/common/zarf.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-inactivity] mpn-identity-inactivity\nSet realmInitEnv.ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS=\"35\" before firstimport. Existingrealm: uds Workflows >disable-inactive-users enabled=true, disable-user stepafter35d; realm-admin excluded by shipped workflow. Source establishes exact operator action without asking DU to prove it.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/how-to-guides/identity-and-authorization/configure-account-inactivity-disable.mdx\n\n[mpn-identity-init-only] mpn-identity-init-only\nrealmInitEnv and realmAuthFlows are initial realm import inputs. Existing realm is persisted in DB; redeploying Secret does not change it. Core documents equivalent Keycloak AdminConsole settings. Use controlled non-destructive AdminAPI/console update and persist the desired bundle override; do not advise tearing down production identity.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/how-to-guides/identity-and-authorization/configure-keycloak-login-policies.mdx\n\n[mpn-core-inactivity-setting] mpn-core-inactivity-setting\nGallatin can set keycloak realmInitEnv.ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS=35 to enable initial realm workflow. Source warns realm import is initial-only; existing realm requires directly enabling/updating disable-inactive-users workflow with after35d. Admin accounts are excepted and never-used accounts require handling.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/how-to-guides/identity-and-authorization/configure-account-inactivity-disable.mdx",
          "finding_details": "Actual delivered identity is UDS, so this belongs to the shared platform provider rather than a bundled Horizon realm. Pinned UDS identity0.32 explicitly disables its35-day inactivity workflow unless ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS is supplied. The delivered application profile supplies no such Core override; group/device certificate exceptions may tailor scope but do not turn disabled user automation on. Inactivity implementation already exists in Core and is Gallatin-operated; the source-known disabled default needs Gallatin configuration, not documentation from an external provider. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the same 35-day inactivity decision to similar requirements. Share V-222411 configuration and verification work; confirm current configuration first and preserve only applicable authorized exceptions. The user-account workflow alone does not establish coverage for every group, role or device identifier in broader rules.\n\nNext action: Verify current identity/directory inactivity controls and ownership, then configure the existing 35-day workflow for in-scope human accounts if not already enabled, coordinated with V-222411. Persist the Core override and update the existing realm through a controlled change where needed; verify effective disablement. Confirm permitted emergency/infrequently-used account exceptions against the exact rule and retain configuration and test evidence. Credit an existing compliant directory service only where supported by the rule and deployment evidence."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233022",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233022",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000026",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233022",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000026",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233022r960777_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233022r960777",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000026-CTR-000070",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must automatically audit account creation.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to automatically create audit records on account creation.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to determine if audit records are automatically created upon account creation. \n\nIf audit records are not automatically created upon account creation, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Once an attacker establishes access to a system, the attacker often attempts to create a persistent method of re-establishing access. One way to accomplish this is for the attacker to create a new account. Auditing of account creation is one method for mitigating this risk. A comprehensive account management process will ensure an audit trail documents the creation of application user accounts and, as required, notifies administrators and/or application when accounts are created. Such a process greatly reduces the risk that accounts will be surreptitiously created, and provides logging that can be used for forensic purposes.\n\nTo address access requirements, many application developers choose to integrate their applications with enterprise-level authentication/access/auditing mechanisms that meet or exceed access control policy requirements. Such integration allows the application developer to off-load those access control functions and focus on core application features and functionality.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000018"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "04843f73-8de4-5437-a033-b1c3205765f5",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS realm enables user/admin event logging, and its JSON listener emits event type, time, actor/source/details to stdout. Vector collects those platform records continuously; retain MPN API audit inheritance for native Kubernetes actions.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-id-realm] core-id-realm\nUDS realm defines three-failure lockout, permanentLockout=true, 600-second idle, PBKDF2-SHA256 password policy with 15 characters and classes/history/expiry; JSON event listener is enabled. Some controls remain optional.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[core-id-audit] core-id-audit\nUDS event listener prints user/admin events to stdout with event fields and UTC timestamps. Vector can collect these without a separately configured app forwarding agent.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/plugin/src/main/java/com/defenseunicorns/uds/keycloak/plugin/eventListeners/JSONLogEventListenerProvider.java\n\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS realm enables user/admin event logging, and its JSON listener emits event type, time, actor/source/details to stdout. Vector collects those platform records continuously; retain MPN API audit inheritance for native Kubernetes actions.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233023",
