        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233029",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233029",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000038",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233029",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000038",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233029r1137641_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233029r1137641",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000038-CTR-000105",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must enforce approved authorizations for controlling the flow of information within the container platform based on organization-defined information flow control policies.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to enforce approved authorizations for controlling the flow of information within the container platform based on organization-defined information flow control policies.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform to determine if approved authorizations for controlling the flow of information within the container platform based on organization-defined information flow control policies is being enforced. \n\nIf the organization-defined information flow policies are not being enforced, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Controlling information flow between the container platform components and container user services instantiated by the container platform must enforce organization-defined information flow policies. Example methods for information flow control are using labels and separate namespace for containers to segregate services; user permissions and roles to limit what user services are available to each user; controlling the user the services are able to execute as; and limiting inter-container network traffic and the resources containers can consume.\n\nThis requirement also applies to Zero Trust initiatives.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001368"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "32c3695e-b216-5e4a-8d47-d25b13d8fd53",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS generates default-deny namespace policy and declared Package allow rules; the delivered profile declares ambient mesh and specific UDS issuer/ingress/egress paths. This implements both within-platform and inter-service flow enforcement. Broad IntraNamespace/hosted-model allowances are explicit policy choices to review against MPN approval, not evidence that the platform lacks flow controls or needs a new API implementation.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the declared policy and approved MPN flow list. Track any local-inference/profile mismatch as a deployment-requirement decision rather than duplicating a no-enforcement finding.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-platform-delivered-profile] re-platform-delivered-profile\nSuccessful UDS publication2026.45.4-uds.1 records package d528, app b84b9af, Core1.14.0, identity.mode=uds issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds and12 digest-pinned images; no bundled Keycloak image. This is delivered artifact evidence, not assumed default profile.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-platform-flow] re-platform-flow\nApplication Package CR specifies ambient mesh, explicit ingress, IntraNamespace allows and workload-scoped external egress, including shared UDS issuer rules in uds mode. Those are enforceable declared policies; hosted inference requirement mismatch does not mean no platform flow control exists.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-uds-package/templates/package.yaml\n\n[core-network] core-network\nOperator generates namespace ingress/egress default-deny NetworkPolicy; connectivity requires declared allow rules.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/operator/controllers/network/defaults/default-deny-all.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS generates default-deny namespace policy and declared Package allow rules; the delivered profile declares ambient mesh and specific UDS issuer/ingress/egress paths. This implements both within-platform and inter-service flow enforcement. Broad IntraNamespace/hosted-model allowances are explicit policy choices to review against MPN approval, not evidence that the platform lacks flow controls or needs a new API implementation.\n\nNext action: Retain the declared policy and approved MPN flow list. Track any local-inference/profile mismatch as a deployment-requirement decision rather than duplicating a no-enforcement finding."
        },
