          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Loki is network-isolated to named service identities; Grafana grants Admin/Auditor roles only, and log collection/storage are separate from application namespaces. This supports protection from ordinary application users; object-store administrative IAM is an inherited residual.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-loki] core-loki\nLoki uses ambient mesh; ingress is limited to Grafana, Vector, FalcoSidekick and monitoring service identities; object-store egress is declared separately.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/loki/chart/templates/uds-package.yaml\n\n[core-grafana] core-grafana\nGrafana disables local login, uses UDS OAuth, maps Admin group to Admin and Auditor to Viewer, and rejects unmatched groups. OAuth secrets are file-mounted.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml\n\n[core-network] core-network\nOperator generates namespace ingress/egress default-deny NetworkPolicy; connectivity requires declared allow rules.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/operator/controllers/network/defaults/default-deny-all.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Loki is network-isolated to named service identities; Grafana grants Admin/Auditor roles only, and log collection/storage are separate from application namespaces. This supports protection from ordinary application users; object-store administrative IAM is an inherited residual.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233063",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233063",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000126",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233063",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000126",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233063r960951_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233063r960951",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000126-CTR-000275",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must use FIPS validated cryptographic mechanisms to protect the integrity of log information.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to use FIPS-validated cryptographic mechanisms to protect the integrity of log information.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to determine if FIPS-validated cryptographic mechanisms are being used to protect the integrity of log information. \n\nIf FIPS-validated cryptographic mechanisms are not being used to protect the integrity of log information, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "To fully investigate an incident and to have trust in the audit data that is generated, it is important to put in place data protections. Without integrity protections, unauthorized changes may be made to the audit files and reliable forensic analysis and discovery of the source of malicious system activity may be degraded. Although digital signatures are one example of protecting integrity, this control is not intended to cause a new cryptographic hash to be generated every time a record is added to a log file. \n\nIntegrity protections can also be implemented by using cryptographic techniques for security function isolation and file system protections to protect against unauthorized changes.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001350"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "62e442de-6877-57ef-8bd8-466b54b8705f",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS's supported unicorn flavor selects curated FIPS mesh/identity images, and Core Keycloak explicitly starts in strict FIPS mode. This supplies a concrete offered platform implementation for the prescribed cryptography. Recommendation is conditional on selecting that hardened flavor; upstream/demo flavor does not inherit it.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Bind the MPN Core release/flavor/digests to vendor module-validation evidence and approved mode; do not apply this ruling to an upstream/demo Core deployment.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-flavors] core-flavors\nCore distinguishes unicorn (curated FIPS-validated images), registry1 (Iron Bank hardening) and upstream (development/community images). The production hardened offering exists; actual selected flavor/digests and module mapping remain explicit deployment inputs.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/concepts/platform/flavors.mdx\n\n[core-hardened-mesh] core-hardened-mesh\nUnicorn flavor selects curated istio-pilot-fips and istio-proxy-fips images; ambient ztunnel has its own FIPS image override. This proves the offered implementation, not the flavor of a deployed MPN cluster.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/values/unicorn/istiod.yaml\n\n[core-strict-keycloak] core-strict-keycloak\nCore Keycloak starts with --features=fips and --fips-mode=strict; cgr image handling adds the FIPS JCE security setup. Bundled Horizon Keycloak is a different chart and must not inherit these flags by name alone.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/statefulset.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS's supported unicorn flavor selects curated FIPS mesh/identity images, and Core Keycloak explicitly starts in strict FIPS mode. This supplies a concrete offered platform implementation for the prescribed cryptography. Recommendation is conditional on selecting that hardened flavor; upstream/demo flavor does not inherit it.\n\nNext action: Bind the MPN Core release/flavor/digests to vendor module-validation evidence and approved mode; do not apply this ruling to an upstream/demo Core deployment."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233064",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233064",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000131",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233064",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000131",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233064r981843_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233064r981843",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000131-CTR-000280",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must be built from verified packages.",
          "fix_text": "Rebuild the container platform from verified packages that are digitally signed by known and approved sources.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to verify it has been built from packages that are digitally signed by known and approved sources. \n\nIf the container platform was built from packages that are not digitally signed or are from unknown or nonapproved sources, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "It is important to patch and upgrade the container platform when patches and upgrades are available. More important is to get these patches and upgrades from a known source. To validate the authenticity of any patches and upgrades before installation, the container platform must check that the files are digitally signed by sources approved by the organization.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-003992"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "fa720e4f-e614-5cef-a8da-346bc900e259",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS/Zarf packages enumerate delivered components/images and release provenance; Kubernetes RBAC restricts deployment changes, Pepr admission restricts runnable workloads, and immutable image-based upgrades replace component files. Registry garbage collection is a separate rule.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[images] Image release process\nBuilder verifies source digests and produces archive hashes; sample values with empty digests do not establish that a built release is unpinned. Assessed release/SBOM/scans still needed.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/scripts/build-bundle.py\n\n[core-policy] core-policy\nPepr mutates/validates non-root execution, no privilege escalation and dropped capabilities, including init and ephemeral containers; exemptions are explicit.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts\n\n[app-namespace] app-namespace\nAll declared workload charts target horizon-uds; package supplies dedicated namespace, distinct workload charts and generated deployment credentials.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/zarf.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS/Zarf packages enumerate delivered components/images and release provenance; Kubernetes RBAC restricts deployment changes, Pepr admission restricts runnable workloads, and immutable image-based upgrades replace component files. Registry garbage collection is a separate rule.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233065",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233065",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000131",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233065",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000131",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233065r981844_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233065r981844",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000131-CTR-000285",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must verify container images.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to verify container images are digitally signed and the signature is from a recognized and approved source.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to determine if container images are verified by enforcing image signing and that the image is signed recognized by an approved source. \n\nIf container images are not verified or the signature is not verified as a recognized and approved source, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The container platform must be capable of validating container images are signed and that the digital signature is from a recognized and approved source approved by the organization. Allowing any container image to be introduced into the registry and instantiated into a container can allow for services to be introduced that are not trusted and may contain malicious code, which introduces unwanted services. These unwanted services can cause harm and security risks to the hosting server, the container platform, other services running within the container platform, and the overall organization.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-003992"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "85316eb6-d61e-5980-a7f7-3654852248cb",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Whole-product approved delivery verifies real code-signing certificate identity and DSSE signatures, binds image digests inside exact packages, requires CAT vouch and publishes a digest-fixed bundle. PostgreSQL and application components are installed through that controlled immutable package path. This supplies a source-backed signed-package equivalent; lack of a second generic Pepr image-signature plugin is not by itself an application remediation finding. Acceptance of package-boundary verification versus every arbitrary cluster admission remains explicit tailoring, not a claimed universal admission policy.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the approved package-level signer/trust mapping and restrict deployment to this verified immutable release path; assess arbitrary cluster-admin installation separately.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-images-release] re-images-release\nSuccessful Nov5 trusted release2026.45.4-uds.1 binds Horizonb84b9af/build19710/run42109769948, package d528b7f, Core1.14.0, actual identity.mode=uds issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. Twelve delivered image platform digests are explicit; no bundled Keycloak is shipped. Published package digest sha256:486f55939f94ab081d2a12ecade5439512f8f7c4b3119c40b027c711d386adc9. Read build-info/platform-contract/publication metadata artifact13464284834, not just example chart defaults.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-images-verify] re-images-verify\nReal signer verification uses pinned DU Fulcio/Sigstore TSA anchors, expected subject/SAN/issuer, P256/SHA256 DSSE signature, code-signing EKU and RFC3161 timestamp. release.py check_evidence calls verify_production for all required phases and binds exact archives/build-info/packed image digests; CAT vouch gates publication. Actual delivery run succeeded these gates.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/scripts/ci/verify_attestation.py\n\n[re-images-publish] re-images-publish\nThe main-only protected release workflow selects qualified successful main source, validates image inventory/source digests against packed OCI manifests, binds archive SHA256SUMS, requires cryptographically verified Witness evidence and CAT vouch before registry publication; digest-qualified registry bundle fixes installation content.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/scripts/ci/release.py",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Whole-product approved delivery verifies real code-signing certificate identity and DSSE signatures, binds image digests inside exact packages, requires CAT vouch and publishes a digest-fixed bundle. PostgreSQL and application components are installed through that controlled immutable package path. This supplies a source-backed signed-package equivalent; lack of a second generic Pepr image-signature plugin is not by itself an application remediation finding. Acceptance of package-boundary verification versus every arbitrary cluster admission remains explicit tailoring, not a claimed universal admission policy.\n\nNext action: Retain the approved package-level signer/trust mapping and restrict deployment to this verified immutable release path; assess arbitrary cluster-admin installation separately."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233066",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233066",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000133",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
