          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS namespace default-deny and explicit Package ports govern exposure, while admission denies unapproved host ports/NodePort. The approved PPSM list must match the final MPN profile; no blanket raw-network access is inferred.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-network] core-network\nOperator generates namespace ingress/egress default-deny NetworkPolicy; connectivity requires declared allow rules.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/operator/controllers/network/defaults/default-deny-all.ts\n\n[core-policy] core-policy\nPepr mutates/validates non-root execution, no privilege escalation and dropped capabilities, including init and ephemeral containers; exemptions are explicit.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts\n\n[mesh] Mesh and policy\nPackage chart declares ambient mesh and generated network policy. Disabled chart-local NetworkPolicy and plaintext application URLs do not establish missing mesh enforcement.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-uds-package/templates/package.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS namespace default-deny and explicit Package ports govern exposure, while admission denies unapproved host ports/NodePort. The approved PPSM list must match the final MPN profile; no blanket raw-network access is inferred.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233074",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233074",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000142",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233074",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000142",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233074r1043177_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233074r1043177",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000142-CTR-000330",
          "rule_title": "The container platform runtime must enforce the use of ports that are non-privileged.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to disallow the use of privileged ports by containers. Move any containers that are using privileged ports to non-privileged ports.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration and the containers within the platform by performing the following checks:\n\n1. Verify the container platform is configured to disallow the use of privileged ports by containers.\n2. Validate all containers within the container platform are using non-privileged ports.\n3. Attempt to instantiate a container image that uses a privileged port.\n\nIf the container platform is not configured to disallow the use of privileged ports, this is a finding.\n\nIf the container platform has containers using privileged ports, this is a finding.\n\nIf the container platform allows containers to be instantiated that use privileged ports, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Privileged ports are those ports below 1024 and that require system privileges for their use. If containers are able to use these ports, the container must be run as a privileged user. The container platform must stop containers that try to map to these ports directly. Allowing non-privileged ports to be mapped to the container-privileged port is the allowable method when a certain port is needed. An example is mapping port 8080 externally to port 80 in the container.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
