          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS provides centralized Keycloak identity, OIDC/OTP/X509 flows and individual user identities; Grafana rejects unmatched groups and disables its local login. Native cluster/API administration remains MPN-owned and must retain its inheritance mapping.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-keycloak] core-keycloak\nCore uses identity-config 0.32.0, supports X509 and OTP by default; inactivity auto-disable is opt-in, and concurrent-session limit defaults to unlimited (0). This is platform SSO, not Horizon bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[core-id-realm] core-id-realm\nUDS realm defines three-failure lockout, permanentLockout=true, 600-second idle, PBKDF2-SHA256 password policy with 15 characters and classes/history/expiry; JSON event listener is enabled. Some controls remain optional.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[core-grafana] core-grafana\nGrafana disables local login, uses UDS OAuth, maps Admin group to Admin and Auditor to Viewer, and rejects unmatched groups. OAuth secrets are file-mounted.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS provides centralized Keycloak identity, OIDC/OTP/X509 flows and individual user identities; Grafana rejects unmatched groups and disables its local login. Native cluster/API administration remains MPN-owned and must retain its inheritance mapping.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233079",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233079",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000149",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233079",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000149",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233079r960972_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233079r960972",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000149-CTR-000355",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must use multifactor authentication for network access to privileged accounts.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to use multifactor authentication for network access to privileged accounts.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to determine if the container platform is configured to use multifactor authentication for network access to privileged accounts. \n\nIf the container platform does not use multifactor authentication for network access to privileged accounts, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Without the use of multifactor authentication, the ease of access to privileged functions is greatly increased.\n\nMultifactor authentication requires using two or more factors to achieve authentication.\n\nFactors include: \n(i) something a user knows (e.g., password/PIN); \n(ii) something a user has (e.g., cryptographic identification device, token); or \n(iii) something a user is (e.g., biometric).\n\nA privileged account is defined as an information system account with authorizations of a privileged user.\n\nNetwork access is defined as access to an information system by a user (or a process acting on behalf of a user) communicating through a network (e.g., local area network, wide area network, or the internet).",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000765"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "af8fcf60-5f3c-553b-95aa-44979f3374ce",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Core supplies OTP/X509-capable SSO, token-based authenticated identities and SPIFFE/mTLS workload identity. Device strength and native host/local access remain MPN policy/inheritance conditions, rather than missing Horizon features.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-keycloak] core-keycloak\nCore uses identity-config 0.32.0, supports X509 and OTP by default; inactivity auto-disable is opt-in, and concurrent-session limit defaults to unlimited (0). This is platform SSO, not Horizon bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[core-id-realm] core-id-realm\nUDS realm defines three-failure lockout, permanentLockout=true, 600-second idle, PBKDF2-SHA256 password policy with 15 characters and classes/history/expiry; JSON event listener is enabled. Some controls remain optional.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[core-mtls] core-mtls\nRoot-namespace PeerAuthentication requires STRICT mTLS. Horizon opts into ambient mesh; in-mesh plaintext-looking URLs are protected by mesh rather than application TLS.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/common/chart/templates/peer-authentication.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Core supplies OTP/X509-capable SSO, token-based authenticated identities and SPIFFE/mTLS workload identity. Device strength and native host/local access remain MPN policy/inheritance conditions, rather than missing Horizon features.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
