        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233087",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233087",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000163",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233087",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000163",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233087r981853_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233087r981853",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000163-CTR-000395",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must disable identifiers (individuals, groups, roles, and devices) after 35 days of inactivity.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to disable identifiers (individuals, groups, roles, and devices) after 35 days of inactivity.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to determine if the container platform is configured to disable identifiers (individuals, groups, roles, and devices) after 35 days of inactivity. \n\nIf identifiers are not disabled after 35 days of inactivity, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Inactive identifiers pose a risk to systems and applications. Attackers that are able to exploit an inactive identifier can potentially obtain and maintain undetected access to the application. Owners of inactive accounts will not notice if unauthorized access to their user account has been obtained.\n\nApplications need to track periods of inactivity and disable application identifiers after 35 days of inactivity.\n\nManagement of user identifiers is not applicable to shared information system accounts (e.g., guest and anonymous accounts). It is commonly the case that a user account is the name of an information system account associated with an individual.\n\nTo avoid having to build complex user management capabilities directly into their application, wise developers leverage the underlying OS or other user account management infrastructure (AD, LDAP) that is already in place within the organization and meets organizational user account management requirements.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-003627"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "5f3f8943-cd7f-533f-b214-e0a3a4a1781f",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Actual delivered identity is UDS, so this belongs to the shared platform provider rather than a bundled Horizon realm. Pinned UDS identity0.32 explicitly disables its35-day inactivity workflow unless ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS is supplied. The delivered application profile supplies no such Core override; group/device certificate exceptions may tailor scope but do not turn disabled user automation on. Inactivity implementation already exists in Core and is Gallatin-operated; the source-known disabled default needs Gallatin configuration, not documentation from an external provider. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the same 35-day inactivity decision to similar requirements. Share V-222411 configuration and verification work; confirm current configuration first and preserve only applicable authorized exceptions. The user-account workflow alone does not establish coverage for every group, role or device identifier in broader rules.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Verify current identity/directory inactivity controls and ownership, then configure the existing 35-day workflow for in-scope human accounts if not already enabled, coordinated with V-222411. Persist the Core override and update the existing realm through a controlled change where needed; verify effective disablement. For this broader identifier rule, separately map individuals, groups, roles and devices to appropriate lifecycle controls or justified scope decisions; do not assume disabling human accounts covers all identifier types. Confirm permitted emergency/infrequently-used account exceptions against the exact rule and retain configuration and test evidence. Credit an existing compliant directory service only where supported by the rule and deployment evidence.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-platform-delivered-profile] re-platform-delivered-profile\nSuccessful UDS publication2026.45.4-uds.1 records package d528, app b84b9af, Core1.14.0, identity.mode=uds issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds and12 digest-pinned images; no bundled Keycloak image. This is delivered artifact evidence, not assumed default profile.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-platform-realm] re-platform-realm\nUDS realm provides user/admin event logging and JSON event listener, native enabled/account roles, strong default password policy. Inactivity disable workflow defaults false; terms required-action enablement defaults false; CRL checking defaults false with OCSP checking true. No password blacklist or24h-min-age/eight-character-distance in passwordPolicy.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-platform-realm-params] re-platform-realm-params\nCore exposes realmInitEnv overrides for ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS, TERMS_AND_CONDITIONS_ENABLED and X509_CRL_CHECKING_ENABLED; these are commented/unset in shipped defaults. detailedObservability.alerts.enabled defaults false.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-values] mpn-identity-values\nCore1.14 chart default fields: realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER=\"0\"; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset; terms enabled unset; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=false; realmAuthFlows username/password/X509/OTPtrue, X509MFAfalse. Gallatin owns these settings on MPN, independent of DU managedIL5/IL6 clusters.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-override] mpn-identity-override\nCore value sourcePath .keycloak.keycloak maps to chart root; bundle override path is packages[core].overrides.keycloak.keycloak.values[].path (e.g.realmInitEnv), not Horizonpackageapplication values.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/common/zarf.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-inactivity] mpn-identity-inactivity\nSet realmInitEnv.ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS=\"35\" before firstimport. Existingrealm: uds Workflows >disable-inactive-users enabled=true, disable-user stepafter35d; realm-admin excluded by shipped workflow. Source establishes exact operator action without asking DU to prove it.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/how-to-guides/identity-and-authorization/configure-account-inactivity-disable.mdx\n\n[mpn-identity-init-only] mpn-identity-init-only\nrealmInitEnv and realmAuthFlows are initial realm import inputs. Existing realm is persisted in DB; redeploying Secret does not change it. Core documents equivalent Keycloak AdminConsole settings. Use controlled non-destructive AdminAPI/console update and persist the desired bundle override; do not advise tearing down production identity.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/how-to-guides/identity-and-authorization/configure-keycloak-login-policies.mdx\n\n[mpn-core-inactivity-setting] mpn-core-inactivity-setting\nGallatin can set keycloak realmInitEnv.ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS=35 to enable initial realm workflow. Source warns realm import is initial-only; existing realm requires directly enabling/updating disable-inactive-users workflow with after35d. Admin accounts are excepted and never-used accounts require handling.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/how-to-guides/identity-and-authorization/configure-account-inactivity-disable.mdx",
          "finding_details": "Actual delivered identity is UDS, so this belongs to the shared platform provider rather than a bundled Horizon realm. Pinned UDS identity0.32 explicitly disables its35-day inactivity workflow unless ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS is supplied. The delivered application profile supplies no such Core override; group/device certificate exceptions may tailor scope but do not turn disabled user automation on. Inactivity implementation already exists in Core and is Gallatin-operated; the source-known disabled default needs Gallatin configuration, not documentation from an external provider. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the same 35-day inactivity decision to similar requirements. Share V-222411 configuration and verification work; confirm current configuration first and preserve only applicable authorized exceptions. The user-account workflow alone does not establish coverage for every group, role or device identifier in broader rules.\n\nNext action: Verify current identity/directory inactivity controls and ownership, then configure the existing 35-day workflow for in-scope human accounts if not already enabled, coordinated with V-222411. Persist the Core override and update the existing realm through a controlled change where needed; verify effective disablement. For this broader identifier rule, separately map individuals, groups, roles and devices to appropriate lifecycle controls or justified scope decisions; do not assume disabling human accounts covers all identifier types. Confirm permitted emergency/infrequently-used account exceptions against the exact rule and retain configuration and test evidence. Credit an existing compliant directory service only where supported by the rule and deployment evidence."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233088",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233088",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000164",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233088",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000164",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233088r981854_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233088r981854",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000164-CTR-000400",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must enforce a minimum 15-character password length.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to enforce a minimum 15-character password length.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to determine if the container platform enforces a minimum 15-character password length. \n\nIf the container platform does not enforce a 15-character password length, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The shorter the password, the lower the number of possible combinations that need to be tested before the password is compromised.\n\nPassword complexity, or strength, is a measure of the effectiveness of a password in resisting attempts at guessing and brute-force attacks. Password length is one factor of several that helps to determine strength and how long it takes to crack a password. The shorter the password, the lower the number of possible combinations that need to be tested before the password is compromised.\n\nUse of more characters in a password helps to exponentially increase the time and/or resources required to compromise the password.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-004066"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "e76187e0-905d-5b9a-8b54-5962e19fa479",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS identity-config0.32.0 defines three-failure lockout, permanent lockout, 15-character password policy with required classes and 60-day expiry, and 600-second session idle. These are concrete platform defaults; Horizon bundled realm remains separately assessed.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-id-realm] core-id-realm\nUDS realm defines three-failure lockout, permanentLockout=true, 600-second idle, PBKDF2-SHA256 password policy with 15 characters and classes/history/expiry; JSON event listener is enabled. Some controls remain optional.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[core-keycloak] core-keycloak\nCore uses identity-config 0.32.0, supports X509 and OTP by default; inactivity auto-disable is opt-in, and concurrent-session limit defaults to unlimited (0). This is platform SSO, not Horizon bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS identity-config0.32.0 defines three-failure lockout, permanent lockout, 15-character password policy with required classes and 60-day expiry, and 600-second session idle. These are concrete platform defaults; Horizon bundled realm remains separately assessed.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
