        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233094",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233094",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000170",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233094",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000170",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233094r1107119_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233094r1107119",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000170-CTR-000430",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must require the change of at least eight of the total number of characters when passwords are changed.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to require the change of at least eight of the total number of characters when passwords are changed.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to determine if it requires the change of at least eight of the total number of characters when passwords are changed. \n\nIf the container platform does not require the change of at least eight of the total number of characters when passwords are changed, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "If the application allows the user to consecutively reuse extensive portions of passwords, this increases the chances of password compromise by increasing the window of opportunity for attempts at guessing and brute-force attacks.\n\nThe number of changed characters refers to the number of changes required with respect to the total number of positions in the current password. In other words, characters may be the same within the two passwords; however, the positions of the like characters must be different.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-004066"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "342ab6c7-e0ff-571d-bf17-caa42f62b170",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The actual shared UDS realm supplies strong length/class/history/expiry defaults, but the inspected passwordPolicy still does not enforce this exact eight-character-change or24-hour minimum-age requirement for password users. These are IdP policy/tailoring decisions, not missing API features. Certificate-only/delegated account paths can justify rule-specific exceptions if they are the selected path. This is a known provider policy gap/tailoring decision, not missing proof of an existing Core flag or a reason to duplicate identity in Horizon.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Gallatin resolves the exact eight-character difference or24-hour minimum-change requirement for permitted human-password accounts: use an approved central provider policy extension or accepted certificate-only/account-type tailoring. Core supplies no matching defaultflag; native passwordAge is password-reuse age, not a minimum changeinterval. Do not weaken existing password constraints or invent a supported Helm switch.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-platform-delivered-profile] re-platform-delivered-profile\nSuccessful UDS publication2026.45.4-uds.1 records package d528, app b84b9af, Core1.14.0, identity.mode=uds issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds and12 digest-pinned images; no bundled Keycloak image. This is delivered artifact evidence, not assumed default profile.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-platform-realm] re-platform-realm\nUDS realm provides user/admin event logging and JSON event listener, native enabled/account roles, strong default password policy. Inactivity disable workflow defaults false; terms required-action enablement defaults false; CRL checking defaults false with OCSP checking true. No password blacklist or24h-min-age/eight-character-distance in passwordPolicy.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-platform-realm-params] re-platform-realm-params\nCore exposes realmInitEnv overrides for ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS, TERMS_AND_CONDITIONS_ENABLED and X509_CRL_CHECKING_ENABLED; these are commented/unset in shipped defaults. detailedObservability.alerts.enabled defaults false.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-values] mpn-identity-values\nCore1.14 chart default fields: realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER=\"0\"; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset; terms enabled unset; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=false; realmAuthFlows username/password/X509/OTPtrue, X509MFAfalse. Gallatin owns these settings on MPN, independent of DU managedIL5/IL6 clusters.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-override] mpn-identity-override\nCore value sourcePath .keycloak.keycloak maps to chart root; bundle override path is packages[core].overrides.keycloak.keycloak.values[].path (e.g.realmInitEnv), not Horizonpackageapplication values.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/common/zarf.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-realm] mpn-identity-realm\nKnowndefaults: idle600sec,maxlifespan36000sec; sessionlimit0; permanentlockout threshold3; default passwords length15/classes/history5/expiry60. CRL cachechecking disabled, OCSP checking enabled/failclosed; no eightcharacter-distance/24h minchange interval/selecteddenylist. Explicit source gaps are config/tailoring work, not Unknown.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[mpn-identity-age] mpn-identity-age\nNative passwordAge policy is historical-password reuse-age validation, not24-hour minimum time before changing to a differentnewpassword. Do not falsely propose passwordAge(1) for minchangeinterval; no Coredefault/customvalidator implementing8characterdistance/24hminchange supplied.\nhttps://github.com/keycloak/keycloak/blob/26.7.4/server-spi-private/src/main/java/org/keycloak/policy/AgePasswordPolicyProvider.java",
          "finding_details": "The actual shared UDS realm supplies strong length/class/history/expiry defaults, but the inspected passwordPolicy still does not enforce this exact eight-character-change or24-hour minimum-age requirement for password users. These are IdP policy/tailoring decisions, not missing API features. Certificate-only/delegated account paths can justify rule-specific exceptions if they are the selected path. This is a known provider policy gap/tailoring decision, not missing proof of an existing Core flag or a reason to duplicate identity in Horizon.\n\nNext action: Gallatin resolves the exact eight-character difference or24-hour minimum-change requirement for permitted human-password accounts: use an approved central provider policy extension or accepted certificate-only/account-type tailoring. Core supplies no matching defaultflag; native passwordAge is password-reuse age, not a minimum changeinterval. Do not weaken existing password constraints or invent a supported Helm switch."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233095",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233095",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000171",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233095",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000171",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233095r1268090_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233095r1268090",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000171-CTR-000435",
          "rule_title": "For container platform using password authentication, the application must store only cryptographic representations of passwords.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to store only cryptographic representations of passwords if passwords are being used for authentication.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to determine if it using password authentication and stores only cryptographic representations of the passwords. \n\nIf the container platform is using password authentication and does not store only cryptographic representations of passwords, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Passwords need to be protected at all times, and encryption is the standard method for protecting passwords. If passwords are not encrypted, they can be plainly read and easily compromised. Use of passwords for authentication is intended only for limited situations and should not be used as a replacement for two-factor common access card (CAC)-enabled authentication.\n\nExamples of situations where a user ID and password might be used include:\n\n- When the user does not use a CAC and is not a current DoW employee, member of the military, or DoW contractor.\n\n- When a user has been officially designated as temporarily unable to present a CAC for some reason (lost, damaged, not yet issued, broken card reader) (i.e., Temporary Exception User) and to satisfy urgent organizational needs must be temporarily permitted to use user ID/password authentication until the problem with CAC use has been remedied.\n\n- When the application is publicly available and or hosting publicly releasable data requiring some degree of need-to-know protection.\n\nIf the password is already encrypted and not a plaintext password, this meets this requirement. Implementation of this requirement requires configuration of FIPS-approved cipher block algorithm and block cipher modes for encryption. This method uses a one-way hashing encryption algorithm with a salt value to validate a user's password without having to store the actual password. Performance and time required to access are factors that must be considered, and the one-way hash is the most feasible means of securing the password and providing an acceptable measure of password security.\n\nVerifying the user knows a password is performed using a password verifier. In its simplest form, a password verifier is a computational function that is capable of creating a hash of a password and determining if the value provided by the user matches the hash. A more secure version of verifying a user knowing a password is to store the result of an iterating hash function and a large random salt value as follows:\n\nH0 = H(pwd, H(salt))\nHn = H(Hn-1,H(salt))\n\nIn the above, \"n\" is a cryptographically-strong random [*3] number. \"Hn\" is stored along with the salt. When the application wishes to verify that the user knows a password, it simply repeats the process and compares \"Hn\" with the stored \"Hn\". A salt is essentially a fixed-length cryptographically strong random value.\n\nAnother method is using a keyed-hash message authentication code (HMAC). HMAC calculates a message authentication code via a cryptographic hash function used in conjunction with an encryption key. The key must be protected as with any private key.\n\nThis requirement applies to all accounts including authentication server, AAA, and local account, including the root account and the account of last resort.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-004062"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "2cfa8268-3be6-5667-8476-c2396e272591",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS identity-config0.32.0 defines three-failure lockout, permanent lockout, 15-character password policy with required classes and 60-day expiry, and 600-second session idle. These are concrete platform defaults; Horizon bundled realm remains separately assessed.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-id-realm] core-id-realm\nUDS realm defines three-failure lockout, permanentLockout=true, 600-second idle, PBKDF2-SHA256 password policy with 15 characters and classes/history/expiry; JSON event listener is enabled. Some controls remain optional.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[core-keycloak] core-keycloak\nCore uses identity-config 0.32.0, supports X509 and OTP by default; inactivity auto-disable is opt-in, and concurrent-session limit defaults to unlimited (0). This is platform SSO, not Horizon bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS identity-config0.32.0 defines three-failure lockout, permanent lockout, 15-character password policy with required classes and 60-day expiry, and 600-second session idle. These are concrete platform defaults; Horizon bundled realm remains separately assessed.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233096",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233096",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000172",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233096",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000172",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233096r961029_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233096r961029",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000172-CTR-000440",
          "rule_title": "For accounts using password authentication, the container platform must use FIPS-validated SHA-2 or later protocol to protect the integrity of the password authentication process.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to transmit only encrypted FIPS-validated SHA-2 or later representations of passwords.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the documentation and configuration to determine if the container platform enforces the required FIPS-validated encrypt passwords when they are transmitted. \n\nIf the container platform is not configured to meet this requirement, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Passwords need to be protected on entry, in transmission, during authentication, and when stored. If compromised at any of these security points, a nefarious user can use the password along with stolen user account information to gain access or to escalate privileges. The container platform may require account authentication during container platform tasks and before accessing container platform components, e.g. runtime, registry, and keystore.\n\nDuring any user authentication, the container platform must use FIPS-validated SHA-2 or later protocol to protect the integrity of the password authentication process.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000197"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "05a63e16-a786-5ef2-a8f3-cbec660e0b87",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS's supported unicorn flavor selects curated FIPS mesh/identity images, and Core Keycloak explicitly starts in strict FIPS mode. This supplies a concrete offered platform implementation for the prescribed cryptography. Recommendation is conditional on selecting that hardened flavor; upstream/demo flavor does not inherit it.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Bind the MPN Core release/flavor/digests to vendor module-validation evidence and approved mode; do not apply this ruling to an upstream/demo Core deployment.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-flavors] core-flavors\nCore distinguishes unicorn (curated FIPS-validated images), registry1 (Iron Bank hardening) and upstream (development/community images). The production hardened offering exists; actual selected flavor/digests and module mapping remain explicit deployment inputs.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/concepts/platform/flavors.mdx\n\n[core-hardened-mesh] core-hardened-mesh\nUnicorn flavor selects curated istio-pilot-fips and istio-proxy-fips images; ambient ztunnel has its own FIPS image override. This proves the offered implementation, not the flavor of a deployed MPN cluster.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/values/unicorn/istiod.yaml\n\n[core-strict-keycloak] core-strict-keycloak\nCore Keycloak starts with --features=fips and --fips-mode=strict; cgr image handling adds the FIPS JCE security setup. Bundled Horizon Keycloak is a different chart and must not inherit these flags by name alone.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/statefulset.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS's supported unicorn flavor selects curated FIPS mesh/identity images, and Core Keycloak explicitly starts in strict FIPS mode. This supplies a concrete offered platform implementation for the prescribed cryptography. Recommendation is conditional on selecting that hardened flavor; upstream/demo flavor does not inherit it.\n\nNext action: Bind the MPN Core release/flavor/digests to vendor module-validation evidence and approved mode; do not apply this ruling to an upstream/demo Core deployment."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233097",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233097",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000173",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233097",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000173",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233097r981862_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233097r981862",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000173-CTR-000445",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must enforce 24 hours (one day) as the minimum password lifetime.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to enforce 24 hours/1 day as the minimum password lifetime.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to determine if it enforces 24 hours/1 day as the minimum password lifetime. \n\nIf the container platform does not enforce 24 hours/1 day as the minimum password lifetime, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Enforcing a minimum password lifetime helps prevent repeated password changes to defeat the password reuse or history enforcement requirement.\n\nRestricting this setting limits the user's ability to change their password. Passwords need to be changed at specific policy-based intervals; however, if the application allows the user to immediately and continually change their password, then the password could be repeatedly changed in a short period of time to defeat the organization's policy regarding password reuse.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-004066"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "e10bd06c-0f08-5d65-b2b9-16a20171594a",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The actual shared UDS realm supplies strong length/class/history/expiry defaults, but the inspected passwordPolicy still does not enforce this exact eight-character-change or24-hour minimum-age requirement for password users. These are IdP policy/tailoring decisions, not missing API features. Certificate-only/delegated account paths can justify rule-specific exceptions if they are the selected path. This is a known provider policy gap/tailoring decision, not missing proof of an existing Core flag or a reason to duplicate identity in Horizon.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Gallatin resolves the exact eight-character difference or24-hour minimum-change requirement for permitted human-password accounts: use an approved central provider policy extension or accepted certificate-only/account-type tailoring. Core supplies no matching defaultflag; native passwordAge is password-reuse age, not a minimum changeinterval. Do not weaken existing password constraints or invent a supported Helm switch.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-platform-delivered-profile] re-platform-delivered-profile\nSuccessful UDS publication2026.45.4-uds.1 records package d528, app b84b9af, Core1.14.0, identity.mode=uds issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds and12 digest-pinned images; no bundled Keycloak image. This is delivered artifact evidence, not assumed default profile.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-platform-realm] re-platform-realm\nUDS realm provides user/admin event logging and JSON event listener, native enabled/account roles, strong default password policy. Inactivity disable workflow defaults false; terms required-action enablement defaults false; CRL checking defaults false with OCSP checking true. No password blacklist or24h-min-age/eight-character-distance in passwordPolicy.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-platform-realm-params] re-platform-realm-params\nCore exposes realmInitEnv overrides for ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS, TERMS_AND_CONDITIONS_ENABLED and X509_CRL_CHECKING_ENABLED; these are commented/unset in shipped defaults. detailedObservability.alerts.enabled defaults false.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-values] mpn-identity-values\nCore1.14 chart default fields: realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER=\"0\"; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset; terms enabled unset; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=false; realmAuthFlows username/password/X509/OTPtrue, X509MFAfalse. Gallatin owns these settings on MPN, independent of DU managedIL5/IL6 clusters.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-override] mpn-identity-override\nCore value sourcePath .keycloak.keycloak maps to chart root; bundle override path is packages[core].overrides.keycloak.keycloak.values[].path (e.g.realmInitEnv), not Horizonpackageapplication values.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/common/zarf.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-realm] mpn-identity-realm\nKnowndefaults: idle600sec,maxlifespan36000sec; sessionlimit0; permanentlockout threshold3; default passwords length15/classes/history5/expiry60. CRL cachechecking disabled, OCSP checking enabled/failclosed; no eightcharacter-distance/24h minchange interval/selecteddenylist. Explicit source gaps are config/tailoring work, not Unknown.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[mpn-identity-age] mpn-identity-age\nNative passwordAge policy is historical-password reuse-age validation, not24-hour minimum time before changing to a differentnewpassword. Do not falsely propose passwordAge(1) for minchangeinterval; no Coredefault/customvalidator implementing8characterdistance/24hminchange supplied.\nhttps://github.com/keycloak/keycloak/blob/26.7.4/server-spi-private/src/main/java/org/keycloak/policy/AgePasswordPolicyProvider.java",
          "finding_details": "The actual shared UDS realm supplies strong length/class/history/expiry defaults, but the inspected passwordPolicy still does not enforce this exact eight-character-change or24-hour minimum-age requirement for password users. These are IdP policy/tailoring decisions, not missing API features. Certificate-only/delegated account paths can justify rule-specific exceptions if they are the selected path. This is a known provider policy gap/tailoring decision, not missing proof of an existing Core flag or a reason to duplicate identity in Horizon.\n\nNext action: Gallatin resolves the exact eight-character difference or24-hour minimum-change requirement for permitted human-password accounts: use an approved central provider policy extension or accepted certificate-only/account-type tailoring. Core supplies no matching defaultflag; native passwordAge is password-reuse age, not a minimum changeinterval. Do not weaken existing password constraints or invent a supported Helm switch."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233098",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233098",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000174",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233098",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000174",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233098r1268077_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233098r1268077",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000174-CTR-000450",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must enforce a 180-day maximum password lifetime restriction.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to enforce a 180-day maximum password lifetime restriction.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to determine if it enforces a 180-day maximum password lifetime restriction. \n\nIf the container platform does not enforce a 180-day maximum password lifetime restriction, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Any password, no matter how complex, can eventually be cracked; therefore, passwords need to be changed at specific intervals. \n\nOne method of minimizing this risk is to use complex passwords and periodically change them. If the application does not limit the lifetime of passwords and force users to change their passwords, there is the risk that the system and/or application passwords could be compromised. \n\nThis requirement does not include emergency administration accounts that are meant for access to the application in case of failure. These accounts are not required to have maximum password lifetime restrictions.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-004066"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "d156a67d-fb37-5ba5-a6e1-292fd806f471",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS identity-config0.32.0 defines three-failure lockout, permanent lockout, 15-character password policy with required classes and 60-day expiry, and 600-second session idle. These are concrete platform defaults; Horizon bundled realm remains separately assessed.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-id-realm] core-id-realm\nUDS realm defines three-failure lockout, permanentLockout=true, 600-second idle, PBKDF2-SHA256 password policy with 15 characters and classes/history/expiry; JSON event listener is enabled. Some controls remain optional.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[core-keycloak] core-keycloak\nCore uses identity-config 0.32.0, supports X509 and OTP by default; inactivity auto-disable is opt-in, and concurrent-session limit defaults to unlimited (0). This is platform SSO, not Horizon bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS identity-config0.32.0 defines three-failure lockout, permanent lockout, 15-character password policy with required classes and 60-day expiry, and 600-second session idle. These are concrete platform defaults; Horizon bundled realm remains separately assessed.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233101",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233101",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000177",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233101",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000177",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233101r1268093_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233101r1268093",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000177-CTR-000465",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must map the authenticated identity to the individual user or group account for PKI-based authentication.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to utilize the DoW Enterprise PKI infrastructure.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review documentation and configuration to ensure the container platform provides a PKI integration capability that meets DoW PKI infrastructure requirements. \n\nIf the container platform is not configured to meet this requirement, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The container platform and its components may require authentication before use. When the authentication is PKI-based, the container platform or component must map the certificate to a user account. If the certificate is not mapped to a user account, the ability to determine the identity of the individual user or group will not be available for forensic analysis.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000187"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "16b37f0a-bfa4-5c61-8148-13c40032d99d",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS provides centralized Keycloak identity, OIDC/OTP/X509 flows and individual user identities; Grafana rejects unmatched groups and disables its local login. Native cluster/API administration remains MPN-owned and must retain its inheritance mapping.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-keycloak] core-keycloak\nCore uses identity-config 0.32.0, supports X509 and OTP by default; inactivity auto-disable is opt-in, and concurrent-session limit defaults to unlimited (0). This is platform SSO, not Horizon bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[core-id-realm] core-id-realm\nUDS realm defines three-failure lockout, permanentLockout=true, 600-second idle, PBKDF2-SHA256 password policy with 15 characters and classes/history/expiry; JSON event listener is enabled. Some controls remain optional.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[core-grafana] core-grafana\nGrafana disables local login, uses UDS OAuth, maps Admin group to Admin and Auditor to Viewer, and rejects unmatched groups. OAuth secrets are file-mounted.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS provides centralized Keycloak identity, OIDC/OTP/X509 flows and individual user identities; Grafana rejects unmatched groups and disables its local login. Native cluster/API administration remains MPN-owned and must retain its inheritance mapping.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233102",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233102",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000178",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
