          "group_id": "V-233102",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000178",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233102r961047_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233102r961047",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000178-CTR-000470",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must obscure feedback of authentication information during the authentication process to protect the information from possible exploitation/use by unauthorized individuals.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to obscure feedback of authentication information during the authentication process to protect the information from possible exploitation/use by unauthorized individuals.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review container platform documentation and configuration to determine if any interfaces that are provided for authentication purposes display the user's password when it is typed into the data entry field. \n\nIf authentication information is not obfuscated when entered, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "To prevent the compromise of authentication information such as passwords during the authentication process, the feedback from the container platform and its components, e.g., runtime, registry, and keystore, must not provide any information that would allow an unauthorized user to compromise the authentication mechanism.\n\nObfuscation of user-provided information when typed is a method used in addressing this risk.\n\nDisplaying asterisks when a user types in a password is an example of obscuring feedback of authentication information.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000206"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "7eed4f70-4903-5ed6-b68c-54a18a4e28dc",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS provides centralized Keycloak identity, OIDC/OTP/X509 flows and individual user identities; Grafana rejects unmatched groups and disables its local login. Native cluster/API administration remains MPN-owned and must retain its inheritance mapping.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-keycloak] core-keycloak\nCore uses identity-config 0.32.0, supports X509 and OTP by default; inactivity auto-disable is opt-in, and concurrent-session limit defaults to unlimited (0). This is platform SSO, not Horizon bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[core-id-realm] core-id-realm\nUDS realm defines three-failure lockout, permanentLockout=true, 600-second idle, PBKDF2-SHA256 password policy with 15 characters and classes/history/expiry; JSON event listener is enabled. Some controls remain optional.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[core-grafana] core-grafana\nGrafana disables local login, uses UDS OAuth, maps Admin group to Admin and Auditor to Viewer, and rejects unmatched groups. OAuth secrets are file-mounted.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS provides centralized Keycloak identity, OIDC/OTP/X509 flows and individual user identities; Grafana rejects unmatched groups and disables its local login. Native cluster/API administration remains MPN-owned and must retain its inheritance mapping.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233105",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233105",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000181",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233105",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000181",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233105r961056_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233105r961056",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000181-CTR-000485",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must provide an audit reduction capability that supports on-demand reporting requirements.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to support on-demand reporting requirements.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to determine if the container platform is configured to provide an audit reduction capability that supports on-demand reporting requirements. \n\nIf the container platform is not configured to support on-demand reporting requirements, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The ability to generate on-demand reports, including after the audit data has been subjected to audit reduction, greatly facilitates the organization's ability to generate incident reports as needed to better handle larger-scale or more complex security incidents. \n\nAudit reduction is a process that manipulates collected audit information and organizes such information in a summary format that is more meaningful to analysts. The report generation capability provided by the application must support on-demand (i.e., customizable, ad hoc, and as-needed) reports.\n\nThis requirement is specific to applications with audit reduction capabilities; however, applications need to support on-demand audit review and analysis.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001876"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "f2c3dbb0-7647-50ef-8b87-daad5787a799",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Vector collects pod/node records with namespace/container/host labels and forwards them to Loki; Grafana supplies centralized search/reduction. UDS identity listener timestamps events in UTC with subsecond precision, and container clocks are host-derived.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[core-id-audit] core-id-audit\nUDS event listener prints user/admin events to stdout with event fields and UTC timestamps. Vector can collect these without a separately configured app forwarding agent.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/plugin/src/main/java/com/defenseunicorns/uds/keycloak/plugin/eventListeners/JSONLogEventListenerProvider.java\n\n[core-grafana] core-grafana\nGrafana disables local login, uses UDS OAuth, maps Admin group to Admin and Auditor to Viewer, and rejects unmatched groups. OAuth secrets are file-mounted.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Vector collects pod/node records with namespace/container/host labels and forwards them to Loki; Grafana supplies centralized search/reduction. UDS identity listener timestamps events in UTC with subsecond precision, and container clocks are host-derived.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233106",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233106",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000185",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233106",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000185",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233106r961062_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233106r961062",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000185-CTR-000490",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must employ strong authenticators in the establishment of non-local maintenance and diagnostic sessions.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to employ strong authenticators in the establishment of non-local maintenance and diagnostic sessions.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to determine if the container platform is configured to employ strong authenticators in the establishment of non-local maintenance and diagnostic sessions. \n\nIf the container platform is not configured to employ strong authenticators in the establishment of non-local maintenance and diagnostic sessions, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "If maintenance tools are used by unauthorized personnel, they may accidentally or intentionally damage or compromise the system. The act of managing systems and applications includes the ability to access sensitive application information, such as, system configuration details, diagnostic information, user information, and potentially sensitive application data.\n\nNon-local maintenance and diagnostic activities are those activities conducted by individuals communicating through a network, either an external network (e.g., the internet) or an internal network. Local maintenance and diagnostic activities are those activities carried out by individuals physically present at the information system or information system component and not communicating across a network connection. Typically, strong authentication requires authenticators that are resistant to replay attacks and employ multifactor authentication. Strong authenticators include, for example, PKI where certificates are stored on a token protected by a password, passphrase, or biometric.\n\nThis requirement applies to hardware/software diagnostic test equipment or tools. This requirement does not cover hardware/software components that may support information system maintenance, yet are a part of the system (e.g., the software implementing \"ping,\" \"ls,\" \"ipconfig,\" or the hardware and software implementing the monitoring port of an Ethernet switch).",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000877"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "7df544fa-b921-5382-9fae-d758168aec76",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS provides centralized Keycloak identity, OIDC/OTP/X509 flows and individual user identities; Grafana rejects unmatched groups and disables its local login. Native cluster/API administration remains MPN-owned and must retain its inheritance mapping.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-keycloak] core-keycloak\nCore uses identity-config 0.32.0, supports X509 and OTP by default; inactivity auto-disable is opt-in, and concurrent-session limit defaults to unlimited (0). This is platform SSO, not Horizon bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[core-id-realm] core-id-realm\nUDS realm defines three-failure lockout, permanentLockout=true, 600-second idle, PBKDF2-SHA256 password policy with 15 characters and classes/history/expiry; JSON event listener is enabled. Some controls remain optional.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[core-grafana] core-grafana\nGrafana disables local login, uses UDS OAuth, maps Admin group to Admin and Auditor to Viewer, and rejects unmatched groups. OAuth secrets are file-mounted.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS provides centralized Keycloak identity, OIDC/OTP/X509 flows and individual user identities; Grafana rejects unmatched groups and disables its local login. Native cluster/API administration remains MPN-owned and must retain its inheritance mapping.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233108",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233108",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000190",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233108",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000190",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233108r961068_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233108r961068",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000190-CTR-000500",
          "rule_title": "The application must terminate all network connections associated with a communications session at the end of the session, or as follows: for in-band management sessions (privileged sessions), the session must be terminated after 10 minutes of inactivity.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to terminate user sessions on defined conditions or trigger events.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review documentation and configuration settings to determine if the container platform is configured to close user sessions after defined conditions or trigger events are met.\n\nIf the container platform is not configured or cannot be configured to disconnect users after defined conditions and trigger events are met, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Terminating an idle session within a short time period reduces the window of opportunity for unauthorized personnel to take control of a management session enabled on the console or console port that has been left unattended. In addition, quickly terminating an idle session will also free up resources committed by the managed network element. \n\nTerminating network connections associated with communications sessions includes, for example, de-allocating associated TCP/IP address/port pairs at the operating system level, or de-allocating networking assignments at the application level if multiple application sessions are using a single, operating system level network connection. This does not mean that the application terminates all sessions or network access; it only ends the inactive session and releases the resources associated with that session.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001133"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "d7c65053-4fc1-5c47-9d71-673cbc4e2930",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS identity-config0.32.0 defines three-failure lockout, permanent lockout, 15-character password policy with required classes and 60-day expiry, and 600-second session idle. These are concrete platform defaults; Horizon bundled realm remains separately assessed.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-id-realm] core-id-realm\nUDS realm defines three-failure lockout, permanentLockout=true, 600-second idle, PBKDF2-SHA256 password policy with 15 characters and classes/history/expiry; JSON event listener is enabled. Some controls remain optional.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[core-keycloak] core-keycloak\nCore uses identity-config 0.32.0, supports X509 and OTP by default; inactivity auto-disable is opt-in, and concurrent-session limit defaults to unlimited (0). This is platform SSO, not Horizon bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS identity-config0.32.0 defines three-failure lockout, permanent lockout, 15-character password policy with required classes and 60-day expiry, and 600-second session idle. These are concrete platform defaults; Horizon bundled realm remains separately assessed.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233114",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233114",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000211",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233114",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000211",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233114r1137643_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233114r1137643",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000211-CTR-000530",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must separate user functionality (including user interface services) from information system management functionality.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform and its components to separate management and user functionality.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to determine if management functionality is separated from user functionality. \n\nValidate that the separation is also implemented within the components by trying to execute management functions for each component as a user. \n\nIf the container platform is not configured to separate management and user functionality or if component management and user functionality are not separated, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Separating user functionality from management functionality is a requirement for all the components within the container platform. Without the separation, users may have access to management functions that can degrade the container platform and the services being offered and can offer a method to bypass testing and validation of functions before introduced into a production environment.\n\nThe separation should be enforced by each component within the container platform.\n\nThis requirement also applies to Zero Trust initiatives.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001082"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "e77de05b-b199-5857-9c30-6e01e1200183",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS enforces namespace default-deny and service identity rules plus non-root/no-escalation/drop-capabilities admission; management UIs are separate and SSO-group restricted. Kubernetes RBAC/registry/storage administration remains in the MPN/UDS inherited boundary.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-policy] core-policy\nPepr mutates/validates non-root execution, no privilege escalation and dropped capabilities, including init and ephemeral containers; exemptions are explicit.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts\n\n[core-network] core-network\nOperator generates namespace ingress/egress default-deny NetworkPolicy; connectivity requires declared allow rules.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/operator/controllers/network/defaults/default-deny-all.ts\n\n[core-grafana] core-grafana\nGrafana disables local login, uses UDS OAuth, maps Admin group to Admin and Auditor to Viewer, and rejects unmatched groups. OAuth secrets are file-mounted.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS enforces namespace default-deny and service identity rules plus non-root/no-escalation/drop-capabilities admission; management UIs are separate and SSO-group restricted. Kubernetes RBAC/registry/storage administration remains in the MPN/UDS inherited boundary.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233118",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233118",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000219",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233118",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000219",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233118r1043178_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233118r1043178",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000219-CTR-000550",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must protect authenticity of communications sessions with the use of FIPS-validated 140-2 or 140-3 security requirements for cryptographic modules.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to use FIPS-validated 140-2 or 140-3 cryptographic modules to protect container images during transmission.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to determine if FIPS-validated 140-2 or 140-3 cryptographic modules are being used to protect container images during transmission. \n\nIf FIPS-validated 140-2 or 140-3 cryptographic modules are not being use, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The container platform is responsible for pulling images from trusted sources and placing those images into its registry. To protect the transmission of images, the container platform must use FIPS-validated 140-2 or 140-3 cryptographic modules. This added protection defends against main-in-the-middle attacks where malicious code could be added to an image during transmission.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001184"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "daf9e100-734a-5484-93ba-eb3dd69ec070",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS's supported unicorn flavor selects curated FIPS mesh/identity images, and Core Keycloak explicitly starts in strict FIPS mode. This supplies a concrete offered platform implementation for the prescribed cryptography. Recommendation is conditional on selecting that hardened flavor; upstream/demo flavor does not inherit it.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Bind the MPN Core release/flavor/digests to vendor module-validation evidence and approved mode; do not apply this ruling to an upstream/demo Core deployment.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-flavors] core-flavors\nCore distinguishes unicorn (curated FIPS-validated images), registry1 (Iron Bank hardening) and upstream (development/community images). The production hardened offering exists; actual selected flavor/digests and module mapping remain explicit deployment inputs.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/concepts/platform/flavors.mdx\n\n[core-hardened-mesh] core-hardened-mesh\nUnicorn flavor selects curated istio-pilot-fips and istio-proxy-fips images; ambient ztunnel has its own FIPS image override. This proves the offered implementation, not the flavor of a deployed MPN cluster.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/values/unicorn/istiod.yaml\n\n[core-strict-keycloak] core-strict-keycloak\nCore Keycloak starts with --features=fips and --fips-mode=strict; cgr image handling adds the FIPS JCE security setup. Bundled Horizon Keycloak is a different chart and must not inherit these flags by name alone.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/statefulset.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS's supported unicorn flavor selects curated FIPS mesh/identity images, and Core Keycloak explicitly starts in strict FIPS mode. This supplies a concrete offered platform implementation for the prescribed cryptography. Recommendation is conditional on selecting that hardened flavor; upstream/demo flavor does not inherit it.\n\nNext action: Bind the MPN Core release/flavor/digests to vendor module-validation evidence and approved mode; do not apply this ruling to an upstream/demo Core deployment."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233122",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233122",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000225",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233122",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000225",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233122r961122_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233122r961122",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000225-CTR-000570",
          "rule_title": "The container platform runtime must fail to a secure state if system initialization fails, shutdown fails, or aborts fail.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform runtime to fail to a secure state if system initialization fails, shutdown fails, or aborts fail.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review documentation and configuration to determine if the container platform runtime fails to a secure state if system initialization fails, shutdown fails, or aborts fail. \n\nIf the container platform runtime cannot be configured to fail securely, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The container platform offers services for container image orchestration and services for users. If any of these services were to fail into an insecure state, security measures for user and data separation and image instantiation could become absent. In addition, audit log protections could be relaxed allowing for investigation of what occurred could be lost. To protect services and data, it is important for the container platform to fail to a secure state if the container platform registry initialization fails, shutdown fails, or aborts fail.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001190"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "3ce04ebd-86d2-534b-81cd-3fe3e890f51c",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Admission rejects unsafe resources, mesh/security components have explicit startup/readiness controls, and Vector persists acknowledged log buffers outside the application process. These mechanisms support fail-closed/recovery diagnostics; final failure exercises remain confirmation work.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-policy] core-policy\nPepr mutates/validates non-root execution, no privilege escalation and dropped capabilities, including init and ephemeral containers; exemptions are explicit.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts\n\n[core-accesslog] core-accesslog\nIstio access logs go to stdout; path normalization merges slashes, proxies are held until ready, and forwarded certificate details are sanitized.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/values/base-istiod.yaml\n\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Admission rejects unsafe resources, mesh/security components have explicit startup/readiness controls, and Vector persists acknowledged log buffers outside the application process. These mechanisms support fail-closed/recovery diagnostics; final failure exercises remain confirmation work.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233123",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233123",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000226",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233123",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000226",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233123r961125_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233123r961125",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000226-CTR-000575",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must preserve any information necessary to determine the cause of the disruption or failure.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to preserve information necessary to determine the cause of the disruption or failure.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to determine if information necessary to determine the cause of a disruption or failure is preserved. \n\nIf the information is not preserved, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "When a failure occurs within the container platform, preserving the state of the container platform and its components, along with other container services, helps to facilitate container platform restart and return to the operational mode of the organization with less disruption to mission essential processes. When preserving state, considerations for preservation of data confidentiality and integrity must be taken into consideration.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
