          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "37d49742-16e4-5139-b5f3-e640fbb91504",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS enforces namespace default-deny and service identity rules plus non-root/no-escalation/drop-capabilities admission; management UIs are separate and SSO-group restricted. Kubernetes RBAC/registry/storage administration remains in the MPN/UDS inherited boundary.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-policy] core-policy\nPepr mutates/validates non-root execution, no privilege escalation and dropped capabilities, including init and ephemeral containers; exemptions are explicit.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts\n\n[core-network] core-network\nOperator generates namespace ingress/egress default-deny NetworkPolicy; connectivity requires declared allow rules.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/operator/controllers/network/defaults/default-deny-all.ts\n\n[core-grafana] core-grafana\nGrafana disables local login, uses UDS OAuth, maps Admin group to Admin and Auditor to Viewer, and rejects unmatched groups. OAuth secrets are file-mounted.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS enforces namespace default-deny and service identity rules plus non-root/no-escalation/drop-capabilities admission; management UIs are separate and SSO-group restricted. Kubernetes RBAC/registry/storage administration remains in the MPN/UDS inherited boundary.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233126",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233126",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000234",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233126",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000234",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233126r971528_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233126r971528",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000234-CTR-000590",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must never automatically remove or disable emergency accounts.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to never remove or disable emergency accounts.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform to determine if emergency accounts are automatically removed or disabled. \n\nIf emergency accounts are automatically removed or disabled, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Emergency accounts are administrator accounts that are established in response to crisis situations where the need for rapid account activation is required. Therefore, emergency account activation may bypass normal account authorization processes. If these accounts are automatically disabled, system maintenance during emergencies may not be possible, thus adversely affecting system availability.\n\nEmergency accounts are different from infrequently used accounts (i.e., local logon accounts used by system administrators when network or normal logon/access is not available). Infrequently used accounts also remain available and are not subject to automatic termination dates. However, an emergency account is normally a different account that is created for use by vendors or system maintainers.\n\nTo address access requirements, many application developers choose to integrate their applications with enterprise-level authentication/access mechanisms that meet or exceed access control policy requirements. Such integration allows the application developer to off-load those access control functions and focus on core application features and functionality.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001682"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "da2df7e9-810f-579b-9bcc-cca330dd66a4",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS inactivity disable is off by default; its optional workflow excludes admin accounts. An explicitly designated emergency-account policy still belongs to the platform owner, but source does not show automatic emergency-account deletion.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-keycloak] core-keycloak\nCore uses identity-config 0.32.0, supports X509 and OTP by default; inactivity auto-disable is opt-in, and concurrent-session limit defaults to unlimited (0). This is platform SSO, not Horizon bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS inactivity disable is off by default; its optional workflow excludes admin accounts. An explicitly designated emergency-account policy still belongs to the platform owner, but source does not show automatic emergency-account deletion.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233127",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233127",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000243",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233127",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000243",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233127r1137644_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233127r1137644",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000243-CTR-000595",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must prohibit containers from accessing privileged resources.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to block instantiation of containers requesting access to host system-privileged resources.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review documentation and configuration to determine if the container platform disallows instantiation of containers trying to access host system privileged resources. \n\nIf the container platform does not block containers requesting host system privileged resources, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Containers images instantiated within the container platform may request access to host system resources. Access to privileged resources can allow for unauthorized and unintended transfer of information, but in some cases, these resources may be needed for the service being offered by the container. By default, containers should be denied instantiation when privileged system resources are requested and granted only after approval has been given.\n\nWhen access to privileged resources is necessary for a container, a new policy for execution should be written for the container. The default behavior must not give containers privileged access to host system resources.\n\nExamples of system resources that should be protected are kernel namespaces and host system sensitive directories such as /etc and /usr.\n\nThis requirement also applies to Zero Trust initiatives.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001090"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "2630e892-bbaa-55ac-af50-5e552f9f2aff",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS enforces namespace default-deny and service identity rules plus non-root/no-escalation/drop-capabilities admission; management UIs are separate and SSO-group restricted. Kubernetes RBAC/registry/storage administration remains in the MPN/UDS inherited boundary.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-policy] core-policy\nPepr mutates/validates non-root execution, no privilege escalation and dropped capabilities, including init and ephemeral containers; exemptions are explicit.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts\n\n[core-network] core-network\nOperator generates namespace ingress/egress default-deny NetworkPolicy; connectivity requires declared allow rules.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/operator/controllers/network/defaults/default-deny-all.ts\n\n[core-grafana] core-grafana\nGrafana disables local login, uses UDS OAuth, maps Admin group to Admin and Auditor to Viewer, and rejects unmatched groups. OAuth secrets are file-mounted.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS enforces namespace default-deny and service identity rules plus non-root/no-escalation/drop-capabilities admission; management UIs are separate and SSO-group restricted. Kubernetes RBAC/registry/storage administration remains in the MPN/UDS inherited boundary.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
