          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001312"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "e7271b9c-9c64-50c4-8c8c-d1f011be9113",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Admission rejects unsafe resources, mesh/security components have explicit startup/readiness controls, and Vector persists acknowledged log buffers outside the application process. These mechanisms support fail-closed/recovery diagnostics; final failure exercises remain confirmation work.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-policy] core-policy\nPepr mutates/validates non-root execution, no privilege escalation and dropped capabilities, including init and ephemeral containers; exemptions are explicit.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts\n\n[core-accesslog] core-accesslog\nIstio access logs go to stdout; path normalization merges slashes, proxies are held until ready, and forwarded certificate details are sanitized.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/values/base-istiod.yaml\n\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Admission rejects unsafe resources, mesh/security components have explicit startup/readiness controls, and Vector persists acknowledged log buffers outside the application process. These mechanisms support fail-closed/recovery diagnostics; final failure exercises remain confirmation work.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233142",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233142",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000290",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233142",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000290",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233142r961206_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233142r961206",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000290-CTR-000670",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must use cryptographic mechanisms to protect the integrity of audit tools.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to use cryptographic mechanisms to protect the integrity of audit tools.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to determine if the integrity of the audit tools is protected using cryptographic mechanisms. \n\nIf audit tools are not protected through cryptographic mechanisms, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Protecting the integrity of the tools used for auditing purposes is a critical step to ensuring the integrity of audit data. Audit data includes all information (e.g., audit records, audit settings, and audit reports) needed to successfully audit information system activity.\n\nAudit tools include, but are not limited to, vendor provided and open source audit tools needed to successfully view and manipulate audit information system activity and records. Audit tools include custom queries and report generators.\n\nIt is common for attackers to replace the audit tools or inject code into the existing tools with the purpose of providing the capability to hide or erase system activity from the audit logs.\n\nTo address this risk, audit tools must be cryptographically signed in order to provide the capability to identify when the audit tools have been modified, manipulated, or replaced. An example is a checksum hash of the file or files.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001496"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "733f29d9-2fb4-5f56-8d2b-b738e6e80cc5",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS's supported unicorn flavor selects curated FIPS mesh/identity images, and Core Keycloak explicitly starts in strict FIPS mode. This supplies a concrete offered platform implementation for the prescribed cryptography. Recommendation is conditional on selecting that hardened flavor; upstream/demo flavor does not inherit it.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Bind the MPN Core release/flavor/digests to vendor module-validation evidence and approved mode; do not apply this ruling to an upstream/demo Core deployment.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-flavors] core-flavors\nCore distinguishes unicorn (curated FIPS-validated images), registry1 (Iron Bank hardening) and upstream (development/community images). The production hardened offering exists; actual selected flavor/digests and module mapping remain explicit deployment inputs.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/concepts/platform/flavors.mdx\n\n[core-hardened-mesh] core-hardened-mesh\nUnicorn flavor selects curated istio-pilot-fips and istio-proxy-fips images; ambient ztunnel has its own FIPS image override. This proves the offered implementation, not the flavor of a deployed MPN cluster.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/values/unicorn/istiod.yaml\n\n[core-strict-keycloak] core-strict-keycloak\nCore Keycloak starts with --features=fips and --fips-mode=strict; cgr image handling adds the FIPS JCE security setup. Bundled Horizon Keycloak is a different chart and must not inherit these flags by name alone.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/statefulset.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS's supported unicorn flavor selects curated FIPS mesh/identity images, and Core Keycloak explicitly starts in strict FIPS mode. This supplies a concrete offered platform implementation for the prescribed cryptography. Recommendation is conditional on selecting that hardened flavor; upstream/demo flavor does not inherit it.\n\nNext action: Bind the MPN Core release/flavor/digests to vendor module-validation evidence and approved mode; do not apply this ruling to an upstream/demo Core deployment."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233143",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233143",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000291",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233143",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000291",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233143r981871_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233143r981871",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000291-CTR-000675",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must notify system administrators (SAs) and the information system security officer (ISSO) when accounts are created.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to notify SAs and ISSO when accounts are created.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to determine if system administrators and ISSO are notified when accounts are created. \n\nIf SAs and ISSO are not notified, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Once an attacker establishes access to an application, the attacker often attempts to create a persistent method of re-establishing access. One way to accomplish this is for the attacker to simply create a new account. Sending notification of account creation events to the SA and ISSO is one method for mitigating this risk.\n\nTo address access requirements, many application developers choose to integrate their applications with enterprise-level authentication/access/auditing mechanisms that meet or exceed access control policy requirements. Such integration allows the application developer to offload those access control functions and focus on core application features and functionality.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000015"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "c01c1142-7b43-5fc3-a062-eb5081eef1cd",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS identity emits the required account events into the central logging system. Core also supplies account/admin alert templates, but detailedObservability.alerts.enabled is false by default and no SA/ISSO receiver is selected by the application artifact. Only platform notification enablement/routing remains. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply this decision to SA/ISSO account-change notification requirements. Source logs and optional alert templates do not establish notifications to the required recipients.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Enable centralized account-creation notifications for the assessed component and configure named SA and ISSO recipients. Confirm that alert rules cover the relevant identity event; add or adjust a rule where the existing templates do not. Create a test account and verify notification reaches both recipients. Retain routing configuration and delivery-test evidence.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-platform-delivered-profile] re-platform-delivered-profile\nSuccessful UDS publication2026.45.4-uds.1 records package d528, app b84b9af, Core1.14.0, identity.mode=uds issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds and12 digest-pinned images; no bundled Keycloak image. This is delivered artifact evidence, not assumed default profile.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-platform-id-audit] re-platform-id-audit\nJSON listener emits platform user/admin events to stdout for Vector/Loki collection. UDS identity selected by delivered artifact makes this applicable to Horizon rather than requiring its own duplicated forwarding.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/plugin/src/main/java/com/defenseunicorns/uds/keycloak/plugin/eventListeners/JSONLogEventListenerProvider.java\n\n[re-platform-notify] re-platform-notify\nAccount/admin modification alert PrometheusRules are emitted only if detailedObservability.alerts.enabled; source includes alerts but requires enablement.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/keycloak-logging-alerting-rules.yaml\n\n[re-platform-realm-params] re-platform-realm-params\nCore exposes realmInitEnv overrides for ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS, TERMS_AND_CONDITIONS_ENABLED and X509_CRL_CHECKING_ENABLED; these are commented/unset in shipped defaults. detailedObservability.alerts.enabled defaults false.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[re-platform-monitor] re-platform-monitor\nDefault Kubernetes storage/system alerts enabled; alertmanager exists but no SA/ISSO receiver routes or exact75percent audit repository rule are set in this file.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/prometheus-stack/values/kube-prometheus-stack-values.yaml\n\n[mpn-core-known-identity-settings] mpn-core-known-identity-settings\nKnown Core settings: realmInitEnv.TERMS_AND_CONDITIONS_ENABLED and themeCustomizations.termsAndConditions.text.inline/configmap; realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER (default0 unlimited); realmInitEnv.X509_CRL_CHECKING_ENABLED/RELATIVE_PATH/ABORT_IF_NON_UPDATED; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled(defaultfalse), logging.enabled/defaulttrue. These are Gallatin configuration choices.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-core-account-alerts] mpn-core-account-alerts\nCore account/admin modification PrometheusRules are gated only on detailedObservability.alerts.enabled. Gallatin can enable them and configure recipients in its own Alertmanager deployment; vendor-provided proof is unnecessary.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/keycloak-logging-alerting-rules.yaml",
          "finding_details": "UDS identity emits the required account events into the central logging system. Core also supplies account/admin alert templates, but detailedObservability.alerts.enabled is false by default and no SA/ISSO receiver is selected by the application artifact. Only platform notification enablement/routing remains. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply this decision to SA/ISSO account-change notification requirements. Source logs and optional alert templates do not establish notifications to the required recipients.\n\nNext action: Enable centralized account-creation notifications for the assessed component and configure named SA and ISSO recipients. Confirm that alert rules cover the relevant identity event; add or adjust a rule where the existing templates do not. Create a test account and verify notification reaches both recipients. Retain routing configuration and delivery-test evidence."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233144",
          "group_tree": [
