          "rule_title": "The container platform must notify the system administrator (SA) and information system security officer (ISSO) of account enabling actions.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to notify the SA and ISSO of account enabling actions.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Determine if the container platform is configured to notify system administrator and ISSO of account enabling actions.\n\nIf the container platform is not configured to notify the SA and ISSO of account enabling actions, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Once an attacker establishes access to an application, the attacker often attempts to create a persistent method of re-establishing access. One way to accomplish this is for the attacker to simply enable a new or disabled account. Sending notification of account enabling events to the system administrator and ISSO is one method for mitigating this risk. Such a capability greatly reduces the risk that application accessibility will be negatively affected for extended periods of time and provides logging that can be used for forensic purposes.\n\nTo detect and respond to events that affect user accessibility and application processing, applications must notify the appropriate individuals so they can investigate the event.\n\nTo address access requirements, many application developers choose to integrate their applications with enterprise-level authentication/access/auditing mechanisms that meet or exceed access control policy requirements. Such integration allows the application developer to offload those access control functions and focus on core application features and functionality.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000015"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "5f4de914-822a-5835-868f-ad1bbfb49949",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS identity emits the required account events into the central logging system. Core also supplies account/admin alert templates, but detailedObservability.alerts.enabled is false by default and no SA/ISSO receiver is selected by the application artifact. Only platform notification enablement/routing remains. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply this decision to SA/ISSO account-change notification requirements. Source logs and optional alert templates do not establish notifications to the required recipients.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Enable centralized account-enabling notifications for the assessed component and configure named SA and ISSO recipients. Confirm that alert rules cover the relevant identity event; add or adjust a rule where the existing templates do not. Disable and then enable a test account and verify notification reaches both recipients. Retain routing configuration and delivery-test evidence.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-platform-delivered-profile] re-platform-delivered-profile\nSuccessful UDS publication2026.45.4-uds.1 records package d528, app b84b9af, Core1.14.0, identity.mode=uds issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds and12 digest-pinned images; no bundled Keycloak image. This is delivered artifact evidence, not assumed default profile.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-platform-id-audit] re-platform-id-audit\nJSON listener emits platform user/admin events to stdout for Vector/Loki collection. UDS identity selected by delivered artifact makes this applicable to Horizon rather than requiring its own duplicated forwarding.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/plugin/src/main/java/com/defenseunicorns/uds/keycloak/plugin/eventListeners/JSONLogEventListenerProvider.java\n\n[re-platform-notify] re-platform-notify\nAccount/admin modification alert PrometheusRules are emitted only if detailedObservability.alerts.enabled; source includes alerts but requires enablement.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/keycloak-logging-alerting-rules.yaml\n\n[re-platform-realm-params] re-platform-realm-params\nCore exposes realmInitEnv overrides for ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS, TERMS_AND_CONDITIONS_ENABLED and X509_CRL_CHECKING_ENABLED; these are commented/unset in shipped defaults. detailedObservability.alerts.enabled defaults false.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[re-platform-monitor] re-platform-monitor\nDefault Kubernetes storage/system alerts enabled; alertmanager exists but no SA/ISSO receiver routes or exact75percent audit repository rule are set in this file.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/prometheus-stack/values/kube-prometheus-stack-values.yaml\n\n[mpn-core-known-identity-settings] mpn-core-known-identity-settings\nKnown Core settings: realmInitEnv.TERMS_AND_CONDITIONS_ENABLED and themeCustomizations.termsAndConditions.text.inline/configmap; realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER (default0 unlimited); realmInitEnv.X509_CRL_CHECKING_ENABLED/RELATIVE_PATH/ABORT_IF_NON_UPDATED; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled(defaultfalse), logging.enabled/defaulttrue. These are Gallatin configuration choices.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-core-account-alerts] mpn-core-account-alerts\nCore account/admin modification PrometheusRules are gated only on detailedObservability.alerts.enabled. Gallatin can enable them and configure recipients in its own Alertmanager deployment; vendor-provided proof is unnecessary.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/keycloak-logging-alerting-rules.yaml",
          "finding_details": "UDS identity emits the required account events into the central logging system. Core also supplies account/admin alert templates, but detailedObservability.alerts.enabled is false by default and no SA/ISSO receiver is selected by the application artifact. Only platform notification enablement/routing remains. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply this decision to SA/ISSO account-change notification requirements. Source logs and optional alert templates do not establish notifications to the required recipients.\n\nNext action: Enable centralized account-enabling notifications for the assessed component and configure named SA and ISSO recipients. Confirm that alert rules cover the relevant identity event; add or adjust a rule where the existing templates do not. Disable and then enable a test account and verify notification reaches both recipients. Retain routing configuration and delivery-test evidence."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233162",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233162",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000340",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233162",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000340",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233162r961353_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233162r961353",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000340-CTR-000770",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must prevent non-privileged users from executing privileged functions to include disabling, circumventing, or altering implemented security safeguards/countermeasures.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to security to protect all privileged functionality. Assigning roles that limit what actions a particular user can perform are the most common means of meeting this requirement.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review documentation to obtain the definition of the container platform functionality considered privileged in the context of the information system in question. \n\nReview the container platform security configuration and/or other means used to protect privileged functionality from unauthorized use. \n\nIf the configuration does not protect all of the actions defined as privileged, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Controlling what users can perform privileged functions prevents unauthorized users from performing tasks that may expose data or degrade the container platform. When users are not segregated into privileged and non-privileged users, unauthorized individuals may perform tasks such as deploying containers, pulling images into the register, and modify keys in the keystore. These actions can introduce malicious containers and cause denial-of-service (DoS) attacks and undermine the container platform integrity. The enforcement may take place at the container platform and can be implemented within each container platform component (e.g. runtime, registry, and keystore).",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002235"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "7d7ba070-b280-5074-86e9-debb6c667e9e",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS enforces namespace default-deny and service identity rules plus non-root/no-escalation/drop-capabilities admission; management UIs are separate and SSO-group restricted. Kubernetes RBAC/registry/storage administration remains in the MPN/UDS inherited boundary.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-policy] core-policy\nPepr mutates/validates non-root execution, no privilege escalation and dropped capabilities, including init and ephemeral containers; exemptions are explicit.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts\n\n[core-network] core-network\nOperator generates namespace ingress/egress default-deny NetworkPolicy; connectivity requires declared allow rules.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/operator/controllers/network/defaults/default-deny-all.ts\n\n[core-grafana] core-grafana\nGrafana disables local login, uses UDS OAuth, maps Admin group to Admin and Auditor to Viewer, and rejects unmatched groups. OAuth secrets are file-mounted.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS enforces namespace default-deny and service identity rules plus non-root/no-escalation/drop-capabilities admission; management UIs are separate and SSO-group restricted. Kubernetes RBAC/registry/storage administration remains in the MPN/UDS inherited boundary.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233163",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233163",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000342",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233163",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000342",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233163r961359_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233163r961359",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000342-CTR-000775",
          "rule_title": "Container images instantiated by the container platform must execute using least privileges.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to block instantiation with no more privileges than necessary.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review documentation and configuration to determine if the container platform disallows instantiation of containers trying to execute with more privileges than required or with privileged permissions. \n\nIf the container platform does not block containers requesting privileged permissions, privilege escalation, or allows containers to have more privileges than required, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Containers running within the container platform must execute as non-privileged. When a container can execute as a privileged container, the privileged container is also a privileged user within the hosting system, and the hosting system becomes a major security risk. It is important for the container platform runtime to validate the container user and disallow instantiation if the container is trying to execute with more privileges than required, as a privileged user, or is trying to perform a privilege escalation.\n\nWhen privileged access is necessary for a container, a new policy for execution should be written for the container. The default behavior must not give containers privileged execution.\n\nExamples of privileged users are root, admin, and default service accounts for the container platform.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002233"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "3aba9fbf-6a7e-5ad7-b3a9-ee86319f9592",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS enforces namespace default-deny and service identity rules plus non-root/no-escalation/drop-capabilities admission; management UIs are separate and SSO-group restricted. Kubernetes RBAC/registry/storage administration remains in the MPN/UDS inherited boundary.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-policy] core-policy\nPepr mutates/validates non-root execution, no privilege escalation and dropped capabilities, including init and ephemeral containers; exemptions are explicit.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts\n\n[core-network] core-network\nOperator generates namespace ingress/egress default-deny NetworkPolicy; connectivity requires declared allow rules.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/operator/controllers/network/defaults/default-deny-all.ts\n\n[core-grafana] core-grafana\nGrafana disables local login, uses UDS OAuth, maps Admin group to Admin and Auditor to Viewer, and rejects unmatched groups. OAuth secrets are file-mounted.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS enforces namespace default-deny and service identity rules plus non-root/no-escalation/drop-capabilities admission; management UIs are separate and SSO-group restricted. Kubernetes RBAC/registry/storage administration remains in the MPN/UDS inherited boundary.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233164",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233164",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000343",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233164",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000343",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233164r961362_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233164r961362",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000343-CTR-000780",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must audit the execution of privileged functions.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to log privileged activity.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review container platform documentation and log configuration to verify the application server logs privileged activity. \n\nIf the container platform is not configured to log privileged activity, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Privileged functions within the container platform can be component specific or can envelope the entire container platform. Because of the nature of the commands, it is important to understand what command was executed for either investigation of an incident or for debugging/error correction; therefore, privileged function execution must be audited.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002234"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "6d40358f-5127-5259-9f98-989362bd6ff3",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Native platform/maintenance activity has node logs, Kubernetes audit inheritance and Falco/identity event sources collected centrally. Full command recording and individual attribution are mechanisms supplied by the platform boundary, with MPN's exact audit policy a retained inheritance condition.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[core-falco] core-falco\nFalco loads shipped rule sets, detects runtime behavior and ships Sidekick events to Loki. Alertmanager delivery is commented out; detection is not automatic access revocation.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/falco/values/values.yaml\n\n[core-id-audit] core-id-audit\nUDS event listener prints user/admin events to stdout with event fields and UTC timestamps. Vector can collect these without a separately configured app forwarding agent.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/plugin/src/main/java/com/defenseunicorns/uds/keycloak/plugin/eventListeners/JSONLogEventListenerProvider.java\n\n[host] MPN ownership\nCurrent scope assigns control plane/node OS to MPN; no host assessment or live configuration was supplied.\nhttps://gallatin.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/GG/pages/733085804/WIP+STIG+SRG+Compliance+Scope+MPN",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Native platform/maintenance activity has node logs, Kubernetes audit inheritance and Falco/identity event sources collected centrally. Full command recording and individual attribution are mechanisms supplied by the platform boundary, with MPN's exact audit policy a retained inheritance condition.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233165",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233165",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000345",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233165",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000345",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233165r961368_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233165r961368",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000345-CTR-000785",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must automatically lock an account until the locked account is released by an administrator when three unsuccessful login attempts in 15 minutes are exceeded.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to automatically lock an account until the locked account is released by an administrator when three unsuccessful login attempts in 15 minutes are exceeded.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Determine if the container platform is configured to automatically lock an account until the locked account is released by an administrator when three unsuccessful login attempts in 15 minutes are exceeded. \n\nIf the container platform is not configured to lock the account, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "By limiting the number of failed login attempts, the risk of unauthorized system access via user password guessing, otherwise known as brute forcing, is reduced. Limits are imposed by locking the account.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002238"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "adab2dfe-4cee-583f-8972-eca611affa78",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS identity-config0.32.0 defines three-failure lockout, permanent lockout, 15-character password policy with required classes and 60-day expiry, and 600-second session idle. These are concrete platform defaults; Horizon bundled realm remains separately assessed.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-id-realm] core-id-realm\nUDS realm defines three-failure lockout, permanentLockout=true, 600-second idle, PBKDF2-SHA256 password policy with 15 characters and classes/history/expiry; JSON event listener is enabled. Some controls remain optional.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[core-keycloak] core-keycloak\nCore uses identity-config 0.32.0, supports X509 and OTP by default; inactivity auto-disable is opt-in, and concurrent-session limit defaults to unlimited (0). This is platform SSO, not Horizon bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS identity-config0.32.0 defines three-failure lockout, permanent lockout, 15-character password policy with required classes and 60-day expiry, and 600-second session idle. These are concrete platform defaults; Horizon bundled realm remains separately assessed.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233166",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233166",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000516",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233166",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000516",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233166r961863_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233166r961863",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000516-CTR-000790",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must provide the configuration for organization-identified individuals or roles to change the auditing to be performed on all components, based on all selectable event criteria within organization-defined time thresholds.",
          "fix_text": "Deploy a container platform that provides the ability for users in authorized roles to reconfigure auditing at any time. Deploy a container platform that allows audit configuration changes to take effect within the timeframe required by the organization and without involving actions or events that the organization rules unacceptable.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review documentation and configuration setting. \n\nIf the container platform does not provide the ability for users in authorized roles to reconfigure auditing at any time of the user's choosing, this is a finding. \n\nIf changes in audit configuration cannot take effect until after a certain time or date, or until some event, such as a server restart, has occurred, and if that time or event does not meet the requirements specified by the organization, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Auditing requirements may change per organization or situation within and organization. With the container platform allowing an organization to customize the auditing, an organization can decide to extend or limit auditing as necessary to meet organizational requirements. Auditing that is limited to conserve resources may be extended to address certain threat situations. In addition, auditing may be limited to a specific set of events to facilitate audit reduction, analysis, and reporting. Organizations can establish time thresholds in which audit actions are changed, for example, near real-time, within minutes, or within hours.\n\nModifying auditing within the container platform must be controlled to only those individuals or roles identified by the organization to modify auditable events.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000366"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "9dd3acf2-37e8-572e-8ea6-65fa071952be",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Audit selection is managed by privileged Kubernetes configuration and identity-admin policy, with configurable log/rule definitions; ordinary application users cannot edit the isolated logging stack through its declared service interface.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-loki] core-loki\nLoki uses ambient mesh; ingress is limited to Grafana, Vector, FalcoSidekick and monitoring service identities; object-store egress is declared separately.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/loki/chart/templates/uds-package.yaml\n\n[core-grafana] core-grafana\nGrafana disables local login, uses UDS OAuth, maps Admin group to Admin and Auditor to Viewer, and rejects unmatched groups. OAuth secrets are file-mounted.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml\n\n[core-policy] core-policy\nPepr mutates/validates non-root execution, no privilege escalation and dropped capabilities, including init and ephemeral containers; exemptions are explicit.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Audit selection is managed by privileged Kubernetes configuration and identity-admin policy, with configurable log/rule definitions; ordinary application users cannot edit the isolated logging stack through its declared service interface.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233168",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233168",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000357",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233168",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000357",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233168r961392_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233168r961392",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000357-CTR-000800",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must allocate audit record storage capacity in accordance with organization-defined audit record storage requirements.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to allocate audit record storage capacity in accordance with organization-defined audit record storage requirements.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to determine if audit record storage capacity is allocated in accordance with organization-defined audit record storage requirements. \n\nIf audit record storage capacity is not allocated in accordance with organization-defined audit record storage requirements, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "In order to ensure applications have a sufficient storage capacity in which to write the audit logs, applications need to be able to allocate audit record storage capacity.\n\nThe task of allocating audit record storage capacity is usually performed during initial installation of the application and is closely associated with the DBA and system administrator roles. The DBA or system administrator will usually coordinate the allocation of physical drive space with the application owner/installer and the application will prompt the installer to provide the capacity information, the physical location of the disk, or both.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001849"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "bf49446e-5047-5d3b-8eb7-450408403fc1",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Vector allocates acknowledged disk buffers per sink and Loki provides offloaded object storage; source establishes an explicit buffer/storage allocation. Final sizing against an organization-defined event volume remains a capacity acceptance input.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[core-loki] core-loki\nLoki uses ambient mesh; ingress is limited to Grafana, Vector, FalcoSidekick and monitoring service identities; object-store egress is declared separately.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/loki/chart/templates/uds-package.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Vector allocates acknowledged disk buffers per sink and Loki provides offloaded object storage; source establishes an explicit buffer/storage allocation. Final sizing against an organization-defined event volume remains a capacity acceptance input.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233169",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233169",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000358",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233169",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000358",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233169r961395_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233169r961395",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000358-CTR-000805",
          "rule_title": "Audit records must be stored at a secondary location.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to off-load the logs to a remote log or management server.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify the log records are being off-loaded to a separate system or transferred from the container platform storage location to a storage location other than the container platform itself. \n\nThe information system may demonstrate this capability using a log management application, system configuration, or other means. \n\nIf logs are not being off-loaded, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Auditable events are used in the investigation of incidents and must be protected from being deleted or altered. Often, events that took place in the past must be viewed to understand the entire incident. For the purposes of audit event protection and recall, audit events are often off-loaded to an external storage location. The container platform must provide a mechanism to assist in the off-loading of the audit data or at a minimum, must not hinder an external process used for audit event off-loading.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001851"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "17914773-3252-5d1e-9836-79be16d1bc0c",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Vector moves records from workload/node local files into a separate Loki service/object-store backend, satisfying a secondary storage mechanism; backend lifecycle/IAM must preserve required records.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[core-loki] core-loki\nLoki uses ambient mesh; ingress is limited to Grafana, Vector, FalcoSidekick and monitoring service identities; object-store egress is declared separately.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/loki/chart/templates/uds-package.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Vector moves records from workload/node local files into a separate Loki service/object-store backend, satisfying a secondary storage mechanism; backend lifecycle/IAM must preserve required records.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233170",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233170",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000359",
