            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233170",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000359",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233170r961398_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233170r961398",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000359-CTR-000810",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must provide an immediate warning to the SA and ISSO (at a minimum) when allocated audit record storage volume reaches 75 percent of repository maximum audit record storage capacity.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to provide an immediate real-time alert to the SA and ISSO when allocated audit record storage volume reaches 75 percent of repository maximum audit record storage capacity.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to determine if it is configured to provide an immediate warning to the SA and ISSO (at a minimum) when allocated audit record storage volume reaches 75 percent of repository maximum audit record storage capacity. \n\nIf the container platform is not configured to provide an immediate real-time alert, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "If security personnel are not notified immediately upon storage volume utilization reaching 75 percent, they are unable to plan for storage capacity expansion.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001855"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "4ca8e85a-a455-55d3-8b22-32f09e8e38cb",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Monitoring and logging capability is partially implemented. Central logging, storage metrics and Alertmanager already exist outside the application. Inspected Core defaults do not define the specific75percent audit capacity threshold plus required audit-failure routes to SA/ISSO. Reuse/tune those shared monitors; this is not a requirement for every API/container to send email or maintain its own audit-volume monitor. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the recommendation where existing capabilities need alerting configuration. Credit existing sources and monitoring; configure and verify the specific required alert coverage and recipient delivery. This does not establish that every required detector or alert rule is already present.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Identify the assessed audit/log repository and capacity metric; configure an immediate warning at 75 percent utilization to the recipients specified by this exact rule. Use a different threshold only where this rule expressly permits it and the required approval is retained. Test threshold triggering and delivery. Retain event-source mappings, rules, routes and delivery-test evidence. Reuse shared monitoring rather than create a separate application alert engine.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[re-platform-monitor] re-platform-monitor\nDefault Kubernetes storage/system alerts enabled; alertmanager exists but no SA/ISSO receiver routes or exact75percent audit repository rule are set in this file.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/prometheus-stack/values/kube-prometheus-stack-values.yaml\n\n[re-platform-falco] re-platform-falco\nFalco/Sidekick sends findings to stdout/Loki; direct Alertmanager delivery is commented out. No required recipient delivery is established by the pipeline profile.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/falco/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Monitoring and logging capability is partially implemented. Central logging, storage metrics and Alertmanager already exist outside the application. Inspected Core defaults do not define the specific75percent audit capacity threshold plus required audit-failure routes to SA/ISSO. Reuse/tune those shared monitors; this is not a requirement for every API/container to send email or maintain its own audit-volume monitor. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the recommendation where existing capabilities need alerting configuration. Credit existing sources and monitoring; configure and verify the specific required alert coverage and recipient delivery. This does not establish that every required detector or alert rule is already present.\n\nNext action: Identify the assessed audit/log repository and capacity metric; configure an immediate warning at 75 percent utilization to the recipients specified by this exact rule. Use a different threshold only where this rule expressly permits it and the required approval is retained. Test threshold triggering and delivery. Retain event-source mappings, rules, routes and delivery-test evidence. Reuse shared monitoring rather than create a separate application alert engine."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233171",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233171",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000360",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233171",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000360",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233171r961401_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233171r961401",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000360-CTR-000815",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must provide an immediate real-time alert to the SA and ISSO, at a minimum, of all audit failure events requiring real-time alerts.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to provide an immediate real-time alert to the SA and ISSO of all audit failure events requiring real-time alerts.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to determine if it is configured to provide an immediate real-time alert to the SA and ISSO of all audit failure events requiring real-time alerts. \n\nIf the container platform is not configured to provide an immediate real-time alert, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "It is critical for the appropriate personnel to be aware if a system is at risk of failing to process audit logs as required. Without a real-time alert, security personnel may be unaware of an impending failure of the audit capability and system operation may be adversely affected.\n\nAlerts provide organizations with urgent messages. Real-time alerts provide these messages immediately (i.e., the time from event detection to alert occurs in seconds or less).",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001858"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "fb9a02ea-a1d3-5438-af75-33709f5f34f8",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Monitoring and logging capability is partially implemented. Central logging, storage metrics and Alertmanager already exist outside the application. Inspected Core defaults do not define the specific75percent audit capacity threshold plus required audit-failure routes to SA/ISSO. Reuse/tune those shared monitors; this is not a requirement for every API/container to send email or maintain its own audit-volume monitor. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the recommendation where existing capabilities need alerting configuration. Credit existing sources and monitoring; configure and verify the specific required alert coverage and recipient delivery. This does not establish that every required detector or alert rule is already present.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Identify the audit/log processing failures covered by this exact rule, including capture, hardware/software, collector/backend and storage failures as applicable. Configure the required alerts and named recipients using the existing central monitoring. Test coverage and delivery; verify real-time latency where the rule requires it. Retain event-source mappings, rules, routes and delivery-test evidence. Reuse shared monitoring rather than create a separate application alert engine.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[re-platform-monitor] re-platform-monitor\nDefault Kubernetes storage/system alerts enabled; alertmanager exists but no SA/ISSO receiver routes or exact75percent audit repository rule are set in this file.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/prometheus-stack/values/kube-prometheus-stack-values.yaml\n\n[re-platform-falco] re-platform-falco\nFalco/Sidekick sends findings to stdout/Loki; direct Alertmanager delivery is commented out. No required recipient delivery is established by the pipeline profile.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/falco/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Monitoring and logging capability is partially implemented. Central logging, storage metrics and Alertmanager already exist outside the application. Inspected Core defaults do not define the specific75percent audit capacity threshold plus required audit-failure routes to SA/ISSO. Reuse/tune those shared monitors; this is not a requirement for every API/container to send email or maintain its own audit-volume monitor. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the recommendation where existing capabilities need alerting configuration. Credit existing sources and monitoring; configure and verify the specific required alert coverage and recipient delivery. This does not establish that every required detector or alert rule is already present.\n\nNext action: Identify the audit/log processing failures covered by this exact rule, including capture, hardware/software, collector/backend and storage failures as applicable. Configure the required alerts and named recipients using the existing central monitoring. Test coverage and delivery; verify real-time latency where the rule requires it. Retain event-source mappings, rules, routes and delivery-test evidence. Reuse shared monitoring rather than create a separate application alert engine."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233181",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233181",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000374",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233181",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000374",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233181r961443_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233181r961443",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000374-CTR-000865",
          "rule_title": "All audit records must use UTC or GMT time stamps.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to use UTC or GMT or local time that offset from UTC based time stamps for log records.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform documentation and configuration files to determine if time stamps for log records can be mapped to UTC or GMT or local time that offsets from UTC. \n\nIf the time stamp cannot be mapped to UTC or GMT, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The container platform and its components must generate audit records using either Coordinated Universal Time (UTC) or Greenwich Mean Time (GMT) time stamps or local time that offset from UTC. All the components must use the same standard so that the events can be tied together to understand what took place within the overall container platform.\n\nTime stamps generated by the container platform and its components must include date and time.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001890"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "f68524c7-7b6d-540c-bb77-e681baa7ac3e",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Vector collects pod/node records with namespace/container/host labels and forwards them to Loki; Grafana supplies centralized search/reduction. UDS identity listener timestamps events in UTC with subsecond precision, and container clocks are host-derived.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[core-id-audit] core-id-audit\nUDS event listener prints user/admin events to stdout with event fields and UTC timestamps. Vector can collect these without a separately configured app forwarding agent.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/plugin/src/main/java/com/defenseunicorns/uds/keycloak/plugin/eventListeners/JSONLogEventListenerProvider.java\n\n[core-grafana] core-grafana\nGrafana disables local login, uses UDS OAuth, maps Admin group to Admin and Auditor to Viewer, and rejects unmatched groups. OAuth secrets are file-mounted.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Vector collects pod/node records with namespace/container/host labels and forwards them to Loki; Grafana supplies centralized search/reduction. UDS identity listener timestamps events in UTC with subsecond precision, and container clocks are host-derived.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233182",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233182",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000375",
