          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000383-CTR-000910",
          "rule_title": "All nonessential, unnecessary, and unsecure DoW ports, protocols, and services must be disabled in the container platform.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to only utilize secure ports and protocols required for operation that have been accepted for use as per the Ports, Protocols, and Services Category Assignments List (CAL) from DISA (PPSM).",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to determine if services or capabilities presently on the information system are required for operational or mission needs. \n\nIf additional services or capabilities are present on the system, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "To properly offer services to the user and to orchestrate containers, the container platform may offer services that use ports and protocols that best fit those services. The container platform, when offering the services, must only offer the services on ports and protocols authorized by the DoW.\n\nTo validate that the services are using only the approved ports and protocols, the organization must perform a periodic scan/review of the container platform and disable functions, ports, protocols, and services deemed to be unneeded or nonsecure.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001762"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "1dc684d6-996f-593b-a384-889bb8ae5d10",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS namespace default-deny and explicit Package ports govern exposure, while admission denies unapproved host ports/NodePort. The approved PPSM list must match the final MPN profile; no blanket raw-network access is inferred.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-network] core-network\nOperator generates namespace ingress/egress default-deny NetworkPolicy; connectivity requires declared allow rules.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/operator/controllers/network/defaults/default-deny-all.ts\n\n[core-policy] core-policy\nPepr mutates/validates non-root execution, no privilege escalation and dropped capabilities, including init and ephemeral containers; exemptions are explicit.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts\n\n[mesh] Mesh and policy\nPackage chart declares ambient mesh and generated network policy. Disabled chart-local NetworkPolicy and plaintext application URLs do not establish missing mesh enforcement.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-uds-package/templates/package.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS namespace default-deny and explicit Package ports govern exposure, while admission denies unapproved host ports/NodePort. The approved PPSM list must match the final MPN profile; no blanket raw-network access is inferred.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233191",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233191",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000384",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233191",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000384",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233191r961473_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233191r961473",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000384-CTR-000915",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must prevent component execution in accordance with organization-defined policies regarding software program usage and restrictions, and/or rules authorizing the terms and conditions of software program usage.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform so that any platform components that are not required in order to meet the organization's mission are disabled or removed. Document the components that must be disabled or removed for reference.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review documentation and configuration setting to determine if policies, rules, or restrictions exist regarding usage of container platform components. \n\nIf no such no restrictions are in place, this is not a finding. \n\nIdentify any components the organization requires to be disabled or removed and configure the container platform according to that policy. \n\nIf the container platform components are not disabled or removed according to the organization's policy, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The container platform may offer components such as DNS services, firewall services, router services, or web services that are not required by every organization to meet their needs. Container platform components may also add capabilities that run counter to the mission or that provide users with functionality that exceeds mission requirements. To meet the requirements of an organization, the container platform must have a method to remove or disable components not required to meet the organization's mission.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001764"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "41e9535b-1728-5976-971e-af7e07fdff9e",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS/Zarf packages enumerate delivered components/images and release provenance; Kubernetes RBAC restricts deployment changes, Pepr admission restricts runnable workloads, and immutable image-based upgrades replace component files. Registry garbage collection is a separate rule.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[images] Image release process\nBuilder verifies source digests and produces archive hashes; sample values with empty digests do not establish that a built release is unpinned. Assessed release/SBOM/scans still needed.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/scripts/build-bundle.py\n\n[core-policy] core-policy\nPepr mutates/validates non-root execution, no privilege escalation and dropped capabilities, including init and ephemeral containers; exemptions are explicit.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts\n\n[app-namespace] app-namespace\nAll declared workload charts target horizon-uds; package supplies dedicated namespace, distinct workload charts and generated deployment credentials.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/zarf.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS/Zarf packages enumerate delivered components/images and release provenance; Kubernetes RBAC restricts deployment changes, Pepr admission restricts runnable workloads, and immutable image-based upgrades replace component files. Registry garbage collection is a separate rule.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233192",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233192",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000386",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233192",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000386",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233192r961479_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233192r961479",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000386-CTR-000920",
          "rule_title": "The container platform registry must employ a deny-all, permit-by-exception (whitelist) policy to allow only authorized container images in the container platform.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to utilize a deny-all, permit-by-exception policy when allowing the execution of authorized software.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review documentation and configuration settings to identify if the container platform whitelisting specifies which container platform components are allowed to execute.\n\nCheck for the existence of policy settings or policy files that can be configured to restrict container platform component execution. Demonstrate how the program execution is restricted. Look for a deny-all, permit-by-exception policy of restriction.\n\nSome methods for restricting execution include but are not limited to the use of custom capabilities built into the application or Software Restriction Policies, Application Security Manager, or Role-Based Access Controls (RBAC).\n\nIf container platform whitelisting is not utilized or does not follow a deny-all, permit-by-exception (whitelist) policy, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Controlling the sources where container images can be pulled from allows the organization to define what software can be run within the container platform. Allowing any container image to be introduced and instantiated within the container platform may introduce malicious code and vulnerabilities to the platform and the hosting system. \n\nThe container platform registry must deny all container images except for those signed by organizational-approved sources.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001774"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "23407fdf-0242-5a14-a269-27810158584b",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The official check explicitly permits RBAC as an execution-restriction method. The approved delivery path qualifies named main builds, verifies an enumerated image inventory/digests and publishes only its vouched immutable package through protected release credentials; ordinary application identities have no installation interface. Use platform deployment RBAC plus this permit-by-exception release inventory as the proposed equivalent, rather than demand a new application allow-list. Cluster-admin exceptions must retain platform-owner approval.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Document the approved inventory, deployment RBAC boundary and privileged exception policy as the proposed platform equivalent.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-images-release] re-images-release\nSuccessful Nov5 trusted release2026.45.4-uds.1 binds Horizonb84b9af/build19710/run42109769948, package d528b7f, Core1.14.0, actual identity.mode=uds issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. Twelve delivered image platform digests are explicit; no bundled Keycloak is shipped. Published package digest sha256:486f55939f94ab081d2a12ecade5439512f8f7c4b3119c40b027c711d386adc9. Read build-info/platform-contract/publication metadata artifact13464284834, not just example chart defaults.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-images-publish] re-images-publish\nThe main-only protected release workflow selects qualified successful main source, validates image inventory/source digests against packed OCI manifests, binds archive SHA256SUMS, requires cryptographically verified Witness evidence and CAT vouch before registry publication; digest-qualified registry bundle fixes installation content.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/scripts/ci/release.py\n\n[re-images-verify] re-images-verify\nReal signer verification uses pinned DU Fulcio/Sigstore TSA anchors, expected subject/SAN/issuer, P256/SHA256 DSSE signature, code-signing EKU and RFC3161 timestamp. release.py check_evidence calls verify_production for all required phases and binds exact archives/build-info/packed image digests; CAT vouch gates publication. Actual delivery run succeeded these gates.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/scripts/ci/verify_attestation.py",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. The official check explicitly permits RBAC as an execution-restriction method. The approved delivery path qualifies named main builds, verifies an enumerated image inventory/digests and publishes only its vouched immutable package through protected release credentials; ordinary application identities have no installation interface. Use platform deployment RBAC plus this permit-by-exception release inventory as the proposed equivalent, rather than demand a new application allow-list. Cluster-admin exceptions must retain platform-owner approval.\n\nNext action: Document the approved inventory, deployment RBAC boundary and privileged exception policy as the proposed platform equivalent."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233193",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233193",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000389",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233193",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000389",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233193r1268095_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233193r1268095",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000389-CTR-000925",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must require users to reauthenticate when organization-defined circumstances or situations require reauthentication.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to require a user to reauthenticate when organization-defined circumstances or situations are met.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review documentation and configuration to determine if the container platform requires a user to reauthenticate when organization-defined circumstances or situations are met. \n\nIf the container platform does not meet this requirement, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Controlling user access is paramount in securing the container platform. During a user's access to the container platform, events may occur that change the user's access and which require reauthentication. For instance, if the capability to change security roles or escalate privileges is implemented, it is critical the user reauthenticate.\n\nIn addition to the reauthentication requirements associated with change in security roles or privilege escalation, organizations may require reauthentication of individuals in other situations, including (but not limited to) the following circumstances:\n\n(i) When authenticators change; \n(ii) When roles change; \n(iii) When security categories of information systems change; \n(iv) When the execution of privileged functions occurs; \n(v) After a fixed period of time; or\n(vi) Periodically.\n\nWithin the DoW, the minimum circumstances requiring reauthentication are privilege escalation and role changes.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002038"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "6ffd16bc-768d-5097-bdd7-5fc92c662eef",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Delivered authentication uses shared UDS identity with60-second access tokens,600-second idle and10-hour maximum sessions, but expiry/silent renewal is not the minimum required fresh authentication on role change or privilege escalation. The pinned realm has no configured step-up/role-change reauthentication action and the delivered client does not request it. Reuse provider policy or a central authorization transition hook; no second password prompt/login service belongs in the API.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Configure/document fresh authentication for role changes and privilege escalation at the Gallatin-operated shared IdP/administrative transition boundary; link the shared session-policy workstream. Gallatin owns this policy/integration; Core default settings are source-known.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-platform-delivered-profile] re-platform-delivered-profile\nSuccessful UDS publication2026.45.4-uds.1 records package d528, app b84b9af, Core1.14.0, identity.mode=uds issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds and12 digest-pinned images; no bundled Keycloak image. This is delivered artifact evidence, not assumed default profile.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-platform-realm] re-platform-realm\nUDS realm provides user/admin event logging and JSON event listener, native enabled/account roles, strong default password policy. Inactivity disable workflow defaults false; terms required-action enablement defaults false; CRL checking defaults false with OCSP checking true. No password blacklist or24h-min-age/eight-character-distance in passwordPolicy.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-platform-delivered-jwt] re-platform-delivered-jwt\nDelivered API uses jose jwtVerify against selected issuer/audience/JWKS; expiration verification is enabled. It does not introspect each token or implement role-change/privilege-escalation step-up triggers.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.oidc-jwt.service.ts\n\n[re-platform-delivered-browser-auth] re-platform-delivered-browser-auth\nDelivered browser OIDC client follows provider session/token lifecycle with code/PKCE automatic silent renewal; no role-change/privilege-escalation prompt=login/max_age policy is configured here.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/web/src/auth/auth-service.ts\n\n[mpn-identity-values] mpn-identity-values\nCore1.14 chart default fields: realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER=\"0\"; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset; terms enabled unset; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=false; realmAuthFlows username/password/X509/OTPtrue, X509MFAfalse. Gallatin owns these settings on MPN, independent of DU managedIL5/IL6 clusters.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-override] mpn-identity-override\nCore value sourcePath .keycloak.keycloak maps to chart root; bundle override path is packages[core].overrides.keycloak.keycloak.values[].path (e.g.realmInitEnv), not Horizonpackageapplication values.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/common/zarf.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Delivered authentication uses shared UDS identity with60-second access tokens,600-second idle and10-hour maximum sessions, but expiry/silent renewal is not the minimum required fresh authentication on role change or privilege escalation. The pinned realm has no configured step-up/role-change reauthentication action and the delivered client does not request it. Reuse provider policy or a central authorization transition hook; no second password prompt/login service belongs in the API.\n\nNext action: Configure/document fresh authentication for role changes and privilege escalation at the Gallatin-operated shared IdP/administrative transition boundary; link the shared session-policy workstream. Gallatin owns this policy/integration; Core default settings are source-known."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233195",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233195",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000391",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233195",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000391",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233195r1268098_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233195r1268098",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000391-CTR-000935",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must be configured to use multifactor authentication for user authentication.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to accept standard DoW multifactor token-based credentials when users interface with the platform.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review documentation and configuration to ensure the container platform is configured to use an approved DoW multifactor token (CAC) when accessing platform via user interfaces. \n\nIf multifactor authentication is not configured, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Controlling access to the container platform and its components is paramount in having a secure and stable system. Validating users is the first step in controlling the access. Users may be validated by the overall container platform or they may be validated by each component. To standardize and reduce the risks of unauthorized access, the use of multifactor token-based credentials is the preferred method.\n\nDoW has mandated the use of the common access card (CAC) to support identity management and personal authentication for systems covered under HSPD 12, as well as a primary component of layered protection for national security systems.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001953"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "283e611b-fc0e-58b9-aab3-3e466bd6b123",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS provides centralized Keycloak identity, OIDC/OTP/X509 flows and individual user identities; Grafana rejects unmatched groups and disables its local login. Native cluster/API administration remains MPN-owned and must retain its inheritance mapping.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-keycloak] core-keycloak\nCore uses identity-config 0.32.0, supports X509 and OTP by default; inactivity auto-disable is opt-in, and concurrent-session limit defaults to unlimited (0). This is platform SSO, not Horizon bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[core-id-realm] core-id-realm\nUDS realm defines three-failure lockout, permanentLockout=true, 600-second idle, PBKDF2-SHA256 password policy with 15 characters and classes/history/expiry; JSON event listener is enabled. Some controls remain optional.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[core-grafana] core-grafana\nGrafana disables local login, uses UDS OAuth, maps Admin group to Admin and Auditor to Viewer, and rejects unmatched groups. OAuth secrets are file-mounted.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS provides centralized Keycloak identity, OIDC/OTP/X509 flows and individual user identities; Grafana rejects unmatched groups and disables its local login. Native cluster/API administration remains MPN-owned and must retain its inheritance mapping.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233200",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233200",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000400",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233200",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000400",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233200r961521_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233200r961521",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000400-CTR-000960",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must prohibit the use of cached authenticators after an organization-defined time period.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to prohibit the use of cached authenticators after an organization-defined time period.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to determine if the platform is configured to prohibit the use of cached authenticators after an organization-defined time period. \n\nIf the container platform is not configured to prohibit the use of cached authenticators after an organization-defined time period, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "If cached authentication information is out of date, the validity of the authentication information may be questionable.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
