        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233210",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233210",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000414",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233210",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000414",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233210r961563_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233210r961563",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000414-CTR-001010",
          "rule_title": "Vulnerability scanning applications must implement privileged access authorization to all container platform components, containers, and container images for selected organization-defined vulnerability scanning activities.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the vulnerability scanning application to have privileged access to the container platform components, containers, and container images.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Validate that scanning applications have privileged access to container platform components, containers, and container images to properly perform vulnerability scans. \n\nIf privileged access is not given to the scanning application, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "In certain situations, the nature of the vulnerability scanning may be more intrusive, or the container platform component that is the subject of the scanning may contain highly sensitive information. Privileged access authorization to selected system components facilitates more thorough vulnerability scanning and protects the sensitive nature of such scanning.\n\nThe vulnerability scanning application must utilize privileged access authorization for the scanning account.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001067"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "84095caa-4c3b-5a09-a270-aa7d9e7f3f33",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Namespace-scoped service identities and Kubernetes RBAC provide the authorization mechanism for privileged scanning access; Falco has an explicit privileged infrastructure exemption instead of granting ordinary workloads host privileges. The actual continuous CVE scanner integration is separately flagged.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-policy] core-policy\nPepr mutates/validates non-root execution, no privilege escalation and dropped capabilities, including init and ephemeral containers; exemptions are explicit.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts\n\n[core-falco] core-falco\nFalco loads shipped rule sets, detects runtime behavior and ships Sidekick events to Loki. Alertmanager delivery is commented out; detection is not automatic access revocation.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/falco/values/values.yaml\n\n[host] MPN ownership\nCurrent scope assigns control plane/node OS to MPN; no host assessment or live configuration was supplied.\nhttps://gallatin.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/GG/pages/733085804/WIP+STIG+SRG+Compliance+Scope+MPN",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Namespace-scoped service identities and Kubernetes RBAC provide the authorization mechanism for privileged scanning access; Falco has an explicit privileged infrastructure exemption instead of granting ordinary workloads host privileges. The actual continuous CVE scanner integration is separately flagged.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233211",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233211",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000516",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233211",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000516",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233211r961863_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233211r961863",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000416-CTR-001015",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must implement NSA-approved cryptography to protect classified information in accordance with applicable federal laws, Executive Orders, directives, policies, regulations, and standards.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to utilize NSA-approved cryptography to protect classified information.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review documentation to verify that the container platform is using NSA-approved cryptography to protect classified data and applications. \n\nIf the container platform is not using NSA-approved cryptography for classified data and applications, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Use of weak or untested encryption algorithms undermines the purposes of utilizing encryption to protect data and images. The container platform must implement cryptographic modules adhering to the higher standards approved by the federal government since this provides assurance they have been tested and validated.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002450"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "d7ce4d66-ac72-51e2-957c-0ff50b57ab47",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The deployment is classified, so this rule applies. Check the actual cryptography protecting container-platform data, including stored Secrets and persistent data. Core FIPS support alone does not establish NSA approval of the configured encryption. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work pending verification for this and similar classified-cryptography requirements. Confirm mission data scope and the actual inherited MPN storage/transport cryptographic boundary before assigning new implementation. Preserve this rule's exact cryptographic requirements; do not infer blanket classified approval from FIPS naming or duplicate platform protection inside Horizon.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Mission data/security authority and MPN cryptographic-boundary owner; Gallatin for application mapping/configuration residuals\n\nNext action: In the post-classification verification pass, confirm the mission data classification and protection requirements and identify the MPN/Gallatin responsibility boundary. Inspect actual module/solution/configuration evidence for the cryptographic tasks and data covered by this exact rule, credit supported inherited protection, and identify only uncovered application/configuration responsibilities. Use NSA-approved encryption for the platform data and traffic covered by this rule; verify the chosen implementation, approval and settings. Reuse the host/storage/network controls that implement it. Retain a controlled scope/protection mapping and revise the recommendation where coverage is supported.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-mission-nsa-system-solutions] re-mission-nsa-system-solutions\nNSA describes approved classified solutions as properly configured layered system designs, and cautions that partner/non-US implementations follow the applicable national authority. Ordinary product FIPS images are not themselves a registered CSfC system or evidence of an approved classified boundary.\nhttps://www.nsa.gov/Resources/Commercial-Solutions-for-Classified-Program/faq/\n\n[re-mission-nsa-registration] re-mission-nsa-registration\nNSA solution registration requires system diagrams, selected components, CONOPS and the relevant approved solution package/authority artifacts. This supports one boundary-level evidence package rather than an invented requirement to add independent classified encryption inside each application service.\nhttps://www.nsa.gov/Resources/Commercial-Solutions-for-Classified-Program/solution-registration/\n\n[re-mission-nsa-cp] re-mission-nsa-cp\nNSA publishes distinct classified data-in-transit and data-at-rest capability packages with architecture/component/configuration requirements; an approved storage or network boundary must be mapped to its own use case, not assumed from a TLS version or FIPS image label.\nhttps://www.nsa.gov/Resources/Commercial-Solutions-for-Classified-Program/Capability-Packages/\n\n[classified-crypto-definition] classified-crypto-definition\nNSA-approved cryptography includes an approved algorithm, an implementation approved for the relevant environment and supporting key management. A FIPS validation certificate by itself does not establish that full approval.\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/NSA_approved_cryptography",
          "finding_details": "The deployment is classified, so this rule applies. Check the actual cryptography protecting container-platform data, including stored Secrets and persistent data. Core FIPS support alone does not establish NSA approval of the configured encryption. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work pending verification for this and similar classified-cryptography requirements. Confirm mission data scope and the actual inherited MPN storage/transport cryptographic boundary before assigning new implementation. Preserve this rule's exact cryptographic requirements; do not infer blanket classified approval from FIPS naming or duplicate platform protection inside Horizon.\n\nNext action: In the post-classification verification pass, confirm the mission data classification and protection requirements and identify the MPN/Gallatin responsibility boundary. Inspect actual module/solution/configuration evidence for the cryptographic tasks and data covered by this exact rule, credit supported inherited protection, and identify only uncovered application/configuration responsibilities. Use NSA-approved encryption for the platform data and traffic covered by this rule; verify the chosen implementation, approval and settings. Reuse the host/storage/network controls that implement it. Retain a controlled scope/protection mapping and revise the recommendation where coverage is supported."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233220",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233220",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000429",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233220",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000429",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233220r1050650_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233220r1050650",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000429-CTR-001060",
          "rule_title": "The container platform keystore must implement encryption to prevent unauthorized disclosure of information at rest within the container platform.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform keystore encryption to maintain the confidentiality and integrity of information for applicable sensitivity level.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review container platform keystore documentation and configuration to verify encryption levels meet the information sensitivity level. \n\nIf the container platform keystore encryption configuration does not meet system requirements, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Container platform keystore is used for container deployments for persistent storage of all its REST API objects. These objects are sensitive in nature and should be encrypted at rest to avoid any unauthorized disclosure. Selection of a cryptographic mechanism is based on the need to protect the confidentiality of organizational information. The strength of mechanism is commensurate with the security category and/or classification of the information.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002476"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "69718e6a-b4f2-5ddf-8a10-ef04acfd84f6",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Human review on 2026-11-10 selected provisional Likely covered through inherited MPN/platform storage controls under the instruction to apply the same recommendation to similar storage-protection requirements. PR reviewer must confirm or reject platform ownership and the mapping for keystore/Secret/datastore encryption. Actual platform protection and the authoritative responsibility boundary remain unverified; this is not an accepted compliance result or an N/A determination. Gallatin retains application data requirements, use of approved protected storage and any application/configuration residuals. This supersedes any earlier assumption that Gallatin must provision a new storage layer.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: MPN/platform storage owner (proposed; reviewer confirmation required); Gallatin for application requirements/configuration and residuals\n\nNext action: PR reviewer must confirm or reject MPN/platform ownership and inherited coverage for keystore/Secret/datastore encryption. In the post-classification verification pass, obtain the responsibility agreement and evidence for the actual protection, map all data/components covered by this exact rule, and verify Horizon uses the protected boundary correctly. Identify any Gallatin application/configuration residual separately and revise the recommendation if inheritance or protection is unsupported. Do not prescribe new storage infrastructure before that verification. Confirm Kubernetes Secret/datastore encryption and key-management ownership explicitly; do not infer that encrypted data volumes cover this distinct scope.\n\nEvidence:\n[sol-storage-k8-encryption] sol-storage-k8-encryption\nKubernetes Secret storage is plaintext unless API server uses an EncryptionConfiguration with a nonidentity provider first. Configure --encryption-provider-config or the distribution equivalent; existing values need rewriting to migrate stored Secret data after enablement.\nhttps://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/encrypt-data/\n\n[sol-storage-core-keystore] sol-storage-core-keystore\nCore Keycloak PVCs optionally use persistence.storageClassName; storage encryption and identity database protection are supplied below the chart. Protect Kubernetes Secrets/control-plane datastore separately.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/pvc.yaml\n\n[sol-storage-core-boundary] sol-storage-core-boundary\nCore expects an existing cluster and defaultStorageClass; it does not provision MPN host disk encryption. Gallatin owns this infrastructure choice and can bind the app/CorePVCs to a selected encrypted storage class.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/getting-started/production/prerequisites.mdx",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Human review on 2026-11-10 selected provisional Likely covered through inherited MPN/platform storage controls under the instruction to apply the same recommendation to similar storage-protection requirements. PR reviewer must confirm or reject platform ownership and the mapping for keystore/Secret/datastore encryption. Actual platform protection and the authoritative responsibility boundary remain unverified; this is not an accepted compliance result or an N/A determination. Gallatin retains application data requirements, use of approved protected storage and any application/configuration residuals. This supersedes any earlier assumption that Gallatin must provision a new storage layer.\n\nNext action: PR reviewer must confirm or reject MPN/platform ownership and inherited coverage for keystore/Secret/datastore encryption. In the post-classification verification pass, obtain the responsibility agreement and evidence for the actual protection, map all data/components covered by this exact rule, and verify Horizon uses the protected boundary correctly. Identify any Gallatin application/configuration residual separately and revise the recommendation if inheritance or protection is unsupported. Do not prescribe new storage infrastructure before that verification. Confirm Kubernetes Secret/datastore encryption and key-management ownership explicitly; do not infer that encrypted data volumes cover this distinct scope."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233221",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233221",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000431",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233221",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000431",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233221r1137646_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233221r1137646",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000431-CTR-001065",
          "rule_title": "The container platform runtime must maintain separate execution domains for each container by assigning each container a separate address space.",
          "fix_text": "Deploy a container platform runtime capable of maintaining a separate execution domain and namespace for each executing process. Create a namespace for each containers, defining them as logical groups.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review container platform runtime documentation and configuration is maintaining a separate execution domain for each executing process. Different groups of applications, and services with different security needs, should be deployed in separate namespaces as a first level of isolation. \n\nIf container platform runtime is not configured to execute processes in separate domains and namespaces, this is a finding. \n\nIf namespaces use defaults, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Container namespace access is limited upon runtime execution. Each container is a distinct process so that communication between containers is performed in a manner controlled through security policies that limits the communication so one container cannot modify another container. Different groups of containers with different security needs should be deployed in separate namespaces as a first level of isolation.\n\nNamespaces are a key boundary for network policies, orchestrator access control restrictions, and other important security controls. Separating workloads into namespaces can help contain attacks and limit the impact of mistakes or destructive actions by authorized users.\n\nThis requirement also applies to Zero Trust initiatives.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002530"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "c28377aa-6944-557d-83b9-61d55272ebff",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS enforces namespace default-deny and service identity rules plus non-root/no-escalation/drop-capabilities admission; management UIs are separate and SSO-group restricted. Kubernetes RBAC/registry/storage administration remains in the MPN/UDS inherited boundary.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-policy] core-policy\nPepr mutates/validates non-root execution, no privilege escalation and dropped capabilities, including init and ephemeral containers; exemptions are explicit.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts\n\n[core-network] core-network\nOperator generates namespace ingress/egress default-deny NetworkPolicy; connectivity requires declared allow rules.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/operator/controllers/network/defaults/default-deny-all.ts\n\n[core-grafana] core-grafana\nGrafana disables local login, uses UDS OAuth, maps Admin group to Admin and Auditor to Viewer, and rejects unmatched groups. OAuth secrets are file-mounted.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS enforces namespace default-deny and service identity rules plus non-root/no-escalation/drop-capabilities admission; management UIs are separate and SSO-group restricted. Kubernetes RBAC/registry/storage administration remains in the MPN/UDS inherited boundary.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233222",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233222",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000435",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233222",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000435",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233222r961620_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233222r961620",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000435-CTR-001070",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must protect against or limit the effects of all types of denial-of-service (DoS) attacks by employing organization-defined security safeguards.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to protect against or limit the effects of all types of DoS attacks by employing defined security safeguards. Safeguards such as resource limits on memory, storage, and CPU can be used.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review documentation and configuration to determine if the container platform can protect against or limit the effects of all types of DoS attacks by employing defined security safeguards against resource depletion. Examples of resource limits are on memory, storage, and CPU. \n\nIf the container platform cannot be configured to protect against or limit the effects of all types of DoS, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "DoS is a condition when a resource is not available for legitimate users. When this occurs, the organization either cannot accomplish its mission or must operate at degraded capacity.\n\nThis requirement addresses the configuration of the container platform to mitigate the impact of DoS attacks that have occurred. For each container platform component, known and potential DoS attacks must be identified and solutions for each type implemented. A variety of technologies exist to limit or, in some cases, eliminate the effects of DoS attacks (e.g., limiting runtime processes or restricting the number of sessions the container platform runtime open, limiting container resources to memory and CPU).\n\nProcesses are an important indicator of security-and operations-relevant container activity. Process names and their arguments provide important visibility into a container’s activity. If an image includes non-default aliases or renamed binaries, attackers will still attempt to use well-known names.\n\nThe same malicious or unwanted activity might affect multiple deployments across different applications or environments. Staff investigating a potential incident need to find those exposures quickly.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002385"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "c0527d74-ca25-52f7-84f3-bd89baeefa5a",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS admission/no-host access, namespace isolation, resource-constrained workloads and runtime Falco detection constrain resource abuse. Explicit init-container resource exceptions are separately recorded under V-270875.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-policy] core-policy\nPepr mutates/validates non-root execution, no privilege escalation and dropped capabilities, including init and ephemeral containers; exemptions are explicit.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts\n\n[core-network] core-network\nOperator generates namespace ingress/egress default-deny NetworkPolicy; connectivity requires declared allow rules.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/operator/controllers/network/defaults/default-deny-all.ts\n\n[core-falco] core-falco\nFalco loads shipped rule sets, detects runtime behavior and ships Sidekick events to Loki. Alertmanager delivery is commented out; detection is not automatic access revocation.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/falco/values/values.yaml\n\n[app-pods] app-pods\nCandidate service chart applies non-root/read-only/no-escalation contexts, Secret references, probes and resource settings. Rendered app pods corroborate those settings; some init containers lack explicit resources.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-service/templates/deployment.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS admission/no-host access, namespace isolation, resource-constrained workloads and runtime Falco detection constrain resource abuse. Explicit init-container resource exceptions are separately recorded under V-270875.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233224",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233224",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000439",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233224",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000439",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233224r961632_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233224r961632",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000439-CTR-001080",
          "rule_title": "The application must protect the confidentiality and integrity of transmitted information.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to utilize a transmission method that maintains the confidentiality and integrity of information during transmission.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review container platform configuration to determine if it is using a transmission method that maintains the confidentiality and integrity of information during transmission.\n\nIf a transmission method is not being used that maintains the confidentiality and integrity of the data, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Without protection of the transmitted information, confidentiality and integrity may be compromised since unprotected communications can be intercepted and either read or altered. \n\nThis requirement applies only to those applications that either are distributed or can allow access to data non-locally. Use of this requirement will be limited to situations where the data owner has a strict requirement for ensuring data integrity and confidentiality is maintained at every step of the data transfer and handling process. When transmitting data, applications need to leverage transmission protection mechanisms, such as TLS, TLS VPNs, or IPsec.\n\nCommunication paths outside the physical protection of a controlled boundary are exposed to the possibility of interception and modification. Protecting the confidentiality and integrity of organizational information can be accomplished by physical means (e.g., employing physical distribution systems) or by logical means (e.g., employing cryptographic techniques). If physical means of protection are employed, then logical means (cryptography) do not have to be employed, and vice versa.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002418"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "596a86a0-9019-58bc-aac9-884084ec0872",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Core root PeerAuthentication requires STRICT mTLS for enrolled workloads; gateway minimum is TLS1.3 (optionally 1.2), and Horizon declares ambient enrollment. This supports protected traffic without adding application-local TLS everywhere.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-mtls] core-mtls\nRoot-namespace PeerAuthentication requires STRICT mTLS. Horizon opts into ambient mesh; in-mesh plaintext-looking URLs are protected by mesh rather than application TLS.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/common/chart/templates/peer-authentication.yaml\n\n[core-gateway] core-gateway\nGateway redirects HTTP to HTTPS and requires TLS 1.3 by default, optionally TLS 1.2; credentialName references Kubernetes TLS secrets.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/charts/uds-istio-config/templates/gateway.yaml\n\n[mesh] Mesh and policy\nPackage chart declares ambient mesh and generated network policy. Disabled chart-local NetworkPolicy and plaintext application URLs do not establish missing mesh enforcement.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-uds-package/templates/package.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Core root PeerAuthentication requires STRICT mTLS for enrolled workloads; gateway minimum is TLS1.3 (optionally 1.2), and Horizon declares ambient enrollment. This supports protected traffic without adding application-local TLS everywhere.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233226",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233226",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000441",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233226",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000441",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233226r961638_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233226r961638",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000441-CTR-001090",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must maintain the confidentiality and integrity of information during preparation for transmission.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to maintain the confidentiality and integrity of information using mechanisms such as TLS, TLS VPNs, or IPsec during preparation for transmission.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the documentation and deployed configuration to determine if the container platform maintains the confidentiality and integrity of information during preparation before transmission. \n\nIf the confidentiality and integrity are not maintained using mechanisms such as TLS, TLS VPNs, or IPsec during preparation before transmission, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Information may be unintentionally or maliciously disclosed or modified during preparation for transmission within the container platform during aggregation, at protocol transformation points, and during container image runtime. These unauthorized disclosures or modifications compromise the confidentiality or integrity of the information. When transmitting data, the container platform components need to leverage transmission protection mechanisms, such as TLS, TLS VPNs, or IPsec.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002420"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
