        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233228",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233228",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000447",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233228",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000447",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233228r961656_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233228r961656",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000447-CTR-001100",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must behave in a predictable and documented manner that reflects organizational and system objectives when invalid inputs are received.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform behave in a predictable and documented manner that reflects organizational and system objectives when invalid inputs are received.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the configuration to determine if the container platform behaves in a predictable and documented manner that reflects organizational and system objectives when invalid inputs are received. \n\nIf the container platform does not meet this requirement, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Software or code parameters typically follow well-defined protocols that use structured messages (i.e., commands or queries) to communicate between software modules or system components. Structured messages can contain raw or unstructured data interspersed with metadata or control information. If attacker-supplied inputs to construct structured messages without properly encoding such messages, then the attacker could insert malicious commands or special characters that can cause the data to be interpreted as control information or metadata.\n\nThis requirement guards against adverse or unintended system behavior caused by invalid inputs, where container platform components responses to the invalid input may be disruptive or cause the container image runtime to fail into an unsafe state.\n\nThe behavior will be derived from the organizational and system requirements and includes, but is not limited to, notification of the appropriate personnel, creating an audit record, and rejecting invalid input.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002754"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "cf76b95e-2596-5fbe-ae60-aa9b0284641f",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Admission rejects unsafe resources, mesh/security components have explicit startup/readiness controls, and Vector persists acknowledged log buffers outside the application process. These mechanisms support fail-closed/recovery diagnostics; final failure exercises remain confirmation work.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-policy] core-policy\nPepr mutates/validates non-root execution, no privilege escalation and dropped capabilities, including init and ephemeral containers; exemptions are explicit.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts\n\n[core-accesslog] core-accesslog\nIstio access logs go to stdout; path normalization merges slashes, proxies are held until ready, and forwarded certificate details are sanitized.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/values/base-istiod.yaml\n\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Admission rejects unsafe resources, mesh/security components have explicit startup/readiness controls, and Vector persists acknowledged log buffers outside the application process. These mechanisms support fail-closed/recovery diagnostics; final failure exercises remain confirmation work.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233229",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233229",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000450",
