          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "dd527bfd-1686-5f43-ab97-cb026e50a89d",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS/Zarf packages enumerate delivered components/images and release provenance; Kubernetes RBAC restricts deployment changes, Pepr admission restricts runnable workloads, and immutable image-based upgrades replace component files. Registry garbage collection is a separate rule.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[images] Image release process\nBuilder verifies source digests and produces archive hashes; sample values with empty digests do not establish that a built release is unpinned. Assessed release/SBOM/scans still needed.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/scripts/build-bundle.py\n\n[core-policy] core-policy\nPepr mutates/validates non-root execution, no privilege escalation and dropped capabilities, including init and ephemeral containers; exemptions are explicit.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts\n\n[app-namespace] app-namespace\nAll declared workload charts target horizon-uds; package supplies dedicated namespace, distinct workload charts and generated deployment credentials.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/zarf.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS/Zarf packages enumerate delivered components/images and release provenance; Kubernetes RBAC restricts deployment changes, Pepr admission restricts runnable workloads, and immutable image-based upgrades replace component files. Registry garbage collection is a separate rule.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233231",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233231",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000454",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233231",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000454",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233231r961677_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233231r961677",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000454-CTR-001115",
          "rule_title": "The container platform registry must remove old container images after updating versions have been made available.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform registry to update organization-defined images with current approved vendor version and remove obsolete images after updated versions have been installed. Configure the container platform runtime to execute latest organization-defined images from the container platform registry.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review container platform registry documentation and configuration to determine if organization-defined images contains latest approved vendor software image version. \n\nIf organization-defined images do not contain the latest approved vendor software image version, this is a finding. \n\nReview container platform registry documentation and configuration to determine if organization-defined images are removed after updated versions have been installed. \n\nIf organization-defined images are not removed after updated versions have been installed, this is a finding. \n\nReview container platform runtime documentation and configuration to determine if organization-defined images are executing latest image version from the container registry. \n\nIf container platform runtime is not executing latest organization-defined images from the container platform registry, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Obsolete and stale images need to be removed from the registry to ensure the container platform maintains a secure posture. While the storing of these images does not directly pose a threat, they do increase the likelihood of these images being deployed. Removing stale or obsolete images and only keeping the most recent versions of those that are still in use removes any possibility of vulnerable images being deployed.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002617"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "872beee1-d09e-5c86-9c01-158ebf8f1607",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Approved delivered image digests are already identified. Zarf includes a concrete prune implementation for unreferenced images, while the reviewed Core upgrade tasks do not schedule or invoke registry cleanup. Because Gallatin operates this registry, the remaining action is to add controlled cleanup to its own successful-upgrade maintenance path; no DU retention attestation is required and no new registry feature is needed.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE / MPN platform engineering\n\nNext action: Add uds zarf tools registry prune to Gallatin’s MPN post-upgrade maintenance procedure/job after successful deployment, with a deliberate retained rollback-package/image policy and explicit confirmation before deletion. The tool preserves images referenced by current Zarf packages; do not blindly prune unreferenced images still needed for rollback. Track this as one shared registry lifecycle task.\n\nEvidence:\n[mpn-registry-prune] mpn-registry-prune\nZarf0.86 includes tools registry prune, which deletes images not referenced by any current Zarf package; --confirm explicitly authorizes pruning. It is an executable maintenance action, not a Core automatic upgrade hook.\nhttps://github.com/zarf-dev/zarf/blob/v0.86.0/site/src/content/docs/commands/zarf_tools_registry_prune.md\n\n[mpn-core-init-default] mpn-core-init-default\nCore1.14 local setup initializes from Zarf init v0.86.0 without a registry-mode override. bundles/k3d-standard likewise includes that init package without mTLS/proxy selection. Production Core explicitly assumes a separately provided cluster and init bundle, not a DU-operated cluster.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/tasks/setup.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Approved delivered image digests are already identified. Zarf includes a concrete prune implementation for unreferenced images, while the reviewed Core upgrade tasks do not schedule or invoke registry cleanup. Because Gallatin operates this registry, the remaining action is to add controlled cleanup to its own successful-upgrade maintenance path; no DU retention attestation is required and no new registry feature is needed.\n\nNext action: Add uds zarf tools registry prune to Gallatin’s MPN post-upgrade maintenance procedure/job after successful deployment, with a deliberate retained rollback-package/image policy and explicit confirmation before deletion. The tool preserves images referenced by current Zarf packages; do not blindly prune unreferenced images still needed for rollback. Track this as one shared registry lifecycle task."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233233",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233233",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000456",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233233",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000456",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233233r1137649_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233233r1137649",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000456-CTR-001125",
          "rule_title": "The container platform registry must contain the latest images with most recent security-relevant software updates within 30 days unless the time period is directed by an authoritative source (e.g., IAVM, CTOs, DTMs, STIGs).",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform registry to use an approved vendor repository to ensure the latest images containing security-relevant updates are installed within 30 days unless a time period is directed by an authoritative source (IAVM, CTOs, DTMs, STIGs, etc.).",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review documentation and configuration to determine if the container platform registry inspects and contains the latest approved vendor repository images containing security-relevant updates within 30 days unless the time period is directed by an authoritative source (IAVM, CTOs, DTMs, STIGs, etc.). \n\nIf the container platform registry does not contain the latest image with security-relevant updates within 30 days unless a time period directed by the authoritative source, this is a finding.\n\nThe container platform registry should help the user understand where the code in the environment was deployed from, and must provide controls that prevent deployment from untrusted sources or registries.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Software supporting the container platform, images in the registry must stay up to date with the latest patches, service packs, and hot fixes. Not updating the container platform and container images will expose the organization to vulnerabilities.\n\nFlaws discovered during security assessments, continuous monitoring, incident response activities, or information system error handling must also be addressed expeditiously.\n\nOrganization-defined time periods for updating security-relevant container platform components may vary based on a variety of factors including, for example, the security category of the information system or the criticality of the update (i.e., severity of the vulnerability related to the discovered flaw).\n\nThis requirement will apply to software patch management solutions that are used to install patches across the enclave and to applications themselves that are not part of that patch management solution. For example, many browsers today provide the capability to install their own patch software. Patch criticality, as well as system criticality will vary. Therefore, the tactical situations regarding the patch management process will also vary. This means that the time period utilized must be a configurable parameter. Time frames for application of security-relevant software updates may be dependent upon the Information Assurance Vulnerability Management (IAVM) process.\n\nThe container platform components will be configured to check for and install security-relevant software updates within an identified time period from the availability of the update. The container platform registry will ensure the images are current. The specific time period will be defined by an authoritative source (e.g., IAVM, CTOs, DTMs, and STIGs).",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002605"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "f81903dd-64c8-594d-b20c-a0d4be2ccdc3",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The selected delivery is bound to a qualified successful main build with vendor FIPS image pins, automated advisory/update workflows and retained per-image scan records. Patch freshness can therefore be recommended from this delivered source/release evidence rather than left unknown for lack of a runtime command. This is date-bound; delivery source evidence is not a promise that an unseen operator installed future updates.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain release/scan dates and approved patch SLA; refresh this recommendation when the assessed release or advisory deadline changes.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-images-release] re-images-release\nSuccessful Nov5 trusted release2026.45.4-uds.1 binds Horizonb84b9af/build19710/run42109769948, package d528b7f, Core1.14.0, actual identity.mode=uds issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. Twelve delivered image platform digests are explicit; no bundled Keycloak is shipped. Published package digest sha256:486f55939f94ab081d2a12ecade5439512f8f7c4b3119c40b027c711d386adc9. Read build-info/platform-contract/publication metadata artifact13464284834, not just example chart defaults.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-images-compose] re-images-compose\nActual selected source pins node-fips24.22.1-slim@b61e57c2..., nginx-fips1.31.8@72d1e862..., postgres-fips18.6@9760ae95..., python-fips3.13.18; release runtimeSource binds exact platform manifests and PostgreSQL source/build definition.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/compose.yml\n\n[re-images-scans] re-images-scans\nActual qualified run42109769948 succeeded per-image Grype/SBOM and ClamAV jobs, with retained API/web reports; reused PostgreSQL has its own verified prior build/scan evidence. Weekly dependency security-update automation exists. Release packaging scans have a narrower scope than source and application-image scans.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/.github/workflows/build.yml\n\n[re-images-maintained] re-images-maintained\nVendor states FIPS product variants are maintained with daily rebuilds and CVE SLA. Selected delivery is Nov5 (five days before this review) with explicit supported product versions and recent scan records, rather than an unknown model-server implementation substituted for delivered products.\nhttps://www.chainguard.dev/docs/platform/fips/fips-images",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. The selected delivery is bound to a qualified successful main build with vendor FIPS image pins, automated advisory/update workflows and retained per-image scan records. Patch freshness can therefore be recommended from this delivered source/release evidence rather than left unknown for lack of a runtime command. This is date-bound; delivery source evidence is not a promise that an unseen operator installed future updates.\n\nNext action: Retain release/scan dates and approved patch SLA; refresh this recommendation when the assessed release or advisory deadline changes."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233234",
          "group_tree": [
