          ],
          "group_id": "V-233242",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000472",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233242r961731_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233242r961731",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000472-CTR-001170",
          "rule_title": "The organization-defined role must verify correct operation of security functions in the container platform.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform configuration and installation settings to perform verification of the correct operation of security functions.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review container platform documentation and configuration verification of the correct operation of security functions, which may include the valid connection to an external security manager (ESM). \n\nIf verification of the correct operation of security functions is not performed, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Without verification, security functions may not operate correctly and this failure may go unnoticed within the container platform. The container platform components must identity and ensure the security functions are still operational and applicable to the organization.\n\nSecurity functions are responsible for enforcing the system security policy and supporting the isolation of code and data on which the protection is based. Security functionality includes, but is not limited to, establishing system accounts, configuring access authorizations (i.e., permissions, privileges), setting events to be audited, and setting intrusion detection parameters.\n\nNotifications provided by information systems include, for example, electronic alerts to system administrators.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002696"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "8a3e91e0-1305-569f-989d-e365c07ea31c",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Core provides executable privileged on-demand validate/e2e tasks, including account setup, network and Pepr security-denial checks; readiness/startup checks supplement them. The rule permits verification on privileged command (and/or other schedules), so an additional application self-test subsystem is unnecessary.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Include the existing operator validation command/security test suite in the component verification procedure. Retain execution records during normal assessment; do not require new application code.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-platform-verify] re-platform-verify\nPrivileged UDS tasks include validate-packages and e2e-tests; the latter exercises live Keycloak account setup, network policies and Pepr controls. Source establishes an on-demand verification implementation, not merely health endpoints. No new execution claimed.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/tasks/test.yaml\n\n[re-platform-verify-security] re-platform-verify-security\nExecutable security verification asserts unsafe privileged/root pod requests are denied and safe security contexts work. Such tests are callable by operator, satisfying an on-demand verification path rather than requiring a distinct app test subsystem.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/test/vitest/pepr-policies/security.spec.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Core provides executable privileged on-demand validate/e2e tasks, including account setup, network and Pepr security-denial checks; readiness/startup checks supplement them. The rule permits verification on privileged command (and/or other schedules), so an additional application self-test subsystem is unnecessary.\n\nNext action: Include the existing operator validation command/security test suite in the component verification procedure. Retain execution records during normal assessment; do not require new application code."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233243",
