            "CCI-002702"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "a373b3c8-c757-533e-a2d9-397089d7d8e1",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Monitoring and logging capability is partially implemented. UDS Falco/logging/Prometheus provide security-function and tampering detection outside Horizon, but default Falco Alertmanager forwarding is commented out and no site security/operations recipient route is selected. Credit the shared detection stack; remaining configuration is active notification delivery, not duplication of detectors in the API. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the recommendation where existing capabilities need alerting configuration. Credit existing sources and monitoring; configure and verify the specific required alert coverage and recipient delivery. This does not establish that every required detector or alert rule is already present.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Define the security-function anomalies and responsible system-administration/operations roles in the site security plan. Map existing detection sources, configure missing alert rules and routes, and test anomaly notifications to the designated roles. Retain event-source mappings, rules, routes and delivery-test evidence. Reuse shared monitoring rather than create a separate application alert engine.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-platform-falco] re-platform-falco\nFalco/Sidekick sends findings to stdout/Loki; direct Alertmanager delivery is commented out. No required recipient delivery is established by the pipeline profile.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/falco/values/values.yaml\n\n[re-platform-monitor] re-platform-monitor\nDefault Kubernetes storage/system alerts enabled; alertmanager exists but no SA/ISSO receiver routes or exact75percent audit repository rule are set in this file.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/prometheus-stack/values/kube-prometheus-stack-values.yaml\n\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-core-falco-notify] mpn-core-falco-notify\nFalco sends structured events to Loki by default. falcosidekick.config.alertmanager.hostport/endpoint are documented but commented; Gallatin can enable the route to monitoring Alertmanager and configure receivers itself.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/falco/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Monitoring and logging capability is partially implemented. UDS Falco/logging/Prometheus provide security-function and tampering detection outside Horizon, but default Falco Alertmanager forwarding is commented out and no site security/operations recipient route is selected. Credit the shared detection stack; remaining configuration is active notification delivery, not duplication of detectors in the API. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the recommendation where existing capabilities need alerting configuration. Credit existing sources and monitoring; configure and verify the specific required alert coverage and recipient delivery. This does not establish that every required detector or alert rule is already present.\n\nNext action: Define the security-function anomalies and responsible system-administration/operations roles in the site security plan. Map existing detection sources, configure missing alert rules and routes, and test anomaly notifications to the designated roles. Retain event-source mappings, rules, routes and delivery-test evidence. Reuse shared monitoring rather than create a separate application alert engine."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233252",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233252",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000492",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233252",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000492",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233252r961791_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233252r961791",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000492-CTR-001220",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must generate audit records when successful/unsuccessful attempts to access security objects occur.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to generate audit records when successful/unsuccessful attempts to access security objects occur.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to verify audit records are generated on successful/unsuccessful attempts to access security objects. \n\nIf audit records are not generated, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The container platform and its components must generate audit records when successful and unsuccessful access security objects occur. All the components must use the same standard so that the events can be tied together to understand what took place within the overall container platform. This must establish, correlate, and help assist with investigating the events relating to an incident, or identify those responsible.\n\nWithout audit record generation access controls levels can access by unauthorized users unknowingly for malicious intent creating vulnerabilities within the container platform.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000172"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "7be33e62-6f24-5acb-b287-8110cb644946",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Platform/API security events are collected from node/pod sources and UDS identity events, with Falco adding privileged/runtime activity detection. MPN Kubernetes audit configuration remains inherited; app-domain audit coverage is not granted by this platform ruling.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[core-id-audit] core-id-audit\nUDS event listener prints user/admin events to stdout with event fields and UTC timestamps. Vector can collect these without a separately configured app forwarding agent.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/plugin/src/main/java/com/defenseunicorns/uds/keycloak/plugin/eventListeners/JSONLogEventListenerProvider.java\n\n[core-falco] core-falco\nFalco loads shipped rule sets, detects runtime behavior and ships Sidekick events to Loki. Alertmanager delivery is commented out; detection is not automatic access revocation.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/falco/values/values.yaml\n\n[host] MPN ownership\nCurrent scope assigns control plane/node OS to MPN; no host assessment or live configuration was supplied.\nhttps://gallatin.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/GG/pages/733085804/WIP+STIG+SRG+Compliance+Scope+MPN",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Platform/API security events are collected from node/pod sources and UDS identity events, with Falco adding privileged/runtime activity detection. MPN Kubernetes audit configuration remains inherited; app-domain audit coverage is not granted by this platform ruling.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233253",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233253",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000493",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233253",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000493",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233253r961794_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233253r961794",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000493-CTR-001225",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must generate audit records when successful/unsuccessful attempts to access security levels occur.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to generate audit records when successful/unsuccessful attempts to access security levels occur.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to verify audit records are generated on successful/unsuccessful attempts to access security levels. \n\nIf audit records are not generated, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Unauthorized users could access the security levels to exploit vulnerabilities within the container platform component. All the components must use the same standard so that the events can be tied together to understand what took place within the overall container platform. This must establish, correlate, and help assist with investigating the events relating to an incident, or identify those responsible.\n\nWithout audit record generation, unauthorized users can access security levels unknowingly for malicious intent creating vulnerabilities within the container platform.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000172"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "e114d2c1-93ed-5c6f-acee-076418cc9d9d",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Audit coverage is partially implemented through role-change records, UDS identity/admin events and platform audit sources as applicable. Confirm the permissions or authorization-level operations performed by the assessed component and their successful/unsuccessful audit coverage; file classification labels alone do not establish role or elevated-access coverage. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the partially implemented recommendation to similar audit-coverage requirements. Confirm required controls, credit existing coverage and implement only the missing required event coverage; this is not a final compliance finding.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Map the security-level operation in this requirement to actual role or authorization-capability operations and existing records. Implement missing required successful and unsuccessful attempt records, verify both outcomes and retain evidence. Record a scoped applicability decision for operations absent from the component rather than inventing them. As part of the shared V-222431 audit-control review, confirm the required event catalog for this exact requirement, map existing coverage, implement missing desired controls consistent with the STIG minimum, and retain verification evidence. Preserve scoped applicability decisions for nonexistent operations.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-platform-delivered-profile] re-platform-delivered-profile\nSuccessful UDS publication2026.45.4-uds.1 records package d528, app b84b9af, Core1.14.0, identity.mode=uds issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds and12 digest-pinned images; no bundled Keycloak image. This is delivered artifact evidence, not assumed default profile.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[logging] API logging\nCurrent HTTP logger includes service, request/trace IDs, event, remote address and response status, with secret redaction. Its common bindings do not add authenticated actor identity; other records/correlation must be checked.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/api/src/core/observability/logging.ts\n\n[app-permission-table] app-permission-table\nAppend-only role-grant/revocation table includes actor user, subject user, tenant, role, action and timestamp; it does not record every request or failed authorization.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/packages/database/schema/permission_audit.pg.hcl\n\n[app-db-values] app-db-values\nDB runs under UID70 with non-root/read-only/no-escalation and dedicated PGDATA PVC; default replica count is one. Only pgaudit preload is configured, not event classes.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/values.yaml\n\n[app-db-extensions] app-db-extensions\nMigration installs pgvector/PostGIS and attempts pgaudit, catching pgaudit failures as warnings; installation and effective audit classes must not be assumed from preload.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/packages/database/migrations/00000000000002_create_extensions.sql\n\n[pgaudit-default] pgaudit-default\npgAudit18 session audit classes default to none. Preload/CREATE EXTENSION alone does not enable READ/WRITE/ROLE/DDL auditing; log prefix supplies contextual identity fields.\nhttps://github.com/pgaudit/pgaudit/blob/REL_18_STABLE/README.md\n\n[host] MPN ownership\nCurrent scope assigns control plane/node OS to MPN; no host assessment or live configuration was supplied.\nhttps://gallatin.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/GG/pages/733085804/WIP+STIG+SRG+Compliance+Scope+MPN\n\n[re-platform-classification-fields] re-platform-classification-fields\nDelivered app source defines classification_level enum and files.classification field; chunks/assertions reuse it. A real data-security taxonomy exists; API/database audit coverage, not taxonomy discovery, is the remaining concern.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/schema/files.pg.hcl",
          "finding_details": "Audit coverage is partially implemented through role-change records, UDS identity/admin events and platform audit sources as applicable. Confirm the permissions or authorization-level operations performed by the assessed component and their successful/unsuccessful audit coverage; file classification labels alone do not establish role or elevated-access coverage. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the partially implemented recommendation to similar audit-coverage requirements. Confirm required controls, credit existing coverage and implement only the missing required event coverage; this is not a final compliance finding.\n\nNext action: Map the security-level operation in this requirement to actual role or authorization-capability operations and existing records. Implement missing required successful and unsuccessful attempt records, verify both outcomes and retain evidence. Record a scoped applicability decision for operations absent from the component rather than inventing them. As part of the shared V-222431 audit-control review, confirm the required event catalog for this exact requirement, map existing coverage, implement missing desired controls consistent with the STIG minimum, and retain verification evidence. Preserve scoped applicability decisions for nonexistent operations."
        },
        {
