          "uuid": "4468da88-302d-545a-9d4f-3669257b7b99",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Shared UDS identity logs every LOGIN, including concurrent logins, and its JSON listener retains user/session identifiers,client,IP and timestamps in the centralized stream. That permits correlation of the same user from different workstations without a separate concurrent-login detector in Horizon. Native registry/maintenance service identities remain within the inherited platform audit boundary.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the shared LOGIN event field mapping and native platform service-access audit mapping; no duplicated app login detector is needed.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-platform-delivered-profile] re-platform-delivered-profile\nSuccessful UDS publication2026.45.4-uds.1 records package d528, app b84b9af, Core1.14.0, identity.mode=uds issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds and12 digest-pinned images; no bundled Keycloak image. This is delivered artifact evidence, not assumed default profile.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-platform-realm] re-platform-realm\nUDS realm provides user/admin event logging and JSON event listener, native enabled/account roles, strong default password policy. Inactivity disable workflow defaults false; terms required-action enablement defaults false; CRL checking defaults false with OCSP checking true. No password blacklist or24h-min-age/eight-character-distance in passwordPolicy.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-platform-id-audit] re-platform-id-audit\nJSON listener emits platform user/admin events to stdout for Vector/Loki collection. UDS identity selected by delivered artifact makes this applicable to Horizon rather than requiring its own duplicated forwarding.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/plugin/src/main/java/com/defenseunicorns/uds/keycloak/plugin/eventListeners/JSONLogEventListenerProvider.java\n\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[host] MPN ownership\nCurrent scope assigns control plane/node OS to MPN; no host assessment or live configuration was supplied.\nhttps://gallatin.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/GG/pages/733085804/WIP+STIG+SRG+Compliance+Scope+MPN",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Shared UDS identity logs every LOGIN, including concurrent logins, and its JSON listener retains user/session identifiers,client,IP and timestamps in the centralized stream. That permits correlation of the same user from different workstations without a separate concurrent-login detector in Horizon. Native registry/maintenance service identities remain within the inherited platform audit boundary.\n\nNext action: Retain the shared LOGIN event field mapping and native platform service-access audit mapping; no duplicated app login detector is needed."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233267",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233267",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000507",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233267",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000507",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233267r961836_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233267r961836",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000507-CTR-001295",
          "rule_title": "The container platform runtime must generate audit records when successful/unsuccessful attempts to access objects occur.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform runtime to generate audit records on successful/unsuccessful access to objects.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to verify that the runtime generates audit records on successful/unsuccessful access to objects. \n\nIf audit records are not generated by the runtime when objects are successfully/unsuccessfully accessed, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Container platform runtime objects are defined as configuration files, code, etc. This provides the ability to configure resources and software parameters prior to image execution from the container platform registry. An unauthorized user with malicious intent could modify existing objects causing vulnerabilities or attacks. It would be difficult to establish, correlate, and investigate events relating to an incident or identify those responsible without audit record generation.\n\nWithout audit record generation, unauthorized users can access objects unknowingly for malicious intent creating vulnerabilities within the container platform.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
