          "uuid": "29fa4889-e3dd-5879-8b67-256d3681ca2d",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Vector node/pod logging plus the shipped Falco process/container rule sets provide container lifecycle and execution-event sources. Event selection and exclusions must be retained with the runtime package, rather than assuming no platform runtime auditing exists.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[core-falco] core-falco\nFalco loads shipped rule sets, detects runtime behavior and ships Sidekick events to Loki. Alertmanager delivery is commented out; detection is not automatic access revocation.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/falco/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Vector node/pod logging plus the shipped Falco process/container rule sets provide container lifecycle and execution-event sources. Event selection and exclusions must be retained with the runtime package, rather than assuming no platform runtime auditing exists.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233271",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233271",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000514",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233271",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000514",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233271r1137647_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233271r1137647",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000514-CTR-001315",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must use a valid FIPS 140-2 or FIPS 140-3 approved cryptographic module to generate hashes.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to use valid FIPS 140-2/140-3 approved cryptographic modules to generate hashes.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to validate that valid FIPS 140-2/140-3 approved cryptographic modules are being used to generate hashes. \n\nIf non-valid or unapproved FIPS 140-2/140-3 cryptographic modules are being used to generate hashes, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The cryptographic module used must have at least one validated hash algorithm. This validated hash algorithm must be used to generate cryptographic hashes for all cryptographic security function within the container platform components being evaluated.\n\nFIPS 140-2/140-3 precludes the use of invalidated cryptography for the cryptographic protection of sensitive or valuable data within federal systems. Unvalidated cryptography is viewed by NIST as providing no protection to the information or data. In effect, the data would be considered unprotected plaintext. If the agency specifies that the information or data be cryptographically protected, then FIPS 140-2/140-3 is applicable. In essence, if cryptography is required, it must be validated. Cryptographic modules that have been approved for classified use may be used in lieu of modules that have been validated against the FIPS 140-2/140-3 standard.\n\nThis requirement also applies to Zero Trust initiatives.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002450"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "0e28b3e2-7ae1-500a-a5e7-8fdfa5d94acd",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS's supported unicorn flavor selects curated FIPS mesh/identity images, and Core Keycloak explicitly starts in strict FIPS mode. This supplies a concrete offered platform implementation for the prescribed cryptography. Recommendation is conditional on selecting that hardened flavor; upstream/demo flavor does not inherit it.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Bind the MPN Core release/flavor/digests to vendor module-validation evidence and approved mode; do not apply this ruling to an upstream/demo Core deployment.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-flavors] core-flavors\nCore distinguishes unicorn (curated FIPS-validated images), registry1 (Iron Bank hardening) and upstream (development/community images). The production hardened offering exists; actual selected flavor/digests and module mapping remain explicit deployment inputs.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/concepts/platform/flavors.mdx\n\n[core-hardened-mesh] core-hardened-mesh\nUnicorn flavor selects curated istio-pilot-fips and istio-proxy-fips images; ambient ztunnel has its own FIPS image override. This proves the offered implementation, not the flavor of a deployed MPN cluster.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/values/unicorn/istiod.yaml\n\n[core-strict-keycloak] core-strict-keycloak\nCore Keycloak starts with --features=fips and --fips-mode=strict; cgr image handling adds the FIPS JCE security setup. Bundled Horizon Keycloak is a different chart and must not inherit these flags by name alone.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/statefulset.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS's supported unicorn flavor selects curated FIPS mesh/identity images, and Core Keycloak explicitly starts in strict FIPS mode. This supplies a concrete offered platform implementation for the prescribed cryptography. Recommendation is conditional on selecting that hardened flavor; upstream/demo flavor does not inherit it.\n\nNext action: Bind the MPN Core release/flavor/digests to vendor module-validation evidence and approved mode; do not apply this ruling to an upstream/demo Core deployment."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233273",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233273",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000516",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233273",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000516",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233273r1268102_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233273r1268102",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000516-CTR-001325",
          "rule_title": "Container platform components must be configured in accordance with the security configuration settings based on DoW security configuration or implementation guidance, including SRGs, STIGs, NSA configuration guides, CTOs, and DTMs.",
          "fix_text": "Configure container services in accordance with the security configuration settings based on DoW security configuration or implementation guidance, including SRGs, STIGs, NSA configuration guides, CTOs, and DTMs.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to determine the services offered by the container platform and validate that any services that are offered are configured in accordance with the security configuration settings based on DoW security configuration or implementation guidance, including SRGs, STIGs, NSA configuration guides, CTOs, and DTMs. \n\nIf container platform services are not configured in accordance with the security configuration settings based on DoW security configuration or implementation guidance, including SRGs, STIGs, NSA configuration guides, CTOs, and DTMs, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Container platform components are part of the overall container platform, offering services that enable the container platform to fully orchestrate user containers. These components may fall outside the scope of this document, but they still must be secured. Examples of such components are DNS, routers, and firewalls. These and any other services offered by the container platform must follow the appropriate STIG or SRG for the technology offered. If a STIG or SRG is not available for the technology, then best practices for the technology must be used. For example, the Cloud Native Computing Foundation (CNCF) is an open-source organization that is working on container platform best practices.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000366"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "dec92b3d-ced1-5db2-a84d-2007d1b78332",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The delivered profile inherits many actual Core mechanisms and removes the false bundled-identity gaps, but concrete Core configuration residuals remain (disabled consent/inactivity/account notifications, password-rule tailoring). Treat this roll-up as links to those existing operator/package tasks; it is not another independent full-platform implementation project.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Complete or tailor the linked platform/package residuals and retain component hardening evidence. Do not create a duplicate roll-up remediation ticket.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-platform-delivered-profile] re-platform-delivered-profile\nSuccessful UDS publication2026.45.4-uds.1 records package d528, app b84b9af, Core1.14.0, identity.mode=uds issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds and12 digest-pinned images; no bundled Keycloak image. This is delivered artifact evidence, not assumed default profile.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-platform-realm] re-platform-realm\nUDS realm provides user/admin event logging and JSON event listener, native enabled/account roles, strong default password policy. Inactivity disable workflow defaults false; terms required-action enablement defaults false; CRL checking defaults false with OCSP checking true. No password blacklist or24h-min-age/eight-character-distance in passwordPolicy.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-platform-realm-params] re-platform-realm-params\nCore exposes realmInitEnv overrides for ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS, TERMS_AND_CONDITIONS_ENABLED and X509_CRL_CHECKING_ENABLED; these are commented/unset in shipped defaults. detailedObservability.alerts.enabled defaults false.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[re-platform-monitor] re-platform-monitor\nDefault Kubernetes storage/system alerts enabled; alertmanager exists but no SA/ISSO receiver routes or exact75percent audit repository rule are set in this file.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/prometheus-stack/values/kube-prometheus-stack-values.yaml\n\n[re-platform-init-resource] re-platform-init-resource\nUDS identity transformation clears migrations.extraDatabases and removes bundled Keycloak/provisioner components, thereby removing create-databases and wait-for-Keycloak init containers. Reproduced delivered chart captures contain16 main/init containers, all with nonempty resource settings.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/scripts/ci/platform_identity.py",
          "finding_details": "The delivered profile inherits many actual Core mechanisms and removes the false bundled-identity gaps, but concrete Core configuration residuals remain (disabled consent/inactivity/account notifications, password-rule tailoring). Treat this roll-up as links to those existing operator/package tasks; it is not another independent full-platform implementation project.\n\nNext action: Complete or tailor the linked platform/package residuals and retain component hardening evidence. Do not create a duplicate roll-up remediation ticket."
        },
