          "group_id": "V-233276",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000560",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233276r1201983_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233276r1201983",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000560-CTR-001340",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must prohibit communication using TLS versions 1.0 and 1.1, and SSL 2.0 and 3.0.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to prohibit communication using TLS versions 1.0 and 1.1, SSL 2.0 and 3.0.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to determine if TLS versions 1.0 and 1.1, SSL 2.0 and 3.0 are prohibited for communication. \n\nIf communication using TLS versions 1.0 and 1.1, SSL 2.0 and 3.0 is permitted, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The container platform and its components will prohibit the use of SSL and unauthorized versions of TLS protocols to properly secure communication.\n\nThe use of unsupported protocol exposes vulnerabilities to the container platform by rogue traffic interceptions, man-in-the middle-attacks, and impersonation of users or services from the container platform runtime, registry, and keystore.\n\nThe container platform and its components will adhere to NIST 800-52R2.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001453"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "ec21e4f8-ef4a-57c4-ba28-026b2dd77187",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Core root PeerAuthentication requires STRICT mTLS for enrolled workloads; gateway minimum is TLS1.3 (optionally 1.2), and Horizon declares ambient enrollment. This supports protected traffic without adding application-local TLS everywhere.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-mtls] core-mtls\nRoot-namespace PeerAuthentication requires STRICT mTLS. Horizon opts into ambient mesh; in-mesh plaintext-looking URLs are protected by mesh rather than application TLS.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/common/chart/templates/peer-authentication.yaml\n\n[core-gateway] core-gateway\nGateway redirects HTTP to HTTPS and requires TLS 1.3 by default, optionally TLS 1.2; credentialName references Kubernetes TLS secrets.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/charts/uds-istio-config/templates/gateway.yaml\n\n[mesh] Mesh and policy\nPackage chart declares ambient mesh and generated network policy. Disabled chart-local NetworkPolicy and plaintext application URLs do not establish missing mesh enforcement.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-uds-package/templates/package.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Core root PeerAuthentication requires STRICT mTLS for enrolled workloads; gateway minimum is TLS1.3 (optionally 1.2), and Horizon declares ambient enrollment. This supports protected traffic without adding application-local TLS everywhere.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233284",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233284",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000605",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233284",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000605",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233284r961893_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233284r961893",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000605-CTR-001380",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must validate certificates used for Transport Layer Security (TLS) functions by performing an RFC 5280-compliant certification path validation.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to validate certificates used for Transport Layer Security (TLS) functions by performing an RFC 5280-compliant certification path validation and to disable the use of self-signed certificates.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to verify the container platform is validating certificates used for Transport Layer Security (TLS) functions by performing a RFC 5280-compliant certification path validation and that self-signed certificates are not being used. \n\nIf the container platform is not validating certificates used for TLS functions by performing an RFC 5280-compliant certification path validation, this is a finding. \n\nIf self-signed certificates are in use, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "A certification path is the path from the end entity certificate to a trusted root certification authority (CA). Certification path validation is necessary for a relying party to make an informed decision regarding acceptance of an end entity certificate and discourages the use of self-signed certificates.\n\nCertification path validation includes checks such as certificate issuer trust, time validity, and revocation status for each certificate in the certification path. Revocation status information for CA and subject certificates in a certification path is commonly provided via certificate revocation lists (CRLs) or online certificate status protocol (OCSP) responses. Compliance checks should be in accordance to RFC 5280.\n\nNot adhering to RFC 5280 could result in rogue certificates, session hijacks, man-in-the-middle, denial-of-service attacks, malware, and data or information manipulation.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000185"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "b685be13-587c-57ab-aa01-d90bd1f5a0b6",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Istio mesh uses CA-issued workload certificates and authenticated peer identities; the pinned gateway/config supports supplied certificate chains and trust bundles. RFC5280 validation mechanisms are present; approval of the actual trust anchors remains a separate input.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-mtls] core-mtls\nRoot-namespace PeerAuthentication requires STRICT mTLS. Horizon opts into ambient mesh; in-mesh plaintext-looking URLs are protected by mesh rather than application TLS.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/common/chart/templates/peer-authentication.yaml\n\n[core-gateway] core-gateway\nGateway redirects HTTP to HTTPS and requires TLS 1.3 by default, optionally TLS 1.2; credentialName references Kubernetes TLS secrets.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/charts/uds-istio-config/templates/gateway.yaml\n\n[core-provision] core-provision\nProduction requires externally supplied gateway TLS chains, Loki/Velero object stores and a Keycloak database/user. Core cannot determine the supplied CA approvals, storage encryption or bucket lifecycle by itself.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/getting-started/production/provision-services.mdx",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Istio mesh uses CA-issued workload certificates and authenticated peer identities; the pinned gateway/config supports supplied certificate chains and trust bundles. RFC5280 validation mechanisms are present; approval of the actual trust anchors remains a separate input.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233285",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233285",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000610",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233285",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000610",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233285r961896_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233285r961896",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000610-CTR-001385",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must use FIPS-validated SHA-2 or higher hash function for digital signature generation and verification (non-legacy use).",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to use a FIPS-validated SHA-2 or higher hash function for digital signature generation and verification.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to validate that a FIPS-validated SHA-2 or higher hash function is being used for digital signature generation and verification. \n\nIf a FIPS-validated SHA-2 or higher hash function is not being used for digital signature generation and verification, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Without the use of digital signature, information can be altered by unauthorized accounts accessing or modifying the container platform registry, keystore, and container at runtime. Digital signatures provide non-repudiation for transactions between the components within the container platform. Without the use of approved FIPS-validated SHA-2 or higher hash function with digital signatures, the container platform cannot claim the validity of the individual or service identity and guarantee private key is kept secret. Keeping the private keys secure is vital for validating individuals or service identity prior to information exchange. The container platform must be configured to use SHA-2 or higher hash functions for digital signatures in accordance with SP 800-131Ar2.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000803"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "308b72b1-632d-50b0-9ca6-f4e51a4c616b",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS's supported unicorn flavor selects curated FIPS mesh/identity images, and Core Keycloak explicitly starts in strict FIPS mode. This supplies a concrete offered platform implementation for the prescribed cryptography. Recommendation is conditional on selecting that hardened flavor; upstream/demo flavor does not inherit it.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Bind the MPN Core release/flavor/digests to vendor module-validation evidence and approved mode; do not apply this ruling to an upstream/demo Core deployment.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-flavors] core-flavors\nCore distinguishes unicorn (curated FIPS-validated images), registry1 (Iron Bank hardening) and upstream (development/community images). The production hardened offering exists; actual selected flavor/digests and module mapping remain explicit deployment inputs.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/concepts/platform/flavors.mdx\n\n[core-hardened-mesh] core-hardened-mesh\nUnicorn flavor selects curated istio-pilot-fips and istio-proxy-fips images; ambient ztunnel has its own FIPS image override. This proves the offered implementation, not the flavor of a deployed MPN cluster.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/values/unicorn/istiod.yaml\n\n[core-strict-keycloak] core-strict-keycloak\nCore Keycloak starts with --features=fips and --fips-mode=strict; cgr image handling adds the FIPS JCE security setup. Bundled Horizon Keycloak is a different chart and must not inherit these flags by name alone.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/statefulset.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS's supported unicorn flavor selects curated FIPS mesh/identity images, and Core Keycloak explicitly starts in strict FIPS mode. This supplies a concrete offered platform implementation for the prescribed cryptography. Recommendation is conditional on selecting that hardened flavor; upstream/demo flavor does not inherit it.\n\nNext action: Bind the MPN Core release/flavor/digests to vendor module-validation evidence and approved mode; do not apply this ruling to an upstream/demo Core deployment."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233289",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233289",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000635",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233289",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000635",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233289r1137648_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233289r1137648",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000635-CTR-001405",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must use a FIPS-validated cryptographic module to implement encryption services for unclassified information requiring confidentiality.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to use FIPS-validated cryptographic modules to encrypt unclassified information requiring confidentiality.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to ensure FIPS-validated cryptographic modules are implemented to encrypt unclassified information requiring confidentiality. \n\nIf FIPS-validated cryptographic modules are not being used, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Unvalidated cryptography is viewed by NIST as providing no protection to the information or data. In effect, the data would be considered unprotected plaintext. If the agency specifies that the information or data be cryptographically protected, then FIPS 140-2/140-3 is applicable. In essence, if cryptography is required, it must be validated. Cryptographic modules that have been approved for classified use may be used in lieu of modules that have been validated against the FIPS 140-2/140-3 standard.\n\nThe cryptographic module used must have one FIPS-validated encryption algorithm (i.e., validated Advanced Encryption Standard [AES]). This validated algorithm must be used for encryption for cryptographic security function within the container platform component and information residing in the container platform registry and keystore.\n\nThis requirement also applies to Zero Trust initiatives.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002450"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "a0690e39-7298-5ee2-8e32-33bda598c662",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS's supported unicorn flavor selects curated FIPS mesh/identity images, and Core Keycloak explicitly starts in strict FIPS mode. This supplies a concrete offered platform implementation for the prescribed cryptography. Recommendation is conditional on selecting that hardened flavor; upstream/demo flavor does not inherit it.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Bind the MPN Core release/flavor/digests to vendor module-validation evidence and approved mode; do not apply this ruling to an upstream/demo Core deployment.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-flavors] core-flavors\nCore distinguishes unicorn (curated FIPS-validated images), registry1 (Iron Bank hardening) and upstream (development/community images). The production hardened offering exists; actual selected flavor/digests and module mapping remain explicit deployment inputs.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/concepts/platform/flavors.mdx\n\n[core-hardened-mesh] core-hardened-mesh\nUnicorn flavor selects curated istio-pilot-fips and istio-proxy-fips images; ambient ztunnel has its own FIPS image override. This proves the offered implementation, not the flavor of a deployed MPN cluster.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/values/unicorn/istiod.yaml\n\n[core-strict-keycloak] core-strict-keycloak\nCore Keycloak starts with --features=fips and --fips-mode=strict; cgr image handling adds the FIPS JCE security setup. Bundled Horizon Keycloak is a different chart and must not inherit these flags by name alone.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/templates/statefulset.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS's supported unicorn flavor selects curated FIPS mesh/identity images, and Core Keycloak explicitly starts in strict FIPS mode. This supplies a concrete offered platform implementation for the prescribed cryptography. Recommendation is conditional on selecting that hardened flavor; upstream/demo flavor does not inherit it.\n\nNext action: Bind the MPN Core release/flavor/digests to vendor module-validation evidence and approved mode; do not apply this ruling to an upstream/demo Core deployment."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233290",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233290",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000645",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233290",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000645",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233290r1250356_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233290r1250356",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000645-CTR-001410",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must prohibit or restrict the use of protocols that transmit unencrypted authentication information or use flawed cryptographic algorithms for transmission.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to use protocols that transmit authentication data encrypted and to use cryptographic algorithms that are not flawed.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to verify that container platform is not using protocols that transmit authentication data unencrypted and that the container platform is not using flawed cryptographic algorithms for transmission. \n\nIf the container platform is using protocols to transmit authentication data unencrypted or is using flawed cryptographic algorithms, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The use of secure ports, protocols and services within the container platform must be controlled and conform to the PPSM CAL. Those ports, protocols, and services that fall outside the PPSM CAL must be blocked by the runtime. Instructions on the PPSM can be found in DoD Instruction 8551.01 Policy.\n\nUnsecure protocols for transmission will expose the information system data and information, making the session susceptible to manipulation, hijacking, and man-in-the middle attacks.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000382"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "b783df6c-90b4-58ea-b10d-95db7f229da1",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Core root PeerAuthentication requires STRICT mTLS for enrolled workloads; gateway minimum is TLS1.3 (optionally 1.2), and Horizon declares ambient enrollment. This supports protected traffic without adding application-local TLS everywhere.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-mtls] core-mtls\nRoot-namespace PeerAuthentication requires STRICT mTLS. Horizon opts into ambient mesh; in-mesh plaintext-looking URLs are protected by mesh rather than application TLS.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/common/chart/templates/peer-authentication.yaml\n\n[core-gateway] core-gateway\nGateway redirects HTTP to HTTPS and requires TLS 1.3 by default, optionally TLS 1.2; credentialName references Kubernetes TLS secrets.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/charts/uds-istio-config/templates/gateway.yaml\n\n[mesh] Mesh and policy\nPackage chart declares ambient mesh and generated network policy. Disabled chart-local NetworkPolicy and plaintext application URLs do not establish missing mesh enforcement.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-uds-package/templates/package.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Core root PeerAuthentication requires STRICT mTLS for enrolled workloads; gateway minimum is TLS1.3 (optionally 1.2), and Horizon declares ambient enrollment. This supports protected traffic without adding application-local TLS everywhere.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-257291",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-257291",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000318",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-257291",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000318",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-257291r961287_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-257291r961287",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000318-CTR-000740",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must enforce organization-defined circumstances and/or usage conditions for organization-defined accounts.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to enforce organization-defined circumstances and/or usage conditions for organization-defined accounts.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Determine if the container platform is configured to enforce organization-defined circumstances and/or usage conditions for organization-defined accounts. \n\nIf the container platform does not enforce organization-defined circumstances and/or usage conditions for organization-defined accounts, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Activity under unusual conditions can indicate hostile activity. For example, what is normal activity during business hours can indicate hostile activity if it occurs during off hours.\n\nDepending on mission needs and conditions, account usage restrictions based on conditions and circumstances may be critical to limit access to resources and data to comply with operational or mission access control requirements. Thus, the application must be configured to enforce the specific conditions or circumstances under which application accounts can be used (e.g., by restricting usage to certain days of the week, time of day, or specific durations of time).",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002145"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "50246570-0a5f-5d10-b8d5-ee0de1430311",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The delivered profile delegates accounts and organization/group usage conditions to UDS identity, where enabled state, user/group roles and administrative disable/remove operations control account access. The app validates the shared issuer and tenant claims; it need not own a second employee directory or orphan-account database. Authoritative offboarding approval is a provider procedure, while separately flagged automatic inactivity/temporary-expiry rules need their own settings.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Record MPN identity-owner offboarding/usage-condition procedure and its authorization mapping; use existing shared-provider account disablement rather than new Horizon account lifecycle code.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-platform-delivered-profile] re-platform-delivered-profile\nSuccessful UDS publication2026.45.4-uds.1 records package d528, app b84b9af, Core1.14.0, identity.mode=uds issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds and12 digest-pinned images; no bundled Keycloak image. This is delivered artifact evidence, not assumed default profile.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-platform-realm] re-platform-realm\nUDS realm provides user/admin event logging and JSON event listener, native enabled/account roles, strong default password policy. Inactivity disable workflow defaults false; terms required-action enablement defaults false; CRL checking defaults false with OCSP checking true. No password blacklist or24h-min-age/eight-character-distance in passwordPolicy.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. The delivered profile delegates accounts and organization/group usage conditions to UDS identity, where enabled state, user/group roles and administrative disable/remove operations control account access. The app validates the shared issuer and tenant claims; it need not own a second employee directory or orphan-account database. Authoritative offboarding approval is a provider procedure, while separately flagged automatic inactivity/temporary-expiry rules need their own settings.\n\nNext action: Record MPN identity-owner offboarding/usage-condition procedure and its authorization mapping; use existing shared-provider account disablement rather than new Horizon account lifecycle code."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-263586",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-263586",
