          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Core supplies OTP/X509-capable SSO, token-based authenticated identities and SPIFFE/mTLS workload identity. Device strength and native host/local access remain MPN policy/inheritance conditions, rather than missing Horizon features.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-keycloak] core-keycloak\nCore uses identity-config 0.32.0, supports X509 and OTP by default; inactivity auto-disable is opt-in, and concurrent-session limit defaults to unlimited (0). This is platform SSO, not Horizon bundled Keycloak.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[core-id-realm] core-id-realm\nUDS realm defines three-failure lockout, permanentLockout=true, 600-second idle, PBKDF2-SHA256 password policy with 15 characters and classes/history/expiry; JSON event listener is enabled. Some controls remain optional.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[core-mtls] core-mtls\nRoot-namespace PeerAuthentication requires STRICT mTLS. Horizon opts into ambient mesh; in-mesh plaintext-looking URLs are protected by mesh rather than application TLS.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/common/chart/templates/peer-authentication.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Core supplies OTP/X509-capable SSO, token-based authenticated identities and SPIFFE/mTLS workload identity. Device strength and native host/local access remain MPN policy/inheritance conditions, rather than missing Horizon features.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-263591",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-263591",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000830",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-263591",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000830",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-263591r981913_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-263591r981913",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000830-CTR-000190",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must for password-based authentication, maintain a list of commonly used, expected, or compromised passwords on an organization-defined frequency.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to maintain a list of commonly used, expected, or compromised passwords on an organization-defined frequency.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify the container platform is configured to maintain a list of commonly used, expected, or compromised passwords on an organization-defined frequency.\n\nIf the container platform is not configured to maintain a list of commonly used, expected, or compromised passwords on an organization-defined frequency, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Password-based authentication applies to passwords regardless of whether they are used in single-factor or multi-factor authentication. Long passwords or passphrases are preferable over shorter passwords. Enforced composition rules provide marginal security benefits while decreasing usability. However, organizations may choose to establish certain rules for password generation (e.g., minimum character length for long passwords) under certain circumstances and can enforce this requirement in IA-5(1)(h). Account recovery can occur, for example, in situations when a password is forgotten. Cryptographically protected passwords include salted one-way cryptographic hashes of passwords. The list of commonly used, compromised, or expected passwords includes passwords obtained from previous breach corpuses, dictionary words, and repetitive or sequential characters. The list includes context-specific words, such as the name of the service, username, and derivatives thereof.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-004058"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "fb3b80ce-b358-5e5f-8013-d857c0d1385b",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Actual password authentication occurs in UDS Keycloak, not bundled Horizon identity. Its pinned policy lacks a configured compromised-password dictionary/provider and update lifecycle; available password error messages/capability do not activate validation. This can be inherited from a selected upstream identity authority or enabled in the provider, without implementing password handling in Horizon. Native Keycloak already supplies password-denylist validation and reload. The source-known residual is Gallatin corpus delivery/policy/update configuration, not an unimplemented product capability.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE (self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Gallatin mounts its approved maintained denylist at /opt/keycloak/data/password-blacklists, appends passwordBlacklist(filename) to the existing Core realmInitEnv.PASSWORD_POLICY without dropping current FIPS KDF/length/classes/history/expiry, and updates the live realm password policy if already imported. Configure Gallatin-owned corpus cadence/incident updates; Keycloak natively reloads changed files (default60s), so no new API or custom validator service is needed.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-platform-delivered-profile] re-platform-delivered-profile\nSuccessful UDS publication2026.45.4-uds.1 records package d528, app b84b9af, Core1.14.0, identity.mode=uds issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds and12 digest-pinned images; no bundled Keycloak image. This is delivered artifact evidence, not assumed default profile.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-platform-realm] re-platform-realm\nUDS realm provides user/admin event logging and JSON event listener, native enabled/account roles, strong default password policy. Inactivity disable workflow defaults false; terms required-action enablement defaults false; CRL checking defaults false with OCSP checking true. No password blacklist or24h-min-age/eight-character-distance in passwordPolicy.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/realm.json5\n\n[re-platform-realm-params] re-platform-realm-params\nCore exposes realmInitEnv overrides for ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS, TERMS_AND_CONDITIONS_ENABLED and X509_CRL_CHECKING_ENABLED; these are commented/unset in shipped defaults. detailedObservability.alerts.enabled defaults false.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-values] mpn-identity-values\nCore1.14 chart default fields: realmInitEnv.SSO_SESSION_MAX_PER_USER=\"0\"; ACCOUNT_INACTIVITY_DAYS unset; terms enabled unset; detailedObservability.alerts.enabled=false; realmAuthFlows username/password/X509/OTPtrue, X509MFAfalse. Gallatin owns these settings on MPN, independent of DU managedIL5/IL6 clusters.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/chart/values.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-override] mpn-identity-override\nCore value sourcePath .keycloak.keycloak maps to chart root; bundle override path is packages[core].overrides.keycloak.keycloak.values[].path (e.g.realmInitEnv), not Horizonpackageapplication values.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/keycloak/common/zarf.yaml\n\n[mpn-identity-denylist] mpn-identity-denylist\nNative Keycloak26.7.4 passwordBlacklist(filename) policy reads newline-delimited corpus from $KC_HOME/data/password-blacklists (or configured SPI path) and hotreloads changed file at defaultcheckIntervalSeconds60. Mount/update approvedcorpus and addpolicy; no customstrengthmeter/passwordvalidator service required. Frequency/content remain Gallatinsecuritypolicy decisions.\nhttps://github.com/keycloak/keycloak/blob/26.7.4/server-spi-private/src/main/java/org/keycloak/policy/DenylistPasswordPolicyProviderFactory.java\n\n[mpn-identity-init-only] mpn-identity-init-only\nrealmInitEnv and realmAuthFlows are initial realm import inputs. Existing realm is persisted in DB; redeploying Secret does not change it. Core documents equivalent Keycloak AdminConsole settings. Use controlled non-destructive AdminAPI/console update and persist the desired bundle override; do not advise tearing down production identity.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/how-to-guides/identity-and-authorization/configure-keycloak-login-policies.mdx",
          "finding_details": "Actual password authentication occurs in UDS Keycloak, not bundled Horizon identity. Its pinned policy lacks a configured compromised-password dictionary/provider and update lifecycle; available password error messages/capability do not activate validation. This can be inherited from a selected upstream identity authority or enabled in the provider, without implementing password handling in Horizon. Native Keycloak already supplies password-denylist validation and reload. The source-known residual is Gallatin corpus delivery/policy/update configuration, not an unimplemented product capability.\n\nNext action: Gallatin mounts its approved maintained denylist at /opt/keycloak/data/password-blacklists, appends passwordBlacklist(filename) to the existing Core realmInitEnv.PASSWORD_POLICY without dropping current FIPS KDF/length/classes/history/expiry, and updates the live realm password policy if already imported. Configure Gallatin-owned corpus cadence/incident updates; Keycloak natively reloads changed files (default60s), so no new API or custom validator service is needed."
        },
        {
