          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-004192"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "b1e91938-06a6-5e11-a4e7-89b41c0a5259",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: UDS separates administrative and tenant gateways, application namespaces and service identity allow rules; this supplies logically separated management paths. Physical/VLAN routing is inherited from MPN.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-network] core-network\nOperator generates namespace ingress/egress default-deny NetworkPolicy; connectivity requires declared allow rules.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/operator/controllers/network/defaults/default-deny-all.ts\n\n[core-grafana] core-grafana\nGrafana disables local login, uses UDS OAuth, maps Admin group to Admin and Auditor to Viewer, and rejects unmatched groups. OAuth secrets are file-mounted.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. UDS separates administrative and tenant gateways, application namespaces and service identity allow rules; this supplies logically separated management paths. Physical/VLAN routing is inherited from MPN.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-263599",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-263599",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000910",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-263599",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000910",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-263599r982473_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-263599r982473",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000910-CTR-000300",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must include only approved trust anchors in trust stores or certificate stores managed by the organization.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to include only approved trust anchors in trust stores or certificate stores managed by the organization.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify the container platform is configured to include only approved trust anchors in trust stores or certificate stores managed by the organization.\n\nIf the container platform is not configured to include only approved trust anchors in trust stores or certificate stores managed by the organization, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Public key infrastructure (PKI) certificates are certificates with visibility external to organizational systems and certificates related to the internal operations of systems, such as application-specific time services. In cryptographic systems with a hierarchical structure, a trust anchor is an authoritative source (i.e., a certificate authority) for which trust is assumed and not derived. A root certificate for a PKI system is an example of a trust anchor. A trust store or certificate store maintains a list of trusted root certificates.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-004909"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "47a2b439-b120-51a1-a25e-3f39b947528a",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The delivered profile uses UDS identity, whose actual image build downloads the official DoD approved external PKI trust-chain bundle, requires a pinned SHA256 and removes expired certs before importing the truststore. Thus approved trust-anchor content exists in source; it is not wholly unspecified. Custom gateway/system trust overrides remain a platform-owner review item and do not justify duplicating a truststore in Horizon.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the shipped DoD truststore mapping and confirm only intentional MPN custom trust additions/overrides against the approved CA set.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-platform-delivered-profile] re-platform-delivered-profile\nSuccessful UDS publication2026.45.4-uds.1 records package d528, app b84b9af, Core1.14.0, identity.mode=uds issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds and12 digest-pinned images; no bundled Keycloak image. This is delivered artifact evidence, not assumed default profile.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-platform-trust] re-platform-trust\nActual default IdP trust source is official DoD approved external PKI chains ZIP, with required SHA256 check and expired-certificate rejection before populating truststore. It is not an unspecified empty mechanism.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/truststore/ca-to-jks.sh\n\n[re-platform-trust-boundary] re-platform-trust-boundary\nCore documents default DoD UNCLASS IdP certificate bundle and explicit custom-image process to replace it. Shared platform trust defaults are inspectable; gateway/custom trust overrides are separately owner-managed.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/how-to-guides/identity-and-authorization/configure-truststore.mdx\n\n[re-platform-trust-distribution] re-platform-trust-distribution\nOperator distributes selected custom/DoD/curated public trust to UDS components. Custom/public global trust-bundle inclusion is opt-in; default identity DoD truststore is a separate mechanism.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/how-to-guides/networking/manage-trust-bundles.mdx",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. The delivered profile uses UDS identity, whose actual image build downloads the official DoD approved external PKI trust-chain bundle, requires a pinned SHA256 and removes expired certs before importing the truststore. Thus approved trust-anchor content exists in source; it is not wholly unspecified. Custom gateway/system trust overrides remain a platform-owner review item and do not justify duplicating a truststore in Horizon.\n\nNext action: Retain the shipped DoD truststore mapping and confirm only intentional MPN custom trust additions/overrides against the approved CA set."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-263600",
          "group_tree": [
