          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "0d29a519-a5d1-52ba-9904-c2ab593ac74d",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Core/gateway keys are kept in platform-managed Secrets/identity stores with namespace/RBAC and file-mount controls, rather than application-public files. Additional HSM requirements, if imposed by the organization, remain a deployment-specific safeguard.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-gateway] core-gateway\nGateway redirects HTTP to HTTPS and requires TLS 1.3 by default, optionally TLS 1.2; credentialName references Kubernetes TLS secrets.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/charts/uds-istio-config/templates/gateway.yaml\n\n[core-network] core-network\nOperator generates namespace ingress/egress default-deny NetworkPolicy; connectivity requires declared allow rules.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/operator/controllers/network/defaults/default-deny-all.ts\n\n[core-provision] core-provision\nProduction requires externally supplied gateway TLS chains, Loki/Velero object stores and a Keycloak database/user. Core cannot determine the supplied CA approvals, storage encryption or bucket lifecycle by itself.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/getting-started/production/provision-services.mdx",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Core/gateway keys are kept in platform-managed Secrets/identity stores with namespace/RBAC and file-mount controls, rather than application-public files. Additional HSM requirements, if imposed by the organization, remain a deployment-specific safeguard.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-263601",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-263601",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000920",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-263601",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000920",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-263601r982477_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-263601r982477",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000920-CTR-000320",
          "rule_title": "The container platform must synchronize system clocks within and between systems or system components.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to synchronize system clocks within and between systems or system components.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify the container platform is configured to synchronize system clocks within and between systems or system components.\n\nIf the container platform is not configured to synchronize system clocks within and between systems or system components, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Time synchronization of system clocks is essential for the correct execution of many system services, including identification and authentication processes that involve certificates and time-of-day restrictions as part of access control. Denial of service or failure to deny expired credentials may result without properly synchronized clocks within and between systems and system components. Time is commonly expressed in Coordinated Universal Time (UTC), a modern continuation of Greenwich Mean Time (GMT), or local time with an offset from UTC. The granularity of time measurements refers to the degree of synchronization between system clocks and reference clocks, such as clocks synchronizing within hundreds of milliseconds or tens of milliseconds. Organizations may define different time granularities for system components. Time service can be critical to other security capabilities such as access control and identification and authentication depending on the nature of the mechanisms used to support the capabilities.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-004922"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "5239",
          "uuid": "a2c7ae98-be4a-545c-a119-a312aee1175a",
          "stig_uuid": "8db8e348-871f-528d-9059-3d39cad3f975",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: All UDS workloads use node clocks, and MPN owns the node operating system/time service. No separate application clock mechanism is required; retain host NTP inheritance.\n\nComponents: UDS platform: registry, runtime, Pepr, Istio, logging; delivered workloads\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[host] MPN ownership\nCurrent scope assigns control plane/node OS to MPN; no host assessment or live configuration was supplied.\nhttps://gallatin.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/GG/pages/733085804/WIP+STIG+SRG+Compliance+Scope+MPN\n\n[core-id-audit] core-id-audit\nUDS event listener prints user/admin events to stdout with event fields and UTC timestamps. Vector can collect these without a separately configured app forwarding agent.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-identity-config/blob/74480722e1cc8405b5e9b65ac7d7086ab7f4056d/src/plugin/src/main/java/com/defenseunicorns/uds/keycloak/plugin/eventListeners/JSONLogEventListenerProvider.java",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. All UDS workloads use node clocks, and MPN owns the node operating system/time service. No separate application clock mechanism is required; retain host NTP inheritance.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-270875",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-270875",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000247",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-270875",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000247",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-270875r1050646_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-270875r1050646",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000247-CTR-000330",
          "rule_title": "The container must have resource request limits set.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the container platform to restrict the ability of users or other systems to launch denial-of-service (DoS) attacks from the container platform components by setting resource limits on resources such as memory, storage, and CPU utilization.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the container platform configuration to determine that resource limits are set.\n\nIf the container platform does not enforce resource limits, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Container_Platform__SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Setting a container resource request limit allows the container platform to determine the best location for the container to execute. The container platform looks at the resources available and finds the location that will require the minimum resources for the container to execute. Examples of resources that can be specified are CPU, memory, and storage.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
